To contribute to the objective, the Office will:
Provide the full range of conflict resolution services to United Nations staff and non-staff personnel in three core areas: addressing workplace grievances from staff and non-staff personnel through case intervention; engaging with relevant stakeholders to provide systemic feedback for improvements in the workplace; and strengthening, through capacity-building initiatives, the ability of staff and non-staff personnel to address workplace conflict effectively;
Strengthen the Organization’s informal conflict resolution system through enhanced accessibility, visibility, and effectiveness of services, focusing on outreach efforts to raise awareness of the importance of informal dispute resolution as a preferred first step.
Ensure that staff and non-staff personnel, irrespective of location, have access to the informal pillar of the administration of justice and to informal conflict resolution services, including by providing services in all six official languages of the United Nations;
Conduct regular presentations on “civility, communication and community,” in various languages to global offices to promote and facilitate healthy dialogue among colleagues.
The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
A more harmonious and collaborative work environment for United Nations staff and non-staff personnel;
More productive and more effective functioning of the Organization;
Improved awareness among managers, staff and non-staff personnel of the importance of resilience and flexibility in fostering a harmonious workplace.
With regard to the external factors, the overall plan for 2027 is based on the following planning assumptions:
All staff and non-staff personnel, including those in supervisory functions, avail themselves of opportunities to resolve workplace issues through established informal mechanisms;
All staff and non-staff personnel observe applicable United Nations regulations and rules regarding the terms and conditions of employment and apply lessons learned from decisions of the United Nations Dispute Tribunal and the United Nations Appeals Tribunal;
With regard to cooperation with other entities and inter-agency coordination and liaison, the Office will continue to build stronger collaboration with the formal system of administration of justice to explore opportunities for more referrals from the formal to the informal pillar. The Office will also continue to cooperate with the network of ombudsmen and mediators of the United Nations system so as to contribute to improved system-wide cohesion and impact in the area of conflict resolution in the workplace.
The Office integrates a gender perspective into its operational activities, deliverables and results as appropriate. This includes gender considerations in the delivery of conflict resolution services and in the identification of systemic issues, including feedback on gender-related inequities observed in cases presented to the Office. The Office will also continue to support the implementation of the strategy included in the system-wide policy on gender equality and the empowerment of women. The Office also compiles gender-disaggregated data.
In line with the United Nations Disability Inclusion Strategy, the Office will take into account the needs of persons with disabilities and facilitate their full and effective participation in its activities. In collaboration with the Department of Global Communications, the Office will maintain accessibility features on its website and online platforms to support users with temporary or long-term disabilities.

6.4 To contribute to the objective, the Office will support the work of the Committee on the Peaceful Uses of Outer Space and its subsidiary bodies and promote international cooperation in the exploration and use of outer space for peaceful purposes. Specifically, the Office will:
(a) Provide substantive secretariat services to the Committee, the Scientific and Technical Subcommittee, the Legal Subcommittee, the International Committee on Global Navigation Satellite Systems and its Providers’ Forum and the Space Mission Planning Advisory Group;
(b) Provide secretariat services to the working groups of the Committee and its subsidiary bodies, including the Working Groups on Legal Aspects of Space Resource Activities and on the Long-term Sustainability of Outer Space Activities, the Action Team on Lunar Activities Consultation and the Expert Group on Space Situational Awareness;
(c) Work with Member States, intergovernmental and, where relevant and applicable, non-governmental entities and other partners to promote the peaceful use of outer space, including through: (i) the implementation of the international legal framework governing space activities; (ii) the registration of space objects launched into outer space; (iii) space debris mitigation; (iv) transparency and confidence-building measures in outer space activities, as appropriate; (v) civil satellite-based positioning, navigation, timing and value added services; and (vi) assistance to the Committee in further developing the governance of outer space activities;
(d) Deliver training events and technical advisory support to assist States, upon their request, and institutions in: (i) enhancing capacities in areas such as space law and policy, space sustainability, space economy, climate change, environmental monitoring, natural resources management, precision farming and agriculture, water resources, oceans, global health, satellite communications, education, space weather, space exploration, astronomy, search and rescue, disaster risk reduction and emergency response, and global navigation satellite systems; and (ii) developing collaborative platforms and promoting knowledge-sharing for space-based solutions;
(e) Enhance key initiatives, such as the United Nations Space Bridge, the United Nations Space Sustainability Days and the United Nations Space Debates, to build the capacity of member States of the Committee and facilitate implementation of the international legal framework governing space activities in the lead-up to the convening of the Fourth United Nations Conference on the Exploration and Peaceful Uses of Outer Space (UNISPACE IV);
(f) Cooperate with the regional centres for space science and technology education affiliated to the United Nations to deliver capacity-building activities and training in space science and technology to support Member States in achieving the Sustainable Development Goals;
(g) Augment the impact of the work of the Committee and the Office on progress towards achieving the Sustainable Development Goals, through: (i) targeted outreach activities, publications and materials; (ii) exchanges with relevant stakeholders to promote more systematic sharing of space-based data and information to foster sustainable development and space sustainability; (iii) promotion of the widest possible adherence to, and full compliance with, the Treaty on Principles Governing the Activities of States in the Exploration and Use of Outer Space, including the Moon and Other Celestial Bodies, and of the role of the Committee in discussions on the establishment of new frameworks for space traffic, space debris and space resources; and (iv) the conduct of activities complementary to the work of the Committee and its working groups, including on the Pact for the Future;
(h) Provide support to Member States that express interest in Committee membership with a view to increasing access to space solutions, partnerships and capacity-building, and participation in the governance of outer space activities.
6.5 The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) A safer and more sustainable outer space;
(b) Space-based solutions enabling monitoring of and progress towards the achievement of the Sustainable Development Goals;
(c) Greater understanding, acceptance and application of the international legal framework governing space activities;
(d) An increased number of countries with access to space and space benefits, in particular developing countries, in using science and technology and related applications, including space-based solutions, to achieve sustainable development, disaster risk reduction and emergency response;
(e) Greater compatibility and interoperability of global navigation satellite systems;
(f) Space capabilities being more readily available through strengthened cooperative relationships and new partnerships.
6.6 With regard to the external factors, the overall plan for 2027 is based on the planning assumption that governmental, intergovernmental and non-governmental organizations are able and willing to support the Office in implementing the programme and responding to increased requests for assistance from Member States.
6.7 With regard to cooperation with other entities at the global, regional, national and local levels, the Office will, in line with its mandate, pursue new opportunities with established governmental, intergovernmental and non-governmental partners, including space agencies, industry, academia, institutions and other space-related entities, to strengthen the capacity of countries, in particular developing countries, in using space science and technology and their applications. The Office will also pursue cooperative relationships, including with the private sector. It will enhance transparency by regularly informing the Committee of the projects being implemented and of its partnerships with space agencies, governmental and non-governmental organizations and the private sector.
6.8 With regard to inter-agency coordination and liaison, the Office will lead the Inter-Agency Meeting on Outer Space Activities (UN-Space) to promote coherence and increase collaboration on space-related work among entities of the United Nations system, including through an inter-agency hub for procurement of earth observation imagery, with a view to better coordinating data-sharing and cooperation on the procurement of space-based information.
6.9 The Office integrates a gender perspective into its operational activities, deliverables and results, as appropriate. The Office will work with partners to continue implementing its Space for Women project to strengthen the capacity and skills of individuals and institutions, with an emphasis on enhancing the participation and retention of women and girls in space activities.
6.10 In line with the United Nations Disability Inclusion Strategy, the Office will promote inclusive practices for the employment of persons with disabilities in the space sector.

11.2 Over the past five years, the programme has developed robust analytical frameworks to examine development challenges in Africa through an integrated lens. The central challenge for 2027 is to translate this analytical foundation into policy solutions that address root causes, rather than short-term interventions that treat symptoms. Despite contributing less than 4 per cent of global greenhouse gas emissions, Africa faces climate adaptation costs that are projected to reach $50 billion annually by 2050, with 600 million people still lacking access to electricity. In addition, 307 million Africans, more than 20 per cent of the continent’s population, faced hunger in 2024, while Africa’s food import bill reached $97 billion in 2025 and climate change threatens to reduce crop yields by up to 30 per cent. Moreover, the continent records the world’s lowest science, technology, engineering and mathematics enrolment rates, creating critical capacity gaps precisely when Africa needs technically skilled workers to develop solutions to achieve sustainable agricultural systems, resilient food value chains and just and inclusive energy transitions. Addressing these challenges in isolation produces limited results. The programme’s strategy for 2027 recognizes that strategic investments in education, in particular in science, technology, engineering and mathematics and in science, technology and innovation, create the human capital necessary to innovate solutions, which in turn enable food systems transformation.
11.3 The implementation of the programme’s strategy is guided by the vision of an empowered Africa that drives its own development to fulfil its potential as a continent of hope, opportunities and prosperity, with the support of the international system. The programme will further strengthen its data capacities and knowledge base, to feed advisory services, awareness-raising, communications and advocacy that mobilize the United Nations system, African Member States, international partners, civil society and the private sector at the global and regional levels with regard to the vision of the African Union and in support of the joint implementation of the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development and Agenda 2063. In doing so, the programme will aim to foster improved coherence and synergies between the two agendas, through a consistent partnership on the basis of the strategic objectives of the second 10-year implementation plan of Agenda 2063.
11.4 The Office, ECA and the Department of Global Communications will continue to promote the new narrative through coordinated and complementary action in policy analysis and innovation, research and development, advocacy, communications, global and regional coordination, monitoring and evaluation and support for Member States. The strategic agenda, developed in consultation with the Group of African States and other stakeholders, continues to guide the programme activities in six cluster areas identified as key for Africa’s development because of their multiplying impact: shifting the paradigm on financing for development; achieving sustainable development to deliver durable peace; governance, resilience and human capital; science, technology and innovation; industrialization, the demographic dividend and trade, with a focus on the African Continental Free Trade Area; and a balanced energy mix and climate adaptation. Considering the crucial role that young people play in the continent’s development, special attention will be given to promoting youth-focused policies and proposals under each cluster area.
11.5 With regard to cooperation with other entities at the global, regional, national and local levels, the programme will continue to work closely with African Member States and African Union institutions as its main partners, in particular with the African Union Commission, the secretariat of the African Union Development Agency-NEPAD, the regional economic communities and the African Capacity-Building Foundation. The programme will also work closely with the African Peer Review Mechanism to advance the development goals of Africa and promote good governance, peace, security and sustainable development across the continent. The programme will continue to promote partnerships with an array of Member States, international organizations, civil society, the private sector, academia, think tanks and the African diaspora to enhance common approaches and synergies in their efforts to support Africa’s development in the key areas identified in the programme’s strategy.
11.6 With regard to inter-agency coordination and liaison, the programme will ensure strategic-level coordination among United Nations entities through the interdepartmental task force on African affairs by identifying priorities for building forward and building better, promoting results-based planning to develop the African Union-United Nations Framework for the Implementation of Agenda 2063 and the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development and monitoring the implementation of the agreed plans and priorities. The Regional Collaborative Platform for Africa will ensure operational-level coordination among United Nations entities in the field and with the resident coordinator system.
11.7 With regard to the external factors, the overall plan for 2027 is based on the following planning assumptions:
(a) African countries continue to guide their priorities towards implementing Agenda 2063;
(b) International partners continue to support Africa’s sustainable development through financial contributions, technical support and policy frameworks that support African ownership and empowerment;
(c) The partnership between the United Nations and the African Union is further strengthened through the continued implementation of the Joint United Nations-African Union Framework for Enhanced Partnership in Peace and Security, the renewed approach to the African Union-United Nations Framework for the Implementation of Agenda 2063 and the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development, and the African Union-United Nations Framework on Human Rights;
(d) International organizations and partners are committed to the implementation of multi-stakeholder projects and programmes in support of the 2030 Agenda, Agenda 2063 and the priorities of African countries;
(e) United Nations entities actively engage in the interdepartmental task force on African affairs and the Regional Collaborative Platform for Africa;
(f) External stakeholders and potential partners, including media organizations, academic institutions, civil society organizations and creative communities, continue to engage with the programme’s priorities;
(g) Member States and partners continue to advocate for and find joint solutions to mitigate the impacts of external shocks on Africa.
11.8 The programme integrates a gender perspective into its operational activities, deliverables and results, as appropriate, including through consultation with the United Nations Entity for Gender Equality and the Empowerment of Women (UN-Women), the African Union and specialized civil society organizations. Subprogramme 1 promotes inclusive programme delivery through its analytical and policy documents, as well as its advocacy activities. The Office collaborates with the African Women Leaders Network to support the participation and leadership of women in all areas of development and is working with the African Union to integrate a gender perspective into the African Union-United Nations strategic coordination process. Subprogramme 2 enables United Nations support for the implementation of the priorities of the African Union, including the use of sex-disaggregated statistics, and addressing challenges that have an effect on the employment of women. Subprogramme 3 promotes gender equality and the empowerment of women and girls by producing and disseminating communications products, through Africa Renewal platforms, that counter stereotypes and discrimination against women, and by promoting a balanced presence of women and men in all its multimedia products, promotional campaigns and events.
11.9 In line with the United Nations Disability Inclusion Strategy, the programme will promote analysis, policy recommendations and proposals that specifically foster the inclusion of persons with disabilities and will take the measures necessary to increase the accessibility of its products and events.

11.15 To contribute to the objective, the subprogramme will:
(a) Act as the focal point for the African Union Commission and its agencies at Headquarters; support enhanced collaboration between the United Nations system and the African Union Commission, regional economic communities, the African Union Development Agency-NEPAD, the African Peer Review Mechanism and other African Union communities; and contribute to strengthening the implementation of the African Union-United Nations Framework for the Implementation of Agenda 2063 and the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development;
(b) Coordinate the interdepartmental task force on African affairs and monitor United Nations system support for Africa’s sustainable development to promote strategic planning and programming in the United Nations system in Africa in accordance with the priorities of the African Union and African Member States as enshrined in Agenda 2063 and its second 10-year implementation plan;
(c) Perform horizon scanning and analyse emerging trends, challenges and opportunities in the areas of strategic focus of the programme, as well as develop policy proposals to maximize opportunities and minimize and mitigate identified risks, through the management of data and knowledge, and promote policy innovation initiatives that have the potential to act as multiplier interventions to support Africa’s sustainable development;
(d) Assess commitments made toward Africa’s development aligned with the 2030 Agenda and Agenda 2063, applying a cluster-based approach aimed at identifying challenges faced and, when appropriate, propose corrective measures;
(e) Facilitate intergovernmental deliberations on issues relating to Africa’s sustainable development and support African Member States in their participation in intergovernmental processes;
(f) Develop and implement communication and global advocacy strategies in support of Agenda 2063, in coordination with subprogrammes 2 and 3;
(g) Promote strategic partnerships with African Member States and African Union institutions, leveraging United Nations knowledge and capacities through technical cooperation with United Nations agencies, funds and programmes regarding flagship initiatives and projects;
(h) Implement a fellowship programme for young African scholars and engage in new initiatives with partners to strengthen institutional capacities in policy analysis, design and monitoring.
11.16 The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) Empowerment of African Member States to mobilize increased domestic resources for Africa’s development and maximize their impact through targeted data and evidence-based policies;
(b) Strengthened cooperation between the United Nations system and the African Union and other African institutions, as well as increased complementarity of their activities in support of the implementation of the 2030 Agenda and Agenda 2063;
(c) A more coherent and integrated approach by the United Nations system support for Africa to challenges affecting Africa’s development that includes and addresses existing gaps and leverages opportunities through impact-focused priorities with a multiplying impact;
(d) Stronger alignment of intergovernmental discussions with the priorities of African countries and the African Union, as well as increased integration of African perspectives into global debates and decisions;
(e) Greater accountability of stakeholders in the implementation of commitments to Agenda 2063 and the 2030 Agenda;
(f) Increased awareness and understanding of the international community of the perspective of the African Union and the realities and priorities of African countries.

11.28 To contribute to the objective, the subprogramme will:
(a) Conduct policy research and analysis on African Union priorities and transboundary issues relevant to Africa’s regional transformation and integration, and disseminate the findings across the five subregions of Africa;
(b) Provide technical assistance and advisory services within the framework of Agenda 2063 and the 2030 Agenda, taking into consideration the United Nations-African Union cooperation frameworks and the African Union strategic priorities, including the second 10-year implementation plan of Agenda 2063;
(c) Support the African Union Development Agency-NEPAD in the context of continental priorities in collaboration with subprogrammes 1 and 2, and build the capacity of subregional entities to formulate strategies for the integrated implementation of development priorities of regional economic communities and intergovernmental organizations in collaboration with ECA subprogrammes;
(d) Work under the architecture of the Regional Collaborative Platform for Africa, with the opportunity and issue-based coalitions, to promote coherent United Nations system-wide support for the African Union and for resident coordinators and United Nations country teams towards the realization of Agenda 2063 and the 2030 Agenda.
11.29 The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) Increased and more effective United Nations support for the African Union and its development priorities as reflected in Agenda 2063;
(b) Development and implementation by Member States of their national development priorities, aligned with global and regional frameworks on sustainable development.

11.41 To contribute to the objective, the subprogramme will:
(a) Develop global comprehensive editorial activities on priority issues affecting Africa, including by generating support in Africa and beyond for the African Union Development Agency-NEPAD and the implementation of Agenda 2063 and the 2030 Agenda, as well as the work of subprogrammes 1 and 2 and their advocacy strategies. This is under the umbrella of the strategy of the Africa Section of the Department of Global Communications and in line with the global communications strategy;
(b) Produce external communications content and engagement activities, including through the Africa Renewal digital platforms, media advisories and other public information opportunities, for use by external media, both African and international, academia and civil society groups, and provide external communications support to the Office;
(c) Produce or translate select content into languages such as French and Kiswahili.
11.42 The above-mentioned work is expected to result in enhanced awareness of key thematic issues of the United Nations, the African Union Development Agency-NEPAD and other stakeholders and partners on issues relating to economic and sustainable development in Africa.

To contribute to the objective, the Office will:
(a) Coordinate with United Nations entities, departments and offices to ensure the alignment and institutionalization of strategies to prevent and respond to sexual exploitation and abuse by developing initiatives to mitigate the risks of sexual exploitation and abuse across the United Nations system;
(b) Promote the implementation of the Secretary-General’s strategy to combat sexual exploitation and abuse across the system’s more than 30 affiliated funds, programmes and specialized agencies and act as the secretariat of the Secretary-General’s institutionalized standing bodies for protection against sexual exploitation and abuse, such as the High-level Steering Group on preventing sexual exploitation and abuse, convened by the Chef de Cabinet, which oversees the implementation of the Secretary-General’s strategy;
(c) Guide organization-wide change management to implement the updated Secretary-General’s Bulletin on sexual exploitation and abuse across all categories of United Nations personnel, both uniformed and civilian, and non-United Nations actors;
(d) Consult with international, regional and subregional organizations, Member States, United Nations independent human rights experts, civil society organizations, national human rights institutions, academic institutions and think tanks, as appropriate, to ensure that policies and operational efforts across the United Nations system reflect best practice;
(e) Monitor and enhance the Secretary-General’s public reporting mechanism for allegations of sexual exploitation and abuse;
(f) Strengthen accountability and transparency through a multifaceted approach which engages leadership across Member States and within the United Nations system, reinforces commitment, processes and engagement at the national level, fosters collaboration with Member States, augments investigative capacity, strengthens legislative frameworks and ensure a victim-centred, trauma-informed approach;
(g) Work closely with managers at all levels across the United Nations system to provide strategic support and guidance, and strengthen leadership capacity through advocacy, policy development and practical learning.
The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) Strengthened prevention of and responses to sexual exploitation and abuse in a coherent and coordinated manner, including improved awareness, risk mitigation, data collection, evidence-based interventions, monitoring and analysis of performance and improved victim support and assistance by the United Nations system;
(b) Institutionalization of an organizational culture that prohibits sexual exploitation and abuse and inaction to same, with a robust and consistent understanding among United Nations personnel of standards of conduct and the purpose of mission of the United Nations, to advance cultural change and address the underpinnings of sexual exploitation and abuse, including gender imbalances and the abuse of authority due to power differentials.
With regard to external factors, the overall plan for 2027 is based on the following planning assumptions:
(a) Personnel, at all levels, prioritize combating sexual exploitation and abuse within United Nations operations;
(b) Adequate resources are available to support delivery of the mandate;
(c) The United Nations system supports the alignment and institutionalization of measures to prevent and respond to sexual exploitation and abuse, including through sustained, predictable funding.
With regard to inter-agency coordination, the Office will continue to seek cooperation with all United Nations partners, including agencies, funds and programmes, and the Inter-Agency Standing Committee and its Champion on protection from sexual exploitation and abuse and sexual harassment, to ensure coordination with relevant mandates and a coherent and consistent response. The Office will also continue to collaborate with the Office of the Victims’ Rights Advocate, the Department of Management Strategy, Policy and Compliance and the Department of Global Communications, among other entities within the Organization, to ensure alignment of messages relating to the prevention of and response to sexual exploitation and abuse in internal and external communications. Furthermore, the Office will work closely with the Development Coordination Office and resident coordinators, globally, to support their roles and responsibilities in accordance with the management and accountability framework of the United Nations development and resident coordinator system. The Office continues to serve as the secretariat of the Secretary-General’s circle of leadership on the prevention of and response to sexual exploitation and abuse in United Nations operations, which comprises global leaders who actively support the agenda of combating sexual exploitation and abuse.
In line with the United Nations Disability Inclusion Strategy, the Office will identify ways to ensure the integration of disability inclusion across all programs and interventions. This involves mainstreaming disability inclusion through training and outreach material on preventing and responding to sexual exploitation and abuse, including on victim support, that is tailored to address the distinct needs and challenges of individuals with disabilities.

To contribute to the objective, the mission will:
Support signatory countries in efforts to implement the revitalized Peace, Security and Cooperation Framework;
Provide the Secretary-General’s good offices and enhance support to regional peace processes, including through assistance to mediation efforts and the implementation of agreements;
Support the implementation of regional non-military approaches to disarming, demobilizing, repatriating where applicable, and reintegrating members of armed groups, and help to strengthen existing security cooperation mechanisms;
Support the effective participation of civil society, women and youth in political and peacebuilding processes, and promote the economic empowerment of youth;
Promote regional economic integration, including the implementation of the recommendations of the high-level regional workshop on natural resources held in 2021 in Khartoum, Sudan;
Promote regional judicial cooperation, in close cooperation with the International Conference on the Great Lakes Region (ICGLR);
Support the implementation of the United Nations regional strategy and its Action Plan for the period 2025-2027, through coordination and provision of secretariat support to its Senior Policy Group and Implementation Support Mechanism.
The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
Signatory countries of the Peace, Security and Cooperation Framework implementing recommendations from the revitalization process to address root causes of conflict;
Signatory countries engaging in regional peace processes and implementing political agreements, contributing to the de-escalation of bilateral tensions and improving the security and humanitarian situation in eastern Democratic Republic of the Congo;
Countries concerned enhancing cooperation to address the threat posed by armed groups and coordinating regional disarmament, demobilization, repatriation and reintegration initiatives through the Contact and Coordination Group on non-military measures and its operational cell;
An increased number of civil society organizations, including women and youth, contributing to regional peace processes and cross-border peacebuilding initiatives;
Signatory countries strengthening regional judicial cooperation through the Great Lakes Judicial Cooperation Network;
Signatory countries increasing economic cooperation and implementing initiatives to curb the illicit exploitation of, and trade in natural resources, with a focus on artisanal gold and coltan;
United Nations entities enhancing coordination and ensuring greater alignment between national strategic cooperation frameworks and the objectives of the United Nations regional strategy.
With regard to the external factors, the overall plan for 2027 is based on the following planning assumptions:
Eastern Democratic Republic of the Congo will continue to face insecurity, while relations between some countries will remain tense despite diplomatic efforts;
The Washington and Doha processes and the African Union-led mediation will require continued support;
The African Union-led mediation will continue, requiring sustained support from the United Nations and regional and international partners;
Continued support will be required to strengthen the participation and contribution of women and youth in the implementation of peace agreements, advancement of peace processes, and community peacebuilding;
Signatory countries of the Peace, Security and Cooperation Framework will demonstrate renewed political will to implement their commitments;
International partners will continue to support efforts towards a political settlement of the conflict in the Great Lakes region.
With regard to cooperation with other entities, the mission will enhance coordination with ICGLR to support complementary approaches under the Peace, Security and Cooperation Framework and the ICGLR Pact on Security, Stability and Development in the Great Lakes Region. The Special Envoy will continue to participate in the International Contact Group.
With regard to inter-agency coordination and liaison, the mission will continue to work closely with the United Nations Organization Stabilization Mission in the Democratic Republic of the Congo (MONUSCO), including on political and technical support to the ongoing peace processes, as well as in the context of regional cooperation on non-military measures related to disarmament, demobilization, repatriation and reintegration. Support to regional peace efforts will continue to be coordinated through a mechanism comprising the mission, MONUSCO, the United Nations Regional Office for Central Africa and the United Nations Office to the African Union. The Senior Policy Group overseeing the United Nations regional strategy, chaired by the Special Envoy, along with its Implementation Support Mechanism and integrated secretariat, will continue to ensure alignment among United Nations entities in the region, leveraging expertise and information-sharing across offices, thereby enhancing resource efficiencies and synergies.
The mission integrates a gender perspective into its operational activities, deliverables and results. In addition to high-level advocacy initiatives, the mission supports the Advisory Board for Women, Peace and Security in the Great Lakes Region in its efforts to promote women’s participation in dialogue, peacebuilding, political and electoral processes, mainstream gender equality into regional economic initiatives, including in the mining sector, promote partnerships with member states to sensitise on sexual and gender-based violence, and promote the implementation of regional instruments on protection.

To contribute to the objective, the mission will:
Support regional peace and security, development, integration and inclusive governance, while addressing climate-security linkages, in line with the Pact for the Future. The mission will provide UN political leadership under the 2024-2028 Regional Prevention and Integration Strategy for the Horn of Africa (Regional Strategy), including preventive action on emerging conflicts. Strengthen cooperation with IGAD, the AU and partners to harmonize approaches through multilateral mechanisms;
Foster trust among member States and regional institutions through dialogue on divisive issues and encourage cooperation on climate adaptation, food security, infrastructure, Women Peace and Security (WPS) and Youth, Peace and Security (YPS) Agendas. The mission will leverage existing platforms, including the Friends of the Horn of Africa group and Horn of Africa Ambassadors, to promote constructive dialogues;
Utilize the Secretary-General’s good offices and the collective influence of IGAD member States to encourage dialogue and joint initiatives, including with middle-powers, while harmonising efforts among international partner;
Collaborate with IGAD, the AU and other partners in addressing security threats through improved information-sharing, partner coordination and coherent messaging. The mission will undertake preventive engagement on political transitions and elections with regional implications, including South Sudan in February 2027 and in contexts affected by the reconfiguration of UN presences. The mission will leverage the UN’s convening authority to complement AU and IGAD-led initiatives;
Support efforts to advance WPS and YPS in political dialogue and peace processes, including encouraging meaningful participation of women and youth and promoting inclusive approaches to conflict prevention and peacebuilding.
The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
Enhanced subregional capacities, improved trust among member States, stronger dialogue on cross-border and strategic issues, reduced tension and increased cooperation on shared challenges affecting regional stability;
Greater alignment of regional and international partners with UN, IGAD and AU strategic frameworks, advancing more coordinated regional approaches;
A more coherent One-UN approach in the region that supports the implementation of the Regional Strategy and integrates WPS, climate-related risks and youth participation across peace and security, development and human rights work.
With regard to the external factors, the overall plan for 2027 is based on the following planning assumptions:
IGAD and the AU remain fully engaged in regional peace and security, maintaining focus on Ethiopia, Eritrea, Somalia, the Sudan and South Sudan, and advancing approaches that integrate gender, climate-related and youth dimensions;
Regional states continue constructive engagement with the Special Envoy and the UN on capacity-building, particularly in mediation, conflict prevention and conflict resolution;
South Sudanese parties, with regional support, remain committed to implementing the Revitalized Agreement and ensuring a peaceful conclusion of the transitional period ending in February 2027, if not further extended;
Sudan - South Sudan relations remain constructive despite the war in the Sudan, allowing continued engagement on outstanding bilateral issues, including Abyei;
Relations among countries experiencing tensions continue on a path of de-escalation, allowing space for constructive dialogue;
Regional and national stakeholders remain committed to advancing WPS and YPS agendas, ensuring political space for meaningful participation of women and youth in political and peacebuilding processes.
With regard to cooperation with other entities, the mission works closely with IGAD and its member States, the AU, the European Union and other partners to address regional dimensions of crises, focusing on cross-border implications and supporting political cooperation and regional integration.
With regard to inter-agency coordination and liaison, the mission will continue its close engagement with the Personal Envoy of the Secretary-General for the Sudan, the Special Representative of the Secretary-General to the African Union, the Special Representative and Head of the United Nations Mission in South Sudan, Resident Coordinators, Peace and Development Advisers and UN entities across the Horn of Africa region to ensure a coherent approach to prevention. In 2027, this coordination will strengthen information-sharing and joint analysis, while reducing duplication to enable a more efficient and targeted use of resources.
The mission integrates a gender perspective across its operational activities, including good offices, missions, seminars and workshops. It promotes inclusive messaging and strengthens women’s participation in political and peace processes through engagement in regional and global WPS forums.
In line with the United Nations Disability Inclusion Strategy, the mission will support IGAD and the AU in enhancing the participation of persons with disabilities in political, peace and security initiatives.

To contribute to the objective and address the deteriorating situation in Myanmar, the mission will:
Implement a multitrack approach, in accordance with the Pact for the Future, including strengthening cooperation between the United Nations and ASEAN. In close coordination with the Special Envoy of the ASEAN Chair and United Nations partners, the Special Envoy of the Secretary-General will seek to support Myanmar-led processes for the resolution of the crisis. The Special Envoy will complement regional efforts to support the effective implementation of the ASEAN five-point consensus, aligned with the needs of the people of Myanmar, and will support the coordinated humanitarian and development engagement of the United Nations system;
Advocate for a Myanmar-led process towards a peaceful, democratic and inclusive future and promote a coherent international and regional approach. The mission will continue to initiate inclusive and participatory approaches with minorities, youth and women, whose voices are critical to fostering social cohesion and developing a way out of the current political crisis in line with the will of the people;
Continue efforts to create an environment that is conducive to the voluntary, safe, dignified and sustainable return to Rakhine State of the forcibly displaced Rohingya in the region, including from Bangladesh. The Special Envoy will continue to promote a political solution that addresses the underlying issues in consultation with the Rohingya, and to advocate for the improvement of the conditions on the ground, in line with the recommendations of the Advisory Commission on Rakhine State and the commitments made at the General Assembly High-level Conference on the Situation of Rohingya Muslims and Other Minorities in Myanmar, held on 30 September 2025.
The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
Improved conditions for the safe, voluntary, dignified and sustainable return of forcibly displaced populations;
The creation of conditions that are conducive to a de-escalation of violence and an increase in trust among communities;
Greater international and regional political support towards Myanmar-led processes for the resolution of the crisis;
Key stakeholders advancing discussions towards the establishment of a Myanmar-led process on restoring democratic and human rights norms, and continued engagement with all stakeholders to identify potential for common ground.
With regard to the external factors, the overall plan for 2027 is based on the following planning assumptions:
A viable path back to civilian rule is grounded on cessation of violence and a genuine commitment to inclusive dialogue with the full participation of civil society, including women, youth, ethnic and minority communities;
The impact on civilians from the continued violence since the military takeover, serious human rights violations and the need for civilian protection will remain a significant challenge in restoring peace in the country;
The situation will continue to require concrete actions towards improved conditions to allow for the safe, dignified, voluntary and sustainable return of the Rohingya and their reintegration in Myanmar. The rights and security of the Rohingya people need to be guaranteed and embedded in Myanmar-led solutions towards a peaceful, democratic and inclusive future;
Illicit activities and related financial flows, closely linked to the conflict in Myanmar and the downfall of the formal economy, will continue to hamper conflict resolution and impede socioeconomic and development efforts. Expansion of transnational crime networks emanating from Myanmar will have increasing regional implications.
With regard to cooperation with other entities, the mission works in partnership with ASEAN, especially the Special Envoy of the ASEAN Chair, other key regional organizations, such as the Organization of Islamic Cooperation (OIC) and the European Union, neighbouring countries and international donors to mobilize support to Myanmar-led processes for the resolution of the crisis.
The mission coordinates closely with the United Nations system through established channels, in close consultation with the Resident Coordinators in Myanmar and Bangladesh. The Special Envoy of the Secretary-General continues to carry out the good offices mandate in close cooperation with the United Nations country team, regional offices and Headquarters, as well as mechanisms on Myanmar mandated by the Human Rights Council. The mission also works in partnership with United Nations entities to foster inclusion of diverse and vulnerable groups, such as women and youth, in dialogue processes.
The mission integrates gender perspectives into its operational activities, deliverables and results, as appropriate, and supports efforts to strengthen the capacity of Myanmar women to participate regionally and globally in women and peace and security engagements and to amplify the voices of the most marginalized women.
In line with the United Nations Disability Inclusion Strategy, the mission will foster the inclusion of persons with disabilities in its political strategy and its efforts on building inclusion.

To contribute to the objective, the mission will seek to support efforts to foster consensus on a Lebanese-led and Lebanese-owned national dialogue, which is expected to result in progress on addressing the maintenance of weapons outside State authority.
The above-mentioned work is expected to result in informed Security Council deliberations on progress made by Lebanon on the outstanding provisions of Council resolution 1559 (2004).
With regard to inter-agency coordination and liaison, the mission coordinates with the Office of the United Nations Special Coordinator for Lebanon, in particular in the process of developing the reports of the Secretary-General.
With regard to the external factors, the overall plan for 2027 is based on the following planning assumptions:
The regional environment continues to affect Lebanon’s stability and security;
Following the 2026 legislative elections and the formation of a new government, the Lebanese leadership pursues efforts to deliver reforms and tangible benefits for the population as it faces reconstruction challenges;
The Lebanese government has reaffirmed its commitment to ensuring a State monopoly over the maintenance of weapons;
Member States within and outside the region remain engaged in supporting the disbanding and disarmament of Lebanese and non-Lebanese militias;
Violations of Lebanese sovereignty and territorial integrity persist.
The mission integrates a gender perspective into its operational activities by ensuring appropriate consultations with all stakeholders, including women leaders, and the inclusion of gender-disaggregated data, as appropriate.
In line with the United Nations Disability Inclusion Strategy, the mission will support mitigating measures to overcome accessibility challenges, in addition to the efforts undertaken by relevant United Nations entities to ensure the accessibility of facilities, platforms, security and other infrastructure to facilitate easy access and use by persons with disabilities.

To contribute to the objective, the mission will:
Lead mediation efforts with the parties, supported by regional and international actors, to identify confidence-building measures and advance the political process. To ensure inclusive participation and the reflection of the priorities of the broader Yemeni population, the mission will regularly consult Yemeni political parties, women, youth, civil society and other stakeholders through inclusive consultation formats. The mission will mediate further releases of conflict-related detainees;
Engage the parties and security actors, including through the Military Coordination Committee as part of the efforts to reach a Yemen-wide ceasefire and agreements on longer term security arrangements. The mission will also chair the Redeployment Coordination Committee, monitor and report on relevant developments, and coordinate mine action to implement the residual tasks of the Hudaydah Agreement;
Mediate between the parties, while also engaging technical experts, the private sector, international financial institutions and other relevant stakeholders, to advance near-term economic de-escalation and confidence-building measures, as well as to identify solutions to longer-term economic issues essential to a comprehensive resolution to the conflict;
Continue to support the parties to implement the Hudaydah Agreement through the collection and collation of information pertaining to the implementation of the Agreement, chairing of the Redeployment Coordination Committee in order to achieve and sustain de-escalation, supporting measures to mitigate security risks affecting civilians and civilian movement, advancement of mine action interventions, and further releases of conflict-related detainees in furtherance of the broader UN-facilitated political process.
Engage with regional stakeholders, the UN Security Council, and the wider international community to maintain concerted diplomatic backing for the UN mediation process;
Utilize strategic communications to support the implementation of the mission’s mandate by strengthening public understanding of the UN-facilitated political process, enhancing transparency across the political, economic and security tracks, and fostering an informed information environment that mitigates misinformation and supports constructive engagement among Yemeni stakeholders.
The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
Progress towards agreements between the parties on political, economic and military/security measures that improve living conditions in Yemen and contribute to a sustainable, peaceful and inclusive settlement of the conflict;
Engagement with diverse Yemeni political and social stakeholders, including women, youth, media entities and the broader public, to inform the UN-led mediation process;
Sustained, coherent international and regional support for the UN-led mediation process.
With regard to cooperation with other entities, the mission engages closely with international and regional organizations, including the League of Arab States, the Gulf Cooperation Council, the European Union and the International Committee of the Red Cross, and other international partners, including international financial institutions.
With regard to inter-agency coordination and liaison, the mission cooperates with the Resident/Humanitarian Coordinator and the United Nations country team in Yemen. The mission consults and coordinates closely with the Department of Political and Peacebuilding Affairs and with other special political missions in the region.
With regard to the external factors, the overall plan for 2027 is based on the following planning assumptions:
The parties will continue to engage with the Office and, to the extent possible, facilitate access and conditions necessary to sustain dialogue, support de-escalation efforts and enable continued engagement with Yemeni stakeholders, amid a constrained and evolving operating environment;
Despite a complex mediation environment impacted by regional dynamics, ongoing conflict, and a constrained operating environment, international and Yemeni support for the UN-led mediation process is sustained;
The mission maintains its presence in Yemen in Sana’a and Aden and continues to have access to key Yemeni stakeholders, including through digital means, albeit with restrictions due to the security situation on the ground.
The mission continues to integrate a gender perspective into its operations, including by ensuring the meaningful inclusion of women in the mediation process and by ensuring their perspectives inform the negotiation agenda and outcomes.
In line with the United Nations Disability Inclusion Strategy, the mission will ensure that its engagement approaches are inclusive of persons with disabilities.

To contribute to the objective, the Office will strengthen mechanisms for the protection of children used in armed conflict, prevent these violations from occurring in the first place, and improve measures to better protect conflict-affected children. Specifically, the Office will:
(a) Monitor and report on violations against children in situations of armed conflict through the reinforcement of existing child protection capacities on the ground and facilitate information exchanges to identify and mitigate trends in the use of children in armed conflict, including during transition phases of relevant United Nations peace operations, in line with Security Council resolution 2764 (2024), the most recent Council resolution on children and armed conflict;
(b) Enhance its diplomatic engagement, including through direct dialogue and/or country visits by the Special Representative, with parties to armed conflict to promote ending and preventing the use and abuse of children, and continue to advocate the protection of children affected by conflict and the prevention of grave violations against them through analysis, public outreach and targeted support, as well as the development of groups of friends of the mandate in affected States and regions;
(c) Develop community, national, subregional and regional prevention plans, as well as other preventive measures, guided by Security Council resolutions 2427 (2018) and 2601 (2021), while promoting the adoption of international norms and standards for the protection of children by Member States;
(d) Analyse the best practices emerging from ongoing activities to stop grave violations committed against children and promote the continued roll-out of specific public awareness campaigns in all countries and regions where there is active armed conflict and, whenever possible, in all official languages of the United Nations;
(e) Develop curricula with partners, conduct training for defence and security personnel and assist in capacity-building for governing structures, United Nations partners, academics and civil society;
(f) Undertake research into the six grave violations, with a focus on recruitment and use of children, provide guidelines, as relevant, to assist in their monitoring, promote the comprehensive discussion of reintegration needs and promote the inclusion of child protection issues into peace processes;
(g) Organize and participate in seminars, workshops, conferences and other relevant meetings, including with regard to the implementation of the Pact for the Future and the working group on peace and security, to raise awareness of child protection and prevention best practices and proposed best international standards on the protection and release of children who are victims of the six grave violations;
(h) Enhance data analysis and information management capacity to better capture the differentiated protection needs of children, including on the basis of age, gender and disability, and whether they are on the move, internally displaced, refugees or victims of cross-border trafficking, and the factors that can exacerbate their vulnerability, such as climate change, poverty and inequality, the proliferation of and illicit trafficking in arms, the impact of landmines, explosive remnants of war and improvised explosive devices, and the impact of new technologies.
(i) Support Member States in making progress towards the achievement of the Sustainable Development Goals, including Goals 3, 4, 5 and 8, in particular target 8.7 on the eradication of child labour, including the recruitment and use of child soldiers; Goals 10 and 16, in particular target 16.2 on ending abuse, exploitation, trafficking and all forms of violence against and torture of children; and Goal 17, in particular target 17.18 on enhancing capacity-building support to increase significantly the availability of high-quality, timely and reliable data disaggregated by income, gender, age, race, ethnicity, migratory status, disability, geographical location and other characteristics.
The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) A decrease in the number of violations committed against children in armed conflict and an increase in the number of children released by armed forces and groups and reintegrated, including through repatriation and removal from situations of armed conflict, in line with the commitments agreed to in action plans, handover and release protocols, other commitments and mutually agreed practices;
(b) Stronger national and regional protection and prevention systems and accountability mechanisms through justice, remediation and reparation processes, involving Member States, to address gaps in knowledge, common standards and procedures for dealing with children under threat;
(c) Strengthened efforts to protect children, including those threatened by intercommunal violence and the emergence of new parties to armed conflict, and specific groups of children at risk;
(d) Increased mitigation capacity of Governments to prevent violations and ensure equal access to and the continuation of education in conflict;
(e) An international political consensus that tailored, systematic and sustainable reintegration support should be provided to all released children, including those repatriated or removed from armed conflict zones, and that child protection issues should be included in all peace processes, in order to break the cycle of violence;
(f) Enhanced capacity of Member States and United Nations country task forces on monitoring and reporting, and their equivalents in countries where there is no monitoring and reporting mechanism in place, to support children and vulnerable communities on the ground and to ensure the continuity, effectiveness and sustainability of child protection activities, including during mission transitions or withdrawals;
(g) Increased participation of children in decision-making processes relating to issues that have an impact on them, including their protection and reintegration needs, in accordance with the Convention on the Rights of the Child.
With regard to external factors, the overall plan for 2027 is based on the following planning assumptions:
(a) The political environment would be supportive for the improved measures put in place to end and prevent violations committed against children in situations of armed conflict in all situations of the children and armed conflict agenda;
(b) The political environment would be supportive of international human rights law, in particular the rights of the child, and international humanitarian law, in particular the special respect and protections afforded to children;
(c) The dynamics of contemporary armed conflict in which children are normally victimized allow for access to monitor and assist in the implementation of these measures.
With regard to cooperation with other entities at the global, regional, national and local levels, the Office will continue to garner support for the protection of children in armed conflict, including through engagement with Member States and groups of friends on children and armed conflict, to keep the plight of conflict-affected children high on the global political agenda and to advocate the ratification and implementation of the Convention on the Rights of the Child and its Optional Protocol on the involvement of children in armed conflict, and the endorsement of political commitments. The Office will reinforce partnerships with regional and subregional actors for the adoption of measures to prevent grave violations before they occur and engage with mediation actors to encourage the inclusion of child protection issues in mediation and peace processes. The Office will also work closely with civil society organizations, communities, academia and research institutes to build expertise and raise awareness of the protection of children in armed conflict.
With regard to inter-agency coordination and liaison, the Office will continue to support country task forces on monitoring and reporting on grave violations against children and engage with parties to conflict to strengthen advocacy for the adoption of measures to end and prevent these violations, including through capacity-building initiatives. The Office will continue to support the dissemination of technical guidelines through the Monitoring and Reporting Mechanism Technical Reference Group, an inter-agency group bringing together the Office, the United Nations Children’s Fund (UNICEF), the Department of Peace Operations and the Department of Political and Peacebuilding Affairs to provide guidance to United Nations partners implementing the mandate in the field. The Office will also support the exchange of information within the Task Force on Children and Armed Conflict at United Nations Headquarters. In addition, the Office will continue to strengthen its cooperation with the Offices of the Special Representative of the Secretary-General on Violence against Children and the Special Representative of the Secretary-General on Sexual Violence in Conflict, notably on monitoring and reporting on sexual violence affecting children, through information exchange, common messaging and joint events and initiatives, for a more effective and coordinated advocacy to ensure that the plight of conflict-affected children is visible on the international agenda.
The Office integrates a gender perspective into its operational activities, deliverables and results, as appropriate. In 2027, the Office will continue to mainstream a gender perspective into the Monitoring and Reporting Mechanism to strengthen its gender-sensitive analyses of grave violations against children in armed conflict, including through enhanced data analysis and support for the capacity-building activities of its field partners. The Office will follow up on the outcomes of a specialized workshop delivered to country task forces on monitoring and reporting in 2023 through a review of the draft tipsheet for strengthening gender analysis in monitoring and reporting and examine the operationalization of the workshop’s report and recommendations within the Monitoring and Reporting Mechanism Technical Reference Group, which provides guidance to field-based United Nations monitors of grave violations committed against children in armed conflict. The Special Representative will continue advocacy for the protection of girls’ education from attacks, including within the framework of the endorsement and implementation of the Safe Schools Declaration. The Office will also ensure that the platforms that it creates, alongside partners, will allow for the voices of children to be heard in relevant forums and for the differentiated experience between girls and boys to be highlighted. Wherever possible, the Office will mainstream a gender perspective and examine the intersection between gender and other factors in the research that it conducts and the lessons learned and best practices that it collects and disseminates on the protection of conflict-affected children. The Office will also develop a concise policy aimed at more systematically integrating a gender perspective into its work.
In line with the United Nations Disability Inclusion Strategy, the Office will strengthen its efforts to address the protection requirements of children with disabilities in armed conflict. The Office will build on the discussion paper on the protection of children with disabilities in armed conflict published in 2023 by continuing to disseminate its conclusions and advocating with partners for the implementation of its recommendations, including the strengthening of data collection on children with disabilities in armed conflict; the consultation of persons with disabilities, including children, and the organizations that represent them; and the provision of awareness-raising and capacity-building initiatives on disability inclusion. The Office will continue its advocacy efforts to raise awareness of the plight of children with disabilities in armed conflict, and mainstream disability inclusion and its intersection with other diversity factors.

To contribute to the objective, the Office will continue to:
Monitor and report on sexual violence in conflict to the United Nations Security Council and other relevant United Nations bodies, including by establishing monitoring, analysis and reporting arrangements to compile updates from situations of concern;
Provide coherent and strategic leadership for the United Nations system on addressing conflict-related sexual violence, including through the United Nations Action against Sexual Violence in Conflict network to enhance prevention, service provision and system-wide coordination and response, and through the United Nations Team of Experts on the Rule of Law and Sexual Violence in Conflict to assist national authorities in strengthening the rule of law to ensure accountability for perpetrators of conflict-related sexual violence;
Engage in advocacy, on behalf of the United Nations system including through direct dialogue and/or country visits by the Special Representative, with governments, military and judicial representatives, as well as directly with parties to armed conflict on addressing conflict-related sexual violence, with a priority focus on justice and accountability as a key element of deterrence and prevention;
Support efforts at the country-level to prevent and respond to conflict-related sexual violence, including through the deployment of and support to Women Protection Advisers to situations of conflict, with a particular focus on mission transition contexts and non-mission settings, as well as the development of action plans, curricula and provision of training for national defence and security forces;
Encourage the United Nations sanctions committees to incorporate sexual violence considerations into their programmes of work pursuant to Security Council resolutions 1960(3) (2010); 2106(13) (2012); 2331 (2016); and 2467 (2019).
Enhance data analysis and information management capacity of the United Nations system to better capture the differentiated protection needs of survivors of conflict-related sexual violence, and factors that can exacerbate their vulnerability;
Adopt and promote a survivor-centred approach in the implementation of all aspects of the mandate, in coordination with survivors of sexual violence, survivors’ networks, civil society, regional organizations and relevant United Nations entities, in particular at the country level.
In doing so, the Office will support Member States to make progress towards the achievement of Sustainable Development Goals 5 and 16.
The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
Adoption of concrete and time-bound commitments to prevent and address incidents, patterns and trends of conflict-related sexual violence, and accelerated implementation of existing political and operational agreements with affected States, including through increased measures taken by parties to armed conflict to prevent and protect against conflict-related sexual violence;
Increased access to survivor-centred justice, accountability and reparations for all survivors of conflict-related sexual violence, strengthened capacity of rule of law institutions and accountability mechanisms to deliver justice for CRSV at national level, increased number of national legislations related to conflict-related sexual violence aligned with international standards and best practices;
Improved access to holistic services, including medical care, psychosocial support, and livelihood support for all survivors, as well as for children born of conflict-related sexual violence;
Security Council and other relevant bodies have access to timely and reliable United Nations verified reporting and analysis on trends and patterns of violations and response to conflict-related sexual violence;
Increased capacity, coherence and coordination of the United Nations to prevent and respond to conflict-related sexual violence in line with a survivor-centered and trauma-informed approach;
Increased political consensus in multilateral and national settings that conflict-related sexual violence, in line with United Nations Security Council resolutions, should be addressed in peace, ceasefire, and other conflict-resolution processes and that survivors should have a role in these processes.
With regard to the external factors, the overall plan for 2027 is based on the following planning assumptions:
Continued high level of political will on the part of all stakeholders, in particular Member States, to work towards preventing and responding to sexual violence and ending the impunity of persistent perpetrators in situations of armed conflict and other situations of concern;
Member States and other key stakeholders see a convergence between their strategic interests and priorities and upholding the international protection framework that has been established to prevent and address conflict-related sexual violence;
The dynamics of armed conflict allow for access to monitor and assist in the implementation of political commitments to prevent and respond to conflict-related sexual violence;
Adequate extrabudgetary resources continue to be available to support delivery of the mandate.
With regard to cooperation with other entities at the global, regional, national and local levels, the Office will sustain its engagement with regional and subregional actors and multilateral institutions, including the African Union, the League of Arab States, the European Union, the North Atlantic Treaty Organization, the Organization for Security and Co-operation in Europe, the Economic Community of West African States, the International Conference on the Great Lakes Region, the International Organization of la Francophonie, the Inter-Parliamentary Union and the African Development Bank.
With regard to inter-agency coordination and liaison, the Office will continue to work through the inter-agency network, United Nations Action against Sexual Violence in Conflict, a coalition of 27 United Nations entities, which is chaired by the Special Representative. The network will continue to provide strategic advocacy, knowledge generation, capacity building, coordination, and catalytic country-level support, guided by the recent system-wide needs assessment of the monitoring, analysis and reporting arrangements on conflict-related sexual violence, including through the formulation and dissemination of joint advocacy products to call upon the international community to provide political and financial support for survivors in situations of conflict and the development of new resources and technical guidance on emerging issues. Advocacy, coordination and the delivery of expert advice and assistance on justice and accountability dimensions of the mandate will continue to be conducted through the Team of Experts on the Rule of Law and Sexual Violence in Conflict, which operates under the strategic direction of the Special Representative, and comprises the Office, OHCHR, the United Nations Development Programme and the Department of Peace Operations as the co-lead entities. In addition, the Office will continue to strengthen its cooperation with, inter alia, the Office of the Special Representative of the Secretary-General on Children and Armed Conflict, the Office of the Special Representative of the Secretary-General on Violence against Children, the United Nations Global Counter-Terrorism Coordination Compact, the United Nations Resident Coordinator System and the Department of Political and Peacebuilding Affairs and the Department of Peace Operations through periodic coordination meetings and information-sharing, to inform joint advocacy and operational strategies related to the prevention of and response to conflict-related sexual violence.
The Office integrates a gender perspective into its operational activities, deliverables and results, as appropriate. Given the nature of the Office’s mandate, which is closely linked to the women and peace and security agenda, all its activities are ultimately aimed at promoting gender equality and women’s empowerment, as well as at addressing discrimination and harmful social norms that constitute invisible drivers of conflict-related sexual violence. As such, political commitments negotiated with parties to conflict (e.g., joint communiqués, frameworks of cooperation and implementation plans) will continue to integrate the views of women’s civil society organizations and will continue to promote the meaningful participation of women and girls in countries where they are often absent in political and decision-making processes, including peace processes. Prevention strategies, and response programming will reflect a tailored approach to address sexual violence against all survivors,. Information, including on incidents of conflict-related sexual violence, will continue to be systematically disaggregated by sex to allow for a gender-sensitive analysis and better inform action by the Security Council.
In line with the United Nations Disability Inclusion Strategy, the Office will continue to incorporate issues relating to disability inclusion into policy, advocacy, communications, and key substantive aspects of its work, including programming, monitoring and reporting in priority situations. The NGO-civil society advisory group of the Office includes entities representing individuals with disabilities and, through this modality, the Office will continue to engage with individuals with disabilities and with relevant organizations during the course of field missions in priority countries. The Office’s staff includes individuals with explicit human rights expertise relating to the rights of persons with disabilities, including those targeted with sexual violence in situations of concern.

To contribute to the objective, the Office will:
(a) Promote the acceleration of efforts towards the achievement of the Sustainable Development Goals relating to addressing all forms and drivers of violence against children and gender-based violence by undertaking high-level advocacy at the global, regional and national levels in collaboration with key stakeholders;
(b) Support Member States in the implementation of their pledges arising from the first Global Ministerial Conference on Ending Violence against Children;
(c) Support Member States of the Pathfinding Global Alliance on Ending Violence against Children, noted by the General Assembly in its resolution 80/190 both as a contribution and accelerator of the prevention and protection of children against violence, in the implementation of their identified priority actions facilitating peer learning and knowledge-sharing among States;
(d) Engage and support political, legislative, programmatic and monitoring processes at the global, regional, national and local levels to strengthen investment in child protection and well-being; conduct country visits in close cooperation with United Nations resident coordinators and regional or country teams; provide technical cooperation and practical guidance to Member States, including with respect to strengthening the collection of data on violence against children; share context-specific promising practices; enhance demand-driven bilateral and multilateral partnerships;
(e) Increase the attention given to violence against children in the United Nations system, including through leading the Inter-Agency Working Group on Violence against Children and through advocacy for the system-wide mainstreaming of child rights, including the integration of child protection into United Nations programmatic tools, such as the United Nations Sustainable Development Cooperation Frameworks;
(f) Advocate, as Chair of the United Nations task force on children deprived of liberty, for the use of the detention of children to be only a measure of last resort; work towards prevention, rehabilitation and community-based alternatives to deprivation of liberty; and gather and report on promising practices regarding alternatives to detention and institutionalization;
(g) Promote the role of children as agents of change through inclusive child participation in decision-making processes and the documentation and dissemination of child-led and peer-to-peer initiatives;
(h) Promote investment in integrated, cross-sectoral child- and gender-sensitive protection and violence prevention;
(i) Promote child protection as a cross-cutting theme in global, regional and national action to address trafficking in children.
The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) Action to prevent and respond to violence against children mainstreamed into law, policy and programmes through costed intersectoral action plans supported by strong monitoring, coordination and information management systems;
(b) Increased awareness of and action on key thematic and frontier issues requiring specific attention;
(c) Sustained investment in cross-sectoral services for children and their caregivers to prevent and respond to violence against children and gender-based violence;
(d) Adoption and effective implementation of prevention measures, rehabilitation and community-based alternatives to the deprivation of liberty of children;
(e) More effective and systematic involvement of children in decision-making processes, including through an increase in safe and empowering pathways provided by States.
With regard to the external factors, the overall plan for 2027 is based on the following planning assumptions:
(a) Governments have the political commitment and dedicate resources to accelerate action to prevent and end violence against children, in the framework of the Sustainable Development Goals;
(b) Civil society, faith-based organizations, local communities, the donor community and the private sector are committed to working together for and with children.
With regard to cooperation with other entities at the global, regional, national and local levels, the Office will continue to strengthen cooperation with intergovernmental regional and subregional bodies. The Office will continue to forge regular engagement with United Nations entities, State partners and others, including regional organizations, based away from New York, building on the strong liaison that it has established and ensuring stronger coherence around child protection and wellbeing frameworks. The Office will pursue strong collaboration with civil society and faith-based organizations and the private sector.
With regard to inter-agency coordination and liaison, the Office continues to promote and strengthen collaboration within the United Nations system on ending violence against children, including with the members of the Inter-Agency Working Group on Violence against Children, the members of the United Nations task force on children deprived of liberty, the Office of the Special Representative of the Secretary-General for Children and Armed Conflict, the Special Representative of the Secretary-General on Sexual Violence in Conflict, the special procedures of the Human Rights Council, the human rights treaty bodies, the Special Adviser to the Secretary-General on Africa, the United Nations Youth Office, the Office for Digital and Emerging Technologies, the Department of Economic and Social Affairs, United Nations resident coordinators and country team members.
The Office integrates a gender perspective into its operational activities, deliverables and results, as appropriate, including in its advocacy to highlight how various forms of violence affect boys and girls differently and how this must inform and shape responses. Addressing gender discrimination and harmful social norms, as well as connections between violence against women and violence against children, is crucial to preventing and responding to violence against children and will continue to constitute an integral part of the Office’s work.
In line with the United Nations Disability Inclusion Strategy and the principle of leaving no child behind, the Office places an emphasis on addressing some of the most vulnerable and neglected children, among whom are children with disabilities. Promoting policies and strategies for alternative means of care will continue to be at the core of the Office’s advocacy. The Office will continue to pay particular attention to the inclusion of children with disabilities in all its consultations.

To contribute to the objective, the mission will:
Through good offices, consultations and shuttle diplomacy, support dialogue between the parties towards a cessation of hostilities, enhanced protection of civilians and the launch of an inclusive Sudanese-led political process, with the full, equal, meaningful and safe participation of women, in close collaboration with national, regional and international stakeholders;
Support efforts aimed at reaching and implementing temporary truces and sequenced steps toward a cessation of hostilities, including confidence-building measures, in coordination with relevant regional and international actors;
Support the coordination of international mediation initiatives by bringing together key regional and international stakeholders to enhance the overall effectiveness, coherence and integration of these initiatives and maximize their comparative advantages. Efforts will be aimed at harmonizing tracks of multilateral engagement, maximizing impact and leveraging peacemaking efforts, and advancing strategies on ensuring an inclusive process;
Foster continued regional and international support for resolving the conflict in the Sudan, notably through advocacy, frequent dialogue and information exchange;
Sustain and support consultations with a broad range of Sudanese civilian forces, including women and youth, to contribute to the United Nations initiatives aimed at advancing a cessation of hostilities, improving civilian protection in the Sudan and supporting an inclusive Sudanese-led political process.
The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
Parties making progress towards a negotiated cessation of hostilities, including through localized or issue-specific agreements, including those relating to transitional security arrangements, the protection of civilians and the advancement of humanitarian access;
Technical coordination mechanisms in place to effectively enable efforts to reach and implement negotiated humanitarian truces and cessation of hostilities agreements -including through clear roles and responsibilities, communication mechanisms, and reporting structures;
Peace efforts being well coordinated, with clarity on the roles and respective responsibilities of stakeholders, on the basis of comparative advantage and taking account of capacities, leverage and any prior engagement on the issues, while giving effect to the principle of partnership and ensuring effective coordination of facilitation and other engagements;
Possible outcomes of the peace and political processes being agreed through established mediation or facilitation frameworks;
Civilian stakeholders being effectively engaged and informed on United Nations peacebuilding and peacemaking initiatives.
With regard to cooperation and coordination with other entities, the mission will liaise closely with all international and regional stakeholders involved in support of peace efforts in the Sudan, in particular the African Union, IGAD, LAS, and the European Union as well as neighbouring States. In doing so, it will work through established regional and Member State mechanisms to deepen strategic and operational engagements in carrying out good offices and mediation. It will also liaise closely with civilian actors through the various established civilian peacemaking platforms.
With regard to inter-agency coordination and liaison, the mission will cooperate with the Resident Coordinator/Humanitarian Coordinator and the agencies, funds and programmes of the United Nations operating in the Sudan and the region, and coordinate closely with the Department of Political and Peacebuilding Affairs and special political missions and peacekeeping operations in the region
With regard to the external factors, the overall plan for 2027 is based on the following planning assumptions:
The situation in the Sudan is likely to remain highly volatile, with the possibility of further fragmentation of political dynamics and increased hostilities. While a nationwide agreement on a cessation of hostilities may take time, there could be temporary, localized ceasefires;
Shifting regional and international dynamics may create intermittent openings for de-escalation or renewed political engagement, including limited humanitarian truces or other focused measures creating space to alleviate civilian suffering and build incremental confidence between the parties in view of a permanent ceasefire;
The international community will remain actively engaged in support of peace efforts in the Sudan, and the parties to the conflict will continue to engage in discussions with the Personal Envoy to advance a Sudanese-led political process.
The mission integrates a gender perspective into its operational activities, deliverables and results, as appropriate. This includes promoting the full, equal, meaningful and safe participation of women in political and mediation processes and by ensuring that gender consideration inform analysis, engagement and reporting. The mission supports activities to advance gender-related issues and provides support for greater civic engagement towards more inclusive peace and political processes. The mission prioritizes regular consultations with Sudanese women to solicit their insights, views on developments in Sudan, and perspectives on the protection of civilians, particularly women, equal access to humanitarian aid and other concerns pertaining to participation in peace processes. This inclusive approach will allow Sudanese women to voice their concerns, needs and experiences.
In line with the United Nations Disability Inclusion Strategy, the mission will seek to promote disability inclusion internally by creating space for employment opportunities for qualified candidates. At the political level, it will advocate for inclusion of the views of persons with disabilities in decision-making platforms, as change agents for peace, security and sustainable development.

To contribute to the objective, the resident coordinator system will scale up development support to national Governments in order to implement the 2030 Agenda, including by strengthening the design and implementation of United Nations Sustainable Development Cooperation Frameworks. Specifically, the resident coordinator system will:
(a) Ensure diverse and effective resident coordinator profiles and skill sets tailored to the needs of countries;
(b) Provide effective support, through the Development Coordination Office, at the global and regional levels, to United Nations country teams in delivering individual and collective results on the Sustainable Development Goals, with the Resident Coordinator serving as the gateway to the United Nations’ collective offer for Member States;
(c) Act as the secretariat for the United Nations Sustainable Development Group;
(d) Ensure accountable and transparent results of the resident coordinator system and United Nations development system in support of the 2030 Agenda at the global, regional and national levels;
(e) Support high-quality Sustainable Development Goal analysis and tailored responses by United Nations entities and governments at the country level, by leveraging data-driven solutions, information technology and digital innovation;
(f) Facilitate the achievement of system-wide efficiencies of the United Nations Sustainable Development Group through the following workstreams: business operations strategies, common back offices, global shared services and common premises;
(g) Continue to leverage catalytic funding provided by the Joint Sustainable Development Goals Fund to help countries to accelerate the achievement of the Goals by propelling key transitions in areas such as digitalization, food systems, energy, and social protection and jobs, with an emphasis on the localization of and synergies to be achieved across multiple Goals and fostering systems-level change for the 2030 Agenda;
(h) In line with intergovernmental mandates, support Member States in addressing and proactively managing risks and taking steps to address and prevent crises. This approach will be anchored firmly within the United Nations Sustainable Development Cooperation Frameworks.
The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) A stronger convening role of United Nations development stakeholders to offer countries in-depth policy solutions to advance the achievement of the Sustainable Development Goals;
(b) A coherent and transparent approach to United Nations sustainable development operations at the national, regional and global levels, including consistent implementation of system-wide priorities, policies and approaches across United Nations Sustainable Development Group entities;
(c) Strengthened partnerships to support Member States in their efforts to accelerate progress towards attaining the Sustainable Development Goals, including by attracting and leveraging public and private financing for the Goals;
(d) Increased financial resources and effectiveness for United Nations programme implementation by reducing costs through the implementation of efficiency measures and by enhancing the agility of United Nations development activities;
(e) The safeguarding of lives and livelihoods by continuing to address the socioeconomic and long-term impacts of multiple and interlocking crises whilst protecting and advancing progress on sustainable development.
With regard to the external factors, the overall plan for 2027 is based on the following planning assumptions:
(a) Adequate funding for the resident coordinator system, as well as for the Joint Sustainable Development Goals Fund and other key pooled funds;
(b) Continued and renewed engagement by Member States on the funding compact and the implementation of related commitments to ensure that the quality and quantity of funding allows the United Nations development system to maximize its comparative advantages and further incentivize United Nations coherence.
With regard to inter-agency coordination and liaison, the resident coordinator system will continue to actively coordinate member United Nations entities at the principal, director and technical levels. The resident coordinator system will also work closely with United Nations development entities and Secretariat entities, including peacekeeping and special political missions. The resident coordinators will continue to lead United Nations country teams in supporting national plans and priorities.
With regard to cooperation with other entities at the global, regional, national and local levels, the resident coordinator system will continue to work closely with Member States, international financial institutions and civil society partners to advance the Sustainable Development Goals and the 2030 Agenda.
The resident coordinator system integrates a gender perspective into its operational activities, deliverables and results, as appropriate. The resident coordinator system will continue to enhance implementation of and reporting on the United Nations accountability framework on gender equality. For example, through the United Nations System-wide Gender Equality Acceleration Plan, the system will ensure the application of the United Nations country team gender equality marker to track planned investment by the country teams for gender equality and the empowerment of women and to advance gender-responsive realization of the Sustainable Development Goals.
In line with the United Nations Disability Inclusion Strategy, the resident coordinator system will focus on the implementation of the Strategy and support United Nations country teams in implementing and reporting on the Strategy; in parallel, the system will continue supporting programme country Governments to implement the Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities.

To contribute to the objective, the mission will:
Raise awareness of the causes and dynamics of genocide through predictive and action-oriented analysis, enhanced by the collection and sharing of information from field missions and regional offices in accordance with its early warning mandate;
Alert relevant actors with respect to the risk of genocide, through the implementation of the United Nations Strategy and Plan of Action on Hate Speech, capacity-building and advocacy;
Advocate and mobilize for appropriate action to prevent the risk of genocide through engagement within the United Nations and with external partners;
Strengthen community-level efforts for prevention, including by assisting locally led initiatives aimed at bringing communities together;
Harness technology to enhance prevention and counter its negative impacts, as outlined in Human Rights Council resolution 49/9;
Increase female participation in decision-making roles and support implementation of the plan of action for women in communities to counter hate speech and prevent incitement to violence that could lead to genocide and related crimes;
Prioritize action against Holocaust and genocide denial and the glorification of war crimes and criminals;
Support the universal ratification of the Convention on the Prevention and Punishment of the Crime of Genocide and support efforts towards its implementation at the national level through legislation and implementation mechanisms.
The above-mentioned work is expected to result in increased capacity and action by United Nations intergovernmental bodies, Member States and regional and subregional organizations to prevent and mitigate the risk of genocide, war crimes, ethnic cleansing and crimes against humanity, including their incitement.
With regard to the external factors, the overall plan for 2027 is based on the following planning assumptions:
Continued commitment of Member States, the Security Council and other intergovernmental bodies to engage with the mission to advance the prevention of genocide, war crimes, ethnic cleansing and crimes against humanity, including through conceptual development and consensus-building on the nature of the responsibility to protect;
Active engagement of Member States and regional and other organizations to develop preventive capacity and take appropriate action for the prevention of genocide, war crimes, ethnic cleansing and crimes against humanity, including their incitement.
With regard to cooperation with other entities, the mission will continue to work and partner with regional organizations, including the African Union, the International Conference on the Great Lakes Region, the Economic Community of West African States, the Association of Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN), the Organization of American States, the Organization for Security and Cooperation in Europe (OSCE) and the European Union, among others, as well as with civil society, including religious leaders, traditional leaders, academia and traditional media, on developing capacity and taking appropriate action for the prevention of genocide, war crimes, ethnic cleansing and crimes against humanity, including their incitement, at the national, regional and local levels.
With regard to inter-agency coordination and liaison, the mission works closely with and supports United Nations country teams on the implementation of the United Nations Strategy and Plan of Action on Hate Speech.
The mission integrates a gender perspective into its operational activities, deliverables and results, as appropriate. This includes reflecting the gender dynamics in its monitoring and analysis and striving for the equal participation of women in its capacity-building events and workshops.
In line with the United Nations Disability Inclusion Strategy, the mission has developed and implements a guidance note on integrating disability inclusion into its programmes and activities to ensure that its work systematically incorporates the rights and needs of persons with disabilities.

246. To contribute to the objective, the mission will:
(a) Facilitate assistance to Member States, upon their request, which includes support for national reviews of the status of implementation of resolution 1540 (2004), the development and updating of national implementation action plans, and relevant capacity-building and awareness-raising activities to enhance the implementation of resolution 1540 (2004);
(b) Promote regional cooperation in the implementation of resolution 1540 (2004) by conducting regional, subregional and cross-regional activities, with priority given to training for points of contact;
(c) Prepare for and conduct the 2027 comprehensive review, including activities to take stock of progress since the 2022 review, identify gaps and evaluate needs, and support implementation of the outcome of the 2027 review, including through the effective mobilization of necessary resources.
247. The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) Enhanced national capacity to implement resolution 1540 (2004);
(b) An increased number of reports, updates of reports, and national implementation action plans submitted to the 1540 Committee;
(c) Concrete progress made on the implementation of adopted national action plans;
(d) Increased awareness of resolution 1540 (2004) and the importance of its implementation to address the risks posed by non-State actors acquiring access to weapons of mass destruction and their means of delivery;
(e) Increased cooperation among regional partners, including through peer-review, in implementing resolution 1540 (2004);
(f) Successful conduct of the 2027 comprehensive review of the status of implementation of resolution 1540 (2004).
248. With regard to cooperation with other entities, the mission cooperates with Governments, international, regional and subregional organizations, civil society organizations and other relevant partners to raise awareness of the obligations under resolution 1540 (2004). It also facilitates assistance to Member States, upon request, to build capacity, including through support in drafting legislation, in order to enhance implementation of the resolution.
249. With regard to inter-agency coordination and liaison, the mission works closely with other relevant United Nations entities whose mandates address the risks of the proliferation of weapons of mass destruction to non-State actors. The inter-entity process organized by the mission aims to enhance programme implementation and coordination by facilitating collaboration and the efficient use of resources.
250. The mission also cooperates with the Security Council Committee established pursuant to resolution 1373 (2001) concerning counter-terrorism and its Executive Directorate, as well as the Security Council Committee pursuant to resolutions 1267 (1999), 1989 (2011) and 2253 (2015) concerning Islamic State in Iraq and the Levant (Da’esh), Al-Qaida and associated individuals, groups, undertakings and entities, and the Analytical Support and Sanctions Monitoring Team supporting that Committee, including through support for regular joint briefings to the Security Council by the three Committees.
251. With regard to the external factors, the overall plan for 2027 is based on the following planning assumptions:
(a) The proliferation of weapons of mass destruction and their means of delivery to non-State actors remains a threat to international peace and security;
(b) Members of the 1540 Committee continue to be able to reach agreement on the implementation of planned activities;
(c) Member States continue to demonstrate political commitment to implementing resolution 1540, while expressing an ongoing need for assistance;
(d) Assistance providers and the donor community continue to offer support to implement resolution 1540 (2004);
252. The mission integrates a gender perspective into its operational activities, deliverables and results, as appropriate, and is guided by the gender policy of the Office for Disarmament Affairs.
253. The mission, in support of the Committee, promotes diversity and inclusion and ensures inclusive programming throughout its work, including the inclusion of disability perspectives and other reinforcing factors, such as gender and age. The mission promotes an inclusive work environment that enables all stakeholders to participate fully and effectively in disarmament forums and processes.

To contribute to the objective, the Senior Representative will:
(a) Promote, support and facilitate efforts regarding the repatriation or return of missing Kuwaiti and third-country nationals, or their remains, and the return of Kuwaiti property, including the national archives;
(b) Exercise the good offices of the Secretary-General to facilitate dialogue, build trust and overcome political or technical obstacles to the implementation of the mandate;
(c) Advocate for sustained political commitment from national authorities and international partners and promote coordination and information-sharing between Iraqi and Kuwaiti institutions;
(d) Encourage the use of modern technologies and methodologies, such as DNA analysis, satellite imagery and archival research;
(e) Encourage the strengthening of technical capacity through training and knowledge transfer on modern technologies.
The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) Expedited repatriation of the remains of Kuwaiti and third-country nationals and the accelerated return of Kuwaiti property, including the national archives;
(b) Building of mutual trust and enhanced cooperation between Iraq and Kuwait to foster confidence and sustain engagement and the political will to make progress in the search for missing Kuwaiti and third-country nationals and the return of missing Kuwaiti property, including the national archives;
(c) Increased efficacy of search and identification efforts.
With regard to cooperation and coordination with other entities at the global, regional, national and local levels, the Senior Representative will liaise closely with all international and regional stakeholders involved in support of the implementation of the mandate, in particular the Iraqi authorities, including the Ministry of Defence, Kuwaiti authorities the members of the Tripartite Commission, namely, France, Saudi Arabia, the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland and the United States of America, and ICRC.
With regard to inter-agency coordination and liaison, the Senior Representative will continue to operate in a collaborative way to ensure coherent and effective support for the implementation of Security Council resolution 2792 (2025). The Senior Representative is supported by the Department of Political and Peacebuilding Affairs and will work closely with other United Nations entities, as appropriate.
With regard to the external factors, the overall plan for 2027 is based on the following planning assumptions:
(a) Iraqi and Kuwaiti authorities will continue to maintain constructive engagement with each other and with the Senior Representative;
(b) Adequate financial resources, technical expertise and access to modern technologies will be secured to support search, identification and repatriation efforts;
(c) Security conditions at burial sites of interest will remain sufficiently stable to allow safe access for field missions and technical teams.
The mission will integrate a gender perspective into its operational activities, deliverables and results, as appropriate in line with Security Council resolution 2792 (2025). The mission will encourage Iraqi and Kuwaiti counterparts to apply gender-responsive approaches in the search for missing persons, including in forensic processes and data collection.
In line with the United Nations Disability Inclusion Strategy, the mission will seek to promote disability inclusion internally by creating space for employment opportunities for qualified candidates. At the political level, it will encourage, as appropriate, national authorities and relevant stakeholders to apply disability inclusive approaches in forensic and technological processes related to the search for missing persons and property.

To contribute to the objective, the Office will:
(a) Conduct relevant, timely, credible and high-quality system-wide evaluations of development system results and performance to complement the evaluation work of United Nations entities, focused on activities that cannot be adequately addressed through existing accountability mechanisms, taking into consideration a gender perspective, disability inclusion, non-discrimination, human rights and environmental dimensions, as appropriate;
(b) Manage the evaluation of pooled funds and joint initiatives (in which several United Nations entities are working towards a common objective);
(c) Promote increased collaboration on joint and system-wide evaluation, including by mapping the availability of evaluation evidence to inform the development of the Global System-Wide Evaluation Plan;
(d) Enhance collective knowledge-sharing and learning through the production of syntheses and summaries of evaluation evidence across the United Nations system, and support the use of system-wide evaluation evidence to inform programme planning and the achievement of results across the United Nations development system;
(e) Lead the implementation of the system-wide evaluation policy, providing advice and guidance for the system-wide evaluation function at the country, regional and global levels, including on the design and operationalization of evaluation quality assurance systems to support credible Cooperation Framework evaluations;
(f) Implement systems to ensure efficient and effective evaluation management and follow-up, including to support evaluation quality and adherence to United Nations Evaluation Group norms and standards;
(g) Contribute to the work of the United Nations Evaluation Group and other partners to support the development of common technical guidance and the strengthening of evaluation capacity, including in relation to the generation and use of evidence to inform national progress towards the achievement of the Sustainable Development Goals, in line with the role of evaluation envisaged in the 2030 Agenda.
The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) Increased learning and accountability of development activities at the national, regional and global levels, to contribute to consistent implementation of system-wide priorities, policies and approaches across United Nations sustainable development system entities;
(b) Strengthened capacities and use of system-wide evaluation evidence to inform programme planning and the achievement of results across United Nations development system entities;
(c) Complementarity and subsidiarity of system-wide evaluation with the work of United Nations evaluation, oversight and accountability mechanisms.
With regard to the external factors, the overall plan for 2027 is based on the assumption that adequate voluntary contributions are available to complement regular budget resources to carry out the activities of the Office.
With regard to cooperation at the global, regional national and local levels, the Office will collaborate with other international evaluation entities. The Office will participate as a member of the United Nations Evaluation Group and cooperate with the United Nations System Staff College to contribute to the development of common technical guidance and to evaluation capacity strengthening.
With regard to inter-agency coordination and liaison, the Office will coordinate its work with the Office of Internal Oversight Services, the Joint Inspection Unit and the Board of Auditors; and collaborate with the evaluation offices of United Nations entities to enhance coherence and leverage their work to maximise existing capacities and minimise costs in the conduct of system-wide evaluation activities.
The Office integrates a gender perspective into its operational activities, deliverables and results, as appropriate, including implementing guidance on mainstreaming a gender perspective into activities.
In line with the United Nations Disability Inclusion Strategy, the Office will integrate disability inclusion across all stages of its evaluation activities, in accordance with guidance of the United Nations Evaluation Group.

17.21 To contribute to the objective, the subprogramme will:
(a) Support the normative processes of intergovernmental bodies, including the Commission on the Status of Women, by facilitating the exchange of good practices and lessons learned among Governments and other relevant stakeholders, including through meetings, the issuance of reports and the presentation of analyses and policy options that enable discussion on key gender equality issues;
(b) Lead, coordinate and promote the accountability of the United Nations system for gender equality and the empowerment of women and gender mainstreaming. This includes providing expertise, advocacy, monitoring, the development of relevant tools and guidance and capacity-building within the United Nations system. Actions to continue to implement the system-wide strategy on gender parity towards system-wide improvement of the status of women will also be undertaken;
(c) Develop strategic partnerships by enabling civil society actors and other stakeholders to contribute to relevant intergovernmental processes, including through the convening of stakeholders to share lessons learned and recommendations for actions and the provision of funding for attending capacity- and alliance-building opportunities.
17.22 In doing so, the subprogramme will assist Member States in their efforts to accelerate the implementation of the Beijing Declaration and Platform for Action, achieve Sustainable Development Goal 5 and systematically mainstream a gender perspective into the implementation of the 2030 Agenda.
17.23 The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) A strengthened global normative framework and increased reflection of a gender perspective in the outcomes of intergovernmental processes;
(b) An enabling working environment to accelerate gender mainstreaming, improvement in the status of women and progress towards gender parity goals in the United Nations system, leading to greater achievement of results towards gender equality;
(c) Enhanced cooperation among stakeholders and Governments on gender equality and the empowerment of women, as well as enhanced opportunities for stakeholders to present lessons learned and good practices in conjunction with intergovernmental processes, and the availability of a growing resource base.

17.36 To contribute to the objective, the subprogramme will serve as a global knowledge centre:
(a) By undertaking and disseminating research, analysis and evaluation of trends on gender equality and the empowerment of women and girls in the areas of women’s leadership and decision-making, the economic empowerment of women, ending violence against women, women and peace and security and humanitarian action, including on new and emerging issues and their impact on the situation of women;
(b) By providing advisory, technical and advocacy support, training and capacity development on gender equality, including through field offices, to Member States, upon request, in line with their national priorities;
(c) By offering guidance to the United Nations system, including the United Nations country teams in their work on gender equality and the empowerment of women.
17.37 In doing so, the subprogramme will assist Member States in their efforts to achieve Sustainable Development Goal 5 and to systematically mainstream a gender perspective into the implementation of the 2030 Agenda.
17.38 The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) Policies and actions taken by national and regional bodies to implement United Nations instruments, standards and resolutions, in line with the Beijing Declaration and Platform for Action, the outcome of the twenty-third special session of the General Assembly and the Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination against Women;
(b) Increased capacity of Member States to implement, monitor and evaluate laws, policies and strategies for gender equality and the empowerment of women and girls and the realization of their human rights;
(c) Improved ability of United Nations programmes to more effectively support Member States, at their request, in the implementation of commitments to eliminate discrimination against women and girls.

15.33 To contribute to the objective, the subprogramme will support Member States in developing solutions to expand access to adequate housing, land and basic services (water, sanitation, mobility, waste management and energy) and to promote climate change adaptation and resilience, urban low-emission development strategies, accessibility and sustainability across the rural-urban continuum, with an emphasis on transforming informal settlements, slums and underserved areas, as well as in crisis-affected contexts. Specifically, the subprogramme will:
(a) Provide technical assistance to Member States in the aforementioned areas;
(b) Provide policy advisory services and advocacy in the aforementioned areas;
(c) Develop standards, tools and guidelines for local and national governments;
(d) Conduct research to inform decision-making on affordable housing and equitable access to basic services;
(e) Provide capacity development and institutional support, including the dissemination of learning and best practices.
15.34 In doing so, the subprogramme will assist Member States in making progress towards achieving the Sustainable Development Goals, particularly Goals 1, 3, 5–8, 10–11, 13 and 16.
15.35 The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) Increased access to adequate housing for all, benefiting people in cities and human settlements, particularly those in the poorest and those in vulnerable situations, who are often disproportionally affected by global challenges, such as housing shortages and climate change;
(b) Improved and more equitable access to basic services, with a focus on low-carbon and climate-resilient urban basic services, particularly for people living in informal settlements and slums, as well as those living in crisis-affected contexts;
(c) Integrated land-use planning and management that supports the social and ecological function of land, with a focus on increasing climate-resilient urban development, especially for the urban poor;
(d) More efficient and equitable use of land, including strengthened security of tenure, benefiting communities and households, with a special emphasis on those in vulnerable situations.

15.47 To contribute to the objective, the subprogramme will:
(a) Provide technical assistance and capacity development to Member States in integrated and gender-sensitive spatial planning, municipal finance, urban policy formulation, legal reforms, Sustainable Development Goals localization and participatory multilevel governance;
(b) Facilitate knowledge exchange, peer learning and multi-stakeholder dialogue platforms that connect national, subnational and local governments with civil society, the private sector and development partners;
(c) Support the development and implementation of gender-sensitive, normative frameworks, tools and good practice guidance on urban planning systems, innovative financing mechanisms, policy and legislative reforms and participatory multilevel governance;
(d) Strengthen monitoring, evaluation and evidence generation on urban development trends, financing flows, policy and legislation effectiveness and multilevel governance outcomes to inform decision-making.
15.48 In doing so, the subprogramme will assist Member States in making progress towards achieving the Sustainable Development Goals, particularly Goals 5, 10–11 and 16–17.
15.49 The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) National and local institutions developing and implementing integrated and gender-sensitive urban planning systems, mobilizing domestic resources, managing public expenditure and coordinating multilevel governance for sustainable urban development;
(b) Adoption and implementation of gender-sensitive urban policies, legal frameworks and financing mechanisms aligned with the New Urban Agenda and national development priorities;
(c) Increased engagement and ownership by local governments, communities, women, young people and marginalized groups in participatory urban planning, budgeting and decision-making processes;
(d) Strengthened alignment between national Sustainable Development Goal targets and local action, with improved tracking and reporting on urban-related indicators at subnational levels.

15.59 To contribute to the objective, the subprogramme will:
(a) Generate, synthesize and disseminate policy-relevant knowledge, analytical products and good practices on housing, land and basic services, including through research, global assessments, practical learning tools and policy papers, to support evidence-based decision-making by Member States and stakeholders;
(b) Strengthen the collection, harmonization, analysis and use of data and indicators on housing, land and basic services at the global, national and subnational levels, including through the development of monitoring frameworks and tools aligned with international standards, the Sustainable Development Goals and the New Urban Agenda;
(c) Promote the use of digital solutions and frontier technologies to improve data availability, accessibility and transparency and support innovative approaches, platforms and strategies for planning, monitoring and delivery of housing, land and basic services;
(d) Build institutional and technical capacities of Member States and other stakeholders for the formulation, implementation and monitoring of inclusive housing, land and basic services policies and programmes, including through higher and professional education, training and digital learning, as well as peer learning and co-creation.
15.60 In doing so, the subprogramme will assist Member States in making progress towards achieving the Sustainable Development Goals, particularly Goals 1, 4, 6, 9,11 and 17.
15.61 The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) Increased access to quality knowledge and data to inform housing, land and basic services policies and practice at the global, national and subnational levels;
(b) Increased adoption of digital and innovative approaches to policymaking, implementation and monitoring related to housing, land and basic services;
(c) Strengthened expertise and institutional capacities of Member States, at all levels, and of stakeholders to design, implement and monitor inclusive and equitable policies for housing, land and basic services.

15.71 To contribute to the objective, the subprogramme will:
(a) Build and nurture inclusive partnerships and muti-stakeholder coalitions to support coordinated approaches on housing, land and basic services;
(b) Convene global, regional and national advocacy platforms and facilitate stakeholder engagement, including through the World Urban Forum, World Habitat Day, World Cities Day (in the framework of the Urban October);
(c) Engage in advocacy and strategic communication, outreach and knowledge-sharing to raise awareness and engagement in policy action for adequate housing, land and basic services for all.
15.72 In doing so, the subprogramme will assist Member States in making progress towards achieving the Sustainable Development Goals, particularly Goals 1, 6, 10, 11 and 17.
15.73 The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) Strategic coalitions and cooperation supporting the implementation of solutions for housing, land and basic services at the global, national and local levels;
(b) Improved visibility and accessibility of solutions for housing, land and basic services.

10.22 To contribute to the objective, the subprogramme will:
(a) Provide support to Member States in their intergovernmental processes relating to the priorities of the least developed countries, including by supporting the implementation of the Doha Programme of Action for the Least Developed Countries, including its deliverables, namely, an online university to support graduate and postgraduate university-level education in science, technology, engineering and mathematics, an investment support centre, a resilience-building mechanism, a Sustainable Graduation Support Facility and a system of food stockholding for the least developed countries on a regional and subregional basis, or alternative means, such as cash transfers, taking into account possible economic implications and risks;
(b) Prepare research and analytical materials relating to the six key focus areas of the Doha Programme of Action for the Least Developed Countries and facilitate knowledge exchange and peer learning on critical enablers to poverty eradication and structural transformation, including by mainstreaming a gender perspective;
(c) Promote resource mobilization, especially public and private financing, including innovative finance, for resilience-building, and monitor the road map for the accelerated implementation of the Doha Programme of Action for the Least Developed Countries, in partnership with the United Nations system, parliaments, civil society, the private sector, the media, academia and foundations in focus areas of the Doha Programme of Action, in preparation for the comprehensive high-level midterm review of the Doha Programme of Action;
(d) Lead the inter-agency task forces on: (i) least developed country graduation, including by organizing workshops and technical support on establishing consultative mechanisms with development partners and preparing smooth transition strategies, and by facilitating peer learning between graduated and graduating countries; and (ii) developing operational guidelines for the United Nations system’s support for least developed countries in conflict and post-conflict situations;
(e) Monitor sectoral policy developments of the intergovernmental processes of relevant international and regional organizations, including multilateral development banks;
(f) Build the capacity of national focal points to implement and monitor the Doha Programme of Action for the Least Developed Countries, through annual meetings and training programmes, to share experiences and best practices, including through the application of guidelines for mainstreaming and monitoring the implementation of the Doha Programme of Action, and with a more structured collaboration with resident coordinators, in preparation for the comprehensive high-level midterm review of the Doha Programme of Action;
(g) Partner with the Technology Bank for the Least Developed Countries to strengthen science, technology and innovation capacity, including in graduated and graduating countries.
10.23 The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) Least developed countries accelerating progress towards achieving the Sustainable Development Goals and achieving the focus areas of the Doha Programme of Action for the Least Developed Countries and other internationally agreed development goals;
(b) Graduating countries making a smooth transition out of the least developed country category;
(c) Stronger alignment of intergovernmental discussions with the priorities of least developed countries, reflecting a deeper understanding of their realities;
(d) Greater accountability of least developed countries, development partners, international organizations, civil society and the private sector in the implementation of the commitments of the Doha Programme of Action for the Least Developed Countries.

10.35 To contribute to the objective, the subprogramme will:
(a) Provide support to Member States in their intergovernmental processes relating to the priorities of landlocked developing countries, including by supporting the implementation of the Awaza Programme of Action for Landlocked Developing Countries;
(b) Undertake research and analytical assessments to assist landlocked developing countries in enacting policies conducive to their developmental priorities in line of those contained in the Awaza Programme of Action for Landlocked Developing Countries;
(c) Mobilize international support for the implementation of the Awaza Programme of Action for Landlocked Developing Countries and other relevant agendas;
(d) Coordinate, monitor and assess progress made in relation to the Awaza Programme of Action for Landlocked Developing Countries, including through the Inter-Agency Consultative Group for Landlocked Developing Countries;
(e) Build the capacity of the national focal points of landlocked developing countries, in collaboration with the resident coordinators, to facilitate the mainstreaming of the Awaza Programme of Action for Landlocked Developing Countries into their national development plans.
10.36 The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) Increased support towards landlocked developing countries from the international community to address the needs and challenges arising from their lack of direct access to the sea and transit constraints;
(b) Increased adoption and implementation by landlocked developing countries of initiatives to enhance transit, connectivity, trade facilitation and climate change adaptation and mitigation;
(c) Landlocked developing countries devising, formulating and implementing policies that promote economic diversification, integration into global and regional value chains, and structural economic transformation;
(d) Strengthened social protection for all groups of population in landlocked developing countries, including through access to decent jobs.

10.48 To contribute to the objective, the subprogramme will:
(a) Mobilize support for the special case of small island developing States in achieving sustainable development by engaging with development partners, international financial institutions and the private sector;
(b) Support resource mobilization efforts for small island developing States in priority areas, including through a targeted resource mobilization strategy and by developing tailored policy tools;
(c) Provide structured support through the Small Island Developing States Partnership Framework for the facilitation of collaborative partnerships, especially between small island developing States and the private sector;
(d) Strengthen coherence and coordination at the national, regional and global levels, including by providing more structured support to the small island developing States national focal points network and increasing coordination between United Nations entities supporting those States;
(e) Develop and strengthen the implementation frameworks for the Antigua and Barbuda Agenda for Small Island Developing States.
10.49 The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) Strengthened international support measures aimed at enabling small island developing States to implement the 2030 Agenda, the Antigua and Barbuda Agenda for Small Island Developing States, the Paris Agreement and other priorities of those States for sustainable development;
(b) Enhanced access to finance for small island developing States;
(c) Small island developing States formulating and implementing policies that promote sustainable development and make progress towards the achievement of internationally agreed development goals.

10.61 To contribute to the objective, the subprogramme will:
(a) Generate data analysis and insights and publish analytical research for the least developed countries, landlocked developing countries and small island developing States, including cross-cutting flagship reports, analytical briefs and trend analysis, on topics that align with and support the advancement of three programmes of action and global development agendas;
(b) Provide, in preparing for intergovernmental processes, substantive support on the regional and country specificities of research and trend analysis for ongoing and emerging issues affecting the three groups of countries;
(c) Build research partnerships and networks, in particular in emerging areas and for enhanced data collection and analysis among and within the three groups of countries;
(d) Develop outreach campaigns, using data-driven storytelling and multimedia products, on the specific challenges and opportunities of the three groups of countries and the priorities of the three programmes of action;
(e) Advocate for sustained and adequate international support for the three programmes of action and the integration of these priorities into global agendas by organizing high-level events, convening multi-stakeholder dialogues and engaging in targeted advocacy with decision makers in intergovernmental forums.
10.62 The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) Strengthened implementation of evidence-based policies and decision-making by the least developed countries, landlocked developing countries and small island developing States;
(b) Increased understanding and engagement of target audiences, in particular decision makers, in support of and for the advancement of the priorities of the three programmes of action.

To contribute to the objective, the Mission will:
Coordinate with international donors, organizations and partners in addressing the humanitarian and basic human needs of vulnerable Afghans. The Mission will partner with the United Nations country team to support economic development and increase donor confidence for greater basic human needs support, as emphasized in the independent assessment on Afghanistan requested by the Security Council in its resolution 2679 (2023), and to implement the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development, with a focus on the objectives of the Pact for the Future, particularly with regard to climate financing, poverty alleviation and upholding the principles and values of the Charter of the United Nations. To that end, it will support resource mobilization to enable humanitarian efforts, essential services, the restoration of livelihoods, support to the private sector, economic development and mitigation of the dangers of explosive remnants of war. The Mission will engage with the de facto authorities and other stakeholders to facilitate the support of the United Nations, international financial institutions and other international and national partners to the socioeconomic recovery of Afghanistan;
Provide outreach and good offices, including by facilitating dialogue between Afghan political actors and stakeholders, regional stakeholders and the wider international community. The Mission will use its convening platforms at the national and subnational levels to facilitate engagement and cooperation between the de facto authorities, civil society, young people, communities, women-led organizations and other stakeholders on governance and policy issues, such as inclusion, participation and responsiveness to constituency concerns. It will maintain support for promoting the participation of women and minorities in all spheres. It will also continue to identify further entry points for engagement with the de facto authorities for the benefit of all Afghans, including through the United Nations-facilitated Comprehensive Approach under the Doha Process comprising the contours of a political roadmap (“Mosaic”) and thematic working groups. In addition, the United Nations and international partners will monitor, report on and conduct analysis relating to the political economy. The Mission will provide technical and analytical support to United Nations agencies and humanitarian partners and will continue to engage with civil society, the media, communities and counterpart institutions of the de facto authorities, in compliance with the sanctions set out in relevant Security Council resolutions related to anti-money-laundering and combating the counter-terrorism financing;
Advise stakeholders on and promote respect for the human rights of all in Afghanistan and accountability for human rights violation, including women and girls, children, detainees and the prevention of their torture and ill-treatment, human rights defenders and civic space; and other vulnerable groups. In addition, UNAMA will continue to integrate human rights and gender mainstreaming as cross-cutting issues in mandate implementation. The Mission will promote and support gender equality, the empowerment of women and girls, and the participation and leadership of women in public life and at all levels and stages of decision-making. It will also partner with the United Nations country team to promote the protection and fulfilment of the rights of women and girls and the implementation of Security Council resolution 1325 (2000) on women and peace and security, and successive resolutions on the matter;
Support peace and stability in Afghanistan through consultations and by utilizing its convening power for dialogues with political stakeholders. The Mission will use existing mechanisms to advance regional cooperation on economic development and security to help to foster a regional environment conducive to sustainable peace and stability. In addition, it will propose and support regional approaches to issues, such as the return and reintegration of refugees and internally displaced persons, combating illicit drugs and trafficking, addressing the illicit accumulation and trafficking of small arms and light weapons in Afghanistan and the region, and reducing the illegal economy and its impact. It will also coordinate explosive ordnance threat mitigation measures to enable the safe delivery of humanitarian and basic human needs initiatives;
Intensify its work to implement the United Nations Strategic Framework for Afghanistan, 2023–2025, which has been extended to 2027, by engaging with the de facto authorities, civil society and the media to facilitate the transition of society from conflict to peace and the removal of explosive remnants of war. The Mission will contribute to the goal of reduced dependency on aid, increasing United Nations assistance in critical sectors, such as banking, trade and economic reform. The Deputy Special Representative/Resident Coordinator/Humanitarian Coordinator will ensure a coherent approach to United Nations humanitarian and basic needs programming for the benefit of the Afghan people. The Mission will streamline the delivery, oversight and reporting of aid provided in Afghanistan, in alignment with the Strategic Framework and in coordination with the de facto authorities, civil society and international partners. It will also enhance collaboration on aid architecture and risk management practices.
The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
The advancement of dialogue involving the de facto authorities and other national stakeholders within and outside Afghanistan, as well as the international community, to improve the situation of Afghans, including through efforts aimed at improving the inclusiveness and responsiveness of governing entities, and to support the creation of conditions that could eventually lead to the reintegration of Afghanistan into the international community, in accordance with relevant Security Council resolutions;
Increased compliance by all parties with international humanitarian law and human rights obligations, with improved accountability for human rights violations and abuses, contributing to building confidence in such mechanisms and between the population and the de facto authorities;
Ongoing accurate reporting to the international community on the evolving situation in Afghanistan and its implications for international peace and security;
Sustained local, regional and international support to meet humanitarian and basic human needs and promote economic development, and to create an environment conducive to socioeconomic recovery in Afghanistan.
With regard to cooperation with other entities, the Mission supports constructive dialogue between the relevant Afghan stakeholders, regional actors and the international community that is focused on the rights and socioeconomic progress of the Afghan people.
With regard to inter-agency coordination and liaison, UNAMA engages with the United Nations country team to strengthen the repositioning of the United Nations development system and to deliver more effectively and efficiently in line with the 2030 Agenda, the Pact for the Future, the Sustainable Development Goals and the humanitarian-development-peace nexus. The Mission and the United Nations country team collaborate closely on issues related to governance, the rule of law, human rights and gender, focusing on policy issues and good offices. Given the context in Afghanistan, the Mission’s work also supports the delivery of humanitarian response and meeting basic human needs through the delivery of essential services, livelihoods and community systems, in line with Inter-Agency Standing Committee recommendations. The Mission supports the removal of explosive ordnance, the delivery of risk education and the provision of victim assistance to enable humanitarian and basic human needs activities. It collaborates with various stakeholders to explore new aid approaches targeting economic promotion and recovery. It also leads the coordination of an overall risk management approach of the United Nations in Afghanistan, including mitigating the risk of aid diversion. It engages with the United Nations country team to enhance operational effectiveness through shared services and premises, generating $3.5 million efficiency gains for the Mission under the United Nations Business Operations Strategy, reducing operational footprints and redirecting resources to programme delivery. In addition, the Mission coordinates on specific issues with the United Nations Regional Centre for Preventive Diplomacy for Central Asia to provide good offices and expert advice on confidence-building measures in the region that contribute to stability in Afghanistan.
With regard to external factors, the overall plan for 2027 is based on the following planning assumptions:
The de facto authorities will remain in control of the territory of Afghanistan. However, political, security, human rights, legal, socioeconomic and humanitarian uncertainties will continue. The likely continuation and furthering of restrictive policies will present challenges for the full reintegration of Afghanistan into the international community;
The lack of international recognition, and, to an extent, domestic legitimacy of the de facto authorities due to questions about lack of representation and inclusion, the dissonance between their ideology and international norms and standards, the continued absence of a constitution, the promulgation of repressive decrees, especially those aimed at women and minority groups, and the continued restriction preventing United Nations national female personnel from accessing United Nations compounds, will pose significant challenges in engaging with domestic, regional and international stakeholders on the future of Afghanistan;
The security situation will remain uncertain, with the likelihood of continued attacks by Islamic State in Iraq and the Levant-Khorasan against the de facto authorities and the international presence, activities of anti-Taliban armed opposition groups and dynamics related to the presence of regional and international foreign terrorist fighters and their access to illicitly accumulated small arms and light weapons, with an impact on the Mission, as well as on development and humanitarian actors, in terms of access and physical security. The de facto authorities’ security emergency response and information sharing remain inconsistent and security support systems facilitating UNAMA operations remain significantly dependent on United Nations assistance;
Relations between Afghanistan and some of its neighbours will remain tense due to unresolved security issues, threats of terrorism, migration and water management issues;
Civilians, including those working as humanitarians and supporting basic human needs, will continue to face the threats of explosive ordnance, including those from improvised explosive devices and explosive remnants of war;
Enduring threats, such as terrorism, organized crime and illicit trafficking, including in narcotics, small arms and light weapons, and resource competition, will continue, with threats emerging from issues such as land rights and the disenfranchisement of minorities, population outflow, returnees, unemployment and the impact of evolving regional dynamics, as well as vulnerability to the adverse impact of climate change, ecological changes and natural disasters, which may heighten the risk of local conflicts over land, water and other resources;
The economy and human development will remain adversely affected by the continuing impact of non-recognition and donor sensitivities, the severe restrictions by the de facto authorities on female national staff working for the United Nations and non-governmental organizations (NGOs), and the limitations of the de facto authorities to reinvigorate the economy and reduce poverty, with the resumption of development funding depending on the de facto authorities meeting international norms, especially in relation to the protection of human rights, including for women and girls;
In a context where funding is diminishing and mobilizing additional resources is increasingly challenging, the United Nations is expected to demonstrate increased complementarities and efficiencies in humanitarian assistance and recovery support. This includes focusing on meeting basic human needs by maintaining essential services and preserving community systems;
As viable alternatives for large-scale legitimate financial transactions remain unavailable, the United Nations will continue to rely on and incur the costs associated with the cash importation facility of the Secretariat, managed at Headquarters.
The Mission integrates a gender perspective in its operational activities, deliverables and results. For example, in its engagement with the de facto authorities and political stakeholders, UNAMA continues its advocacy for the full, equal and meaningful participation of women in political processes at all levels. The Mission continues to support the implementation of Security Council resolutions 1325 (2000) and 2493 (2019), particularly on gender equality, the empowerment of women and girls and their human rights.
In line with the United Nations Disability Inclusion Strategy, UNAMA will implement its revised strategic plan, with a focus on: (a) increasing engagement with persons with disabilities through outreach projects; (b) advancing disability inclusion in strategic planning and management, and strengthening institutional capacity; (c) further improving physical and digital accessibility to provide an enabling work environment for an inclusive and diverse workforce; and (d) continuous staff capacity-building and learning on disability inclusion.

To contribute to the objective, the Alliance will:
(a) Promote interreligious and intercultural dialogue, including dialogue among civilizations;
(b) Design and implement peace education initiatives aimed at equipping young people with the critical thinking skills and competencies needed to play an active and positive role in issues of peace and security and in preventing violent extremism and radicalization;
(c) Provide a grant funding mechanism, capacity-building support and mentorship for young people, youth-led organisations and civil society organizations that implement projects related to intercultural and interfaith dialogue, particularly in areas affected by identity-based conflict, with a view to combating intolerance and stereotyping based on religion or belief;
(d) Facilitate dialogues to build stronger partnerships and promote the voices of young people and of civil society at large, including faith actors and grass-roots organizations, thereby fostering mutual understanding and collaboration across divides;
(e) Reinforce synergies with international organizations that work for the integration of migrant communities and promote the role of municipalities and cities in building inclusive and resilient societies;
(f) Develop initiatives that serve to train young people in multimedia storytelling and video production and facilitate collaborative media projects that unite young creators from different cultural and religious backgrounds, with an emphasis on the creation of content that changes the narrative around migrants and promotes tolerance, respect, dialogue and cooperation;
(g) Promote the role and contribution of women as peace mediators, including through the Women’s Alliance for Peace initiative and through intercultural and interreligious mediation training aimed at women leaders in conflict contexts;
(h) Identify, showcase and promote the role of religious leaders and faith-based organizations engaged in interreligious mediation;
(i) Develop, support and participate in global communication, advocacy and programming initiatives aimed at amplifying the message of inclusion and peace through sports;
(j) Collaborate with arts institutions, cultural organizations and educational institutions to integrate arts into broader advocacy efforts aimed at promoting tolerance, respect, dialogue and cooperation;
(k) Engage with United Nations entities for a comprehensive and coordinated approach to addressing and responding to Islamophobia, antisemitism and other forms of intolerance based on religion and belief;
(l) Conduct briefings for Member States to highlight the linkages between religious intolerance and human rights, and to contribute to efforts towards the effective development of system-wide responses to all forms of religious intolerance;
(m) Organize tailored institutional events focused on trends in Islamophobia to provide a platform for a multi-stakeholder approach, including Member States, relevant United Nations entities, media providers, civil society and faith actors, in combating Islamophobia;
(n) Engage in dialogue on Islamophobia with State and non-State actors, in order to address the root causes and explore possible solutions and good practices to address Islamophobia;
(o) Engage with Member States and relevant stakeholders to assist, as appropriate, in the implementation of the recommendations contained in the United Nations Plan of Action to Safeguard Religious Sites, including carrying out the “#forSafeWorship” communication campaign; raising the awareness of the international community and the general public about the universality of religious sites; and issuing press statements in condemnation of attacks on worshippers, acts of violence, and attacks on and destruction of religious places, sites and shrines, which are in violation of international law, in both conflict and non-conflict situations;
(p) Engage with relevant United Nations entities and agencies on the implementation of the recommendations contained in the United Nations action plan to enhance monitoring and response to antisemitism, including the convening of relevant stakeholders (Member States, regional and international organizations, and Jewish organizations and communities) to explore good practices to respond to antisemitism.
The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) Enhanced social stability and an environment conducive to peace and mutual understanding;
(b) Advancement of intercultural and interreligious dialogue, and a culture of peace and mutual respect;
(c) Improved action by Member States and non-State actors in addressing the root causes of all forms of religious intolerance, xenophobia and discrimination based on religion, belief or ethnicity.
With regard to the external factors, the overall plan for 2027 is based on the following planning assumptions:
(a) Governments have the political commitment and dedicate resources to accelerate action to prevent conflicts and address the root causes of all forms of religious intolerance;
(b) Civil society, faith-based organizations, local communities, the private sector and media are committed to working together to advance intercultural and interreligious dialogue;
(c) Personnel, at all levels, prioritize addressing all forms of religious intolerance within United Nations operations.
With regard to cooperation with other entities at the global, regional, national and local levels, the Alliance of Civilizations will continue to garner the support of Member States for the advancement of intercultural and interreligious dialogue, a culture of peace and mutual respect, and for the reinforcement of partnerships with regional and subregional actors for the practical implementation of its activities, including with the European Union, the League of Arab States, the Organization of Islamic Cooperation, the Organization of American States, the Council of Europe, the Black Sea Economic Cooperation Organization, the Organization for Security and Cooperation in Europe, the International Organization of Turkic Culture and the Union for the Mediterranean. In addition, the Alliance will continue to work closely with non-State actors, namely civil society, young people, local communities, faith actors, academic institutions, the media, research institutes and the private sector, to build capacity and raise awareness of the role of intercultural and interreligious dialogue in conflict prevention and addressing the root causes of all forms of religious intolerance, xenophobia and discrimination based on religion, belief or ethnicity.
With regard to inter-agency coordination and liaison, the Alliance will continue to co-chair the United Nations Inter-Agency Task Force on Religion and Sustainable Development and engage its multi-faith advisory council; act as Vice-Chair of the Working Group on Preventing and Countering Violent Extremism Conducive to Terrorism; and lead the establishment and the activities of a United Nations working group to monitor, evaluate and assess the impact of policies and measures to address antisemitism on a regular basis. In addition, the Alliance will continue to strengthen its cooperation with the Department of Global Communications, the Department of Political and Peacebuilding Affairs, the International Organization for Migration, the United Nations Population Fund, the Office of Counter-Terrorism, the United Nations Educational, Scientific and Cultural Organization, the Office of the Special Adviser to the Secretary-General on the Prevention of Genocide, OHCHR, the United Nations Youth Office and the Special Rapporteur on freedom of religion or belief through information exchange, joint events and initiatives, for more effective advocacy and coordinated actions to ensure that the prevention agenda remains prominent on the international agenda and to address all forms of religious intolerance in a coherent manner within the United Nations system.
The Alliance integrates a gender perspective into its operational activities, deliverables and results, as appropriate. In 2027, it will continue to support the Women’s Alliance for Peace and promote the inclusion of women peace mediators in conflict mediation and conflict resolution initiatives. It will also continue its advocacy to ensure that the voices of young women and men that support peace processes at the local level are more actively represented at global events organized by the Alliance and other relevant forums. Information on the beneficiaries of the Alliance’s programmes will continue to be systematically disaggregated by sex in order to present a gender-sensitive analysis of the impact of the Alliance’s programme.
In line with the United Nations Disability Inclusion Strategy, the Alliance will seek to systematically integrate accessibility and reasonable accommodation considerations into the planning and delivery of its conferences, meetings, and capacity-building activities. Accessibility requirements will be applied to conference services, facilities, and information materials, and reasonable accommodation needs will be identified and addressed as part of standard event management processes, with the aim of enabling the effective participation of persons with disabilities.

12.24 To contribute to the objective, the subprogramme will identify specific needs and measures arising from the interdependence of trade, finance, investment, technology and macroeconomic policies, from the point of view of their effects on development, through the three pillars of work of UNCTAD referred to in paragraph 12.2 above. Specifically, the subprogramme will:
(a) Support developing countries in promoting structural economic transformation through statistics, capacity-building and technical cooperation, identify policy options, design industrial and productive transformation strategies and foster regional and cross-regional partnerships for sustainable growth;
(b) Support developing countries in the realization of the 2030 Agenda by fostering a more inclusive and resilient global economy and by enhancing the efficiency of the global economic system, international trade and debt sustainability;
(c) Undertake analytical and technical work on debt issues – fostering debt-management practices, promoting responsible borrowing and lending practices, supporting exchanges among borrower countries on debt challenges, and contributing to global debt data transparency and harmonization;
(d) Support countries in identifying policy options to scale up domestic resources and strengthen international resource mobilization;
(e) Support countries in engaging in discussions on reforming the international financial architecture, multilateral development banks and credit rating agencies, by providing them with relevant analyses and information;
(f) Conduct analytical work on assessing the role of official development assistance as a key component of financing for development, including in mobilizing additional public and private finance and the role of such assistance in international net financial flows, both North-South and South-North;
(g) Undertake research and analysis on the trends of the global economy and prospects for closer cooperation and integration among developing countries to inform their development strategies, including by promoting dialogue and best practice among existing regionally integrated structures;
(h) Provide technical assistance and research and analysis to support the Palestinian people, in line with paragraphs 80.68 and 80.69 of the Geneva Consensus and responding to the request of the General Assembly in its resolutions 77/22 on the Committee on the Exercise of the Inalienable Rights of the Palestinian People and 79/141 on assistance to the Palestinian people;
(i) Conduct analytical work on climate finance and sustainable development to assist developing countries in leveraging financial resources to address vulnerabilities, with the aim of building productive capacities resilient to extreme weather events exacerbated by climate change.
12.25 The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) Increased coherence between international economic rules, practices and processes and national policies and development strategies;
(b) Improved mobilization of domestic financial resources as well as international resources, including through South-South cooperation;
(c) More effective debt management and a shared understanding at the international level of debt issues and debt sustainability, and facilitated exchanges among borrowing countries;
(d) Increased understanding by developing countries of the global economic environment and of policy choices for inclusive and sustained development;
(e) Trade and development policies that are responsive to the challenges posed by climate change and the type of support and action needed.

12.37 To contribute to the objective, the subprogramme will:
(a) Assist member States through research, policy analysis and technical assistance in designing and implementing policies to scale up mobilization of public and private finance and enhanced investment and entrepreneurship for sustainable development;
(b) Monitor, assess and analyse regional and global trends and prospects in international investment and prepare the annual World Investment Report and other analytical publications on international investment for development;
(c) Promote policy frameworks that create an enabling environment at all levels in support of developing countries to better attract, increase and facilitate investments for the achievement of sustainable development;
(d) Conduct reviews of national investment policies and backstop policy dialogue on the international investment regime and support and design new international investment agreements that are better aligned with nationally and internationally agreed development objectives;
(e) Provide policy advice to Governments on investment promotion, including on incentives in the context of the global tax reform implications for developing countries, and support with the implementation of business practices and investment facilitation for development agreements, including through digital government tools;
(f) Promote innovative financing for the Sustainable Development Goals, including through stock exchanges and institutional funds, as well as incentives in the context of the global tax reform implications for developing countries;
(g) Support enhancement of the international competitiveness of member States’ enterprises through advice on policies aimed at stimulating enterprise development, including mobilizing global efforts in financing and investment in micro-, small and medium-sized enterprises to support their sustainable development transformation, as well as by promoting best practices in corporate social responsibility, accounting and sustainability reporting;
(h) Foster dialogue and an exchange of best practices related to investment and enterprise development issues through consensus-building mechanisms and the World Investment Forum;
(i) Provide an assessment of the impact of multiple and cascading challenges on investment, global value chains and enterprise development, as well as policy advice, frameworks and tools for recovery.
12.38 The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) Increased capacity of member States to close the gaps related to Sustainable Development Goal investment, especially with regard to climate change mitigation and adaptation, in particular for countries that are most vulnerable to the adverse impacts of climate change;
(b) Improved ability of countries to leverage policies, innovations and tools, and to address key and emerging issues related to investment and investment policies that promote development, including policies on international investment agreements and on the opportunities of remittances and diaspora investment for development;
(c) Member States collecting, analysing and reporting on foreign direct investment and multinational enterprise data and formulating development-oriented investment policies;
(d) Additional resources leveraged and channelled towards global investment development objectives and addressing concerns pertaining to “SDG washing” and “greenwashing”;
(e) Increased productive capacity, including through attracting investment capital, growing business linkages with multinational enterprises and participating in global and regional value chains;
(f) Mitigation of the effect of multiple and cascading challenges on the ability of member States to attract and benefit from investment for development and to foster private sector recovery;
(g) Progress by member States towards the achievement of Sustainable Development Goals 1, 4, 5, 8, 9, 10, 12, 13, 16 and 17.

12.50 To contribute to the objective, the subprogramme will:
(a) Support developing countries in fostering structural transformation through economic diversification towards a more sustainable and resilient economy;
(b) Enhance the participation of developing countries in the multilateral and regional trading systems, ensuring that developing countries’ participation in trade in goods and services at all levels of the value chain, including through broader UNCTAD support for the analysis and utilization of the relevant data, works as a driver for inclusive and sustainable development;
(c) Support the adoption of, improvement in and enforcement of national and regional competition and consumer protection legislation, through best practices, guidelines and peer reviews;
(d) Strengthen the capacity of trade policymakers to make well-informed policy decisions through improved understanding of and transparency in international trade and trade policy trends and trade-related regulations, and build private sector capacities to cope with policy and market requirements;
(e) Foster the mainstreaming of gender equality and disability inclusion into trade policies and build the capacity of member States through research findings, training workshops and policy dialogues to design and implement trade policies that allow women and persons with disabilities to benefit more from the opportunities arising from international trade;
(f) Contribute to research on international trade as an engine for development in the report of the Inter-Agency Task Force on Financing for Development, The Sustainable Development Goals Report, World Tariff Profiles and World Economic Situation and Prospects;
(g) Bearing in mind the current challenging global context, as described in paragraphs 12, 13, 18 and 19 of the Geneva Consensus, monitor and report on current trade measures, trends and policies and their impact on inclusive, sustainable and resilient development to provide policy recommendations to member States;
(h) Examine the effects of climate change in terms of trade on developing countries, and how environmental sustainability can promote global trade and inclusive development, including through trade policies facilitating global energy transition and supporting the competitiveness of developing countries in the production and trade of renewable and low-emission energy;
(i) Support member States, through research, capacity-building activities and the fostering of policy dialogue, in their efforts to break away from commodity dependence and realize trade and economic diversification gains, including for critical minerals, by adding value to their commodities and strengthening non-commodity activities.
12.51 The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) Enhanced understanding and policy response regarding current trends in the global economy, including ongoing environmental degradation, and improved ability of member States to benefit from international trade and foster the recovery of the trade sectors;
(b) Member States designing and implementing trade-policy responses to climate change from a development perspective;
(c) Member States identifying and addressing trade barriers and supply-side obstacles that disproportionately affect women and girls, and persons with disabilities;
(d) Member States and the international community understanding and addressing the cost and market access impacts of non-tariff measures, as well as their beneficial use for inclusive and sustainable development policies;
(e) Member States achieving positive development outcomes in relation to increased trade integration and activity;
(f) Member States seizing opportunities emerging from commodity trade and enhanced international and regional cooperation;
(g) Member States’ increased adoption, revision and/or effective implementation of competition and consumer protection legislation and institutional frameworks;
(h) Member States harnessing opportunities related to trade in environmentally sustainable products, and increasing capacities to assess the economic potential of the ocean, biodiversity and plastic substitutes-related sectors, and devising integrated sustainable use and trade action plans;
(i) Member States creating an enabling environment for the promotion of services and the creative economy to leapfrog into new, high-growth sectors of the world economy;
(j) Progress by member States towards the achievement of Sustainable Development Goals 1, 2, 3, 5, 7, 8, 9, 12, 13, 14 and 17.

12.63 To contribute to the objective, the subprogramme will:
(a) Provide research and analysis and policy recommendations for harnessing the digital economy and narrowing digital and data divides, including on the challenges of digital and emerging technology such as artificial intelligence, for supporting an enabling environment for digital infrastructure development, for improving digital literacy and skills and for supporting the participation of micro-, small and medium-sized enterprises and women- and youth-led business in the digital economy, and support developing countries in measuring e-commerce and the digital economy;
(b) Foster and ensure that the interests of developing countries are identified and integrated into international policy dialogues on e-commerce and the digital economy, including through the Intergovernmental Group of Experts on E-commerce and the Digital Economy and UNCTAD in synergy with the United Nations system and other organizations, and contribute to initiatives, debates and events on digital trade and digital economy from a development perspective, to the implementation of the World Summit on the Information Society architecture and to the implementation of the relevant commitments of the Global Digital Compact;
(c) Support developing countries in building their readiness to harness gains from electronic commerce and digital trade, including through eTrade Readiness Assessments and follow-up on the implementation of their policy recommendations, as well as through digital trade negotiation support;
(d) Support the development of an enabling environment at all levels for developing countries to mobilize resources through trade and investment, address challenges to their public revenue generation capacity and build circular approaches to the digital economy, to support their digital transformation and the availability of digital public goods, infrastructure and services;
(e) Ensure that international debates on science, technology and innovation incorporate the perspectives and priorities of developing countries through the Commission on Science and Technology for Development and the Technology Facilitation Mechanism;
(f) Inform policies and deliberations on science, technology and innovation for development, especially on frontier technologies, and contribute to the implementation of the World Summit on the Information Society architecture and the relevant commitments of the Global Digital Compact, including by servicing the work of the Working Group on Data Governance of the Commission on Science and Technology for Development;
(g) Foster international cooperation in science, technology and innovation, including through the Commission on Science and Technology for Development, to support national action through capacity-building and effective policy frameworks, and continue to support member States, especially developing countries, in collaboration with other relevant institutions, to enhance their capacity for innovation, research, development and applications of safe, secure and trustworthy emerging technologies, including artificial intelligence;
(h) Support the implementation of trade facilitation reforms, in particular the WTO Agreement on Trade Facilitation, as well as capacity-building for National Trade Facilitation Committees and bodies to increase the harmonization and coordination of trade facilitation measures at the national and regional levels, and continue its support for the development and implementation of appropriate legal and regulatory frameworks that reduce trade transaction costs;
(i) Provide technical assistance, leveraging the flagship customs software and associated capacity-building of the Automated System for Customs Data to boost member States’ capacity to support sustainable economic transformation, collect revenue, protect society and secure borders;
(j) Deliver technical guidance on data and policy recommendations and promote the sharing of best practices regarding the formulation and integration of strategic national policies, with the aim of integrating developing countries into regional and global supply and value chains and transport networks. This includes the flagship publication Review of Maritime Transport and other research products, advice on legislative frameworks and capacity-building, including the TrainForTrade programme on port management;
(k) Continue to assist in designing and implementing policies and actions aimed at improving the efficiency of the management of transport operations, trade transactions and logistics, including through the digitalization and electronic exchange of trade-related documents, and support international and regional transport networks through policy dialogue and cooperation mechanisms, ensuring their sustainability and resilience, including the organization of the UNCTAD Global Supply Chain Forum;
(l) Foster international policy dialogue on trade logistics issues, addressing the interests and challenges of developing countries in regional and international forums and focusing on specific vulnerabilities of landlocked developing countries and small island developing States, including through advocacy on transport, logistics and trade facilitation to enhance their resilience;
(m) Support developing countries in sustainably harnessing the ocean economy and implementing international commitments on maritime transport and ocean governance;
(n) Mainstream the cross-cutting issue of gender equality and the empowerment of women and girls across the three pillars of the subprogramme, with a special focus on empowering women digital entrepreneurs and enhancing their digital skills and opportunities.
12.64 The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) Improved capacity and policy formulation at the national, regional and global levels on e-commerce, digital trade and digital economy for sustainable and inclusive development;
(b) Improved coordination among Governments, together with development partners, civil society and the private sector, to implement policies on e-commerce and the digital economy that work for sustainable and inclusive development;
(c) Improved knowledge and understanding of policy options by policymakers and international consensus on emerging challenges and opportunities in science, technology and innovation for sustainable and inclusive development, as well as increased capacity of policymakers to assess, analyse and formulate policies on key issues on the international economic agenda;
(d) Member States implementing the outcomes of the World Summit on the Information Society and of the Technology Facilitation Mechanism;
(e) Member States harnessing science, technology and innovation for their national development strategies;
(f) Countries addressing key and emerging issues in trade logistics, as well as regional and global supply chains, including maritime transport policies and port management and effective implementation of relevant international commitments on maritime transport and ocean governance;
(g) Member States implementing trade facilitation reforms, including the WTO Agreement on Trade Facilitation;
(h) Enhanced capacity of member States to design and implement policies and action aimed at improving the efficiency of trade transactions and of the management of transport operations, including through the Automated System for Customs Data programme.

12.76 To contribute to the objective, the subprogramme will:
(a) Carry out research and technical cooperation activities to diversify the exports, foster the productive capacities and explore the financing structures of the above-mentioned countries, including through contributions to the reports of the Secretary-General to the General Assembly and the reports on the implementation and follow-up of relevant agendas and programmes of action;
(b) Implement technical cooperation programmes and develop strategic evidence- and policy-based options to promote sustainable structural economic transformation through productive capacity-building, diversification and support aimed at addressing vulnerability and building resilience, in particular for developing countries;
(c) Advocate for consensus in the international development community regarding the policy measures that best address the sustainable development problems of least developed countries, small island developing States and landlocked developing countries, including at the multilateral level, through the identification of new policy approaches and methodologies, training and capacity-building workshops, including for parliamentarians, as well as greater interaction with research institutes in least developed countries and other developing countries, and with development partners;
(d) Strengthen efforts to provide domestic policy support and capacity-building to the targeted groups derived from its innovative research and analysis, development of analytical tools and technical support;
(e) Provide technical support to least developed countries that are either in the process of graduation or recently graduated from the least developed country category, and including through the provision of General Assembly-mandated vulnerability profiles, as applicable, to ensure timely support leading to their graduation with momentum and a smooth transition towards their new status;
(f) Broaden its country-based technical support to include aspects such as national strategies for the development of productive capacities and structural economic transformation, inclusive trade, regional trade integration, rules of origin, inequality, poverty and vulnerability, enhanced market access and preference utilization, value addition in strategic products and geographical indications;
(g) Support member States in addressing the socioeconomic impact of external shocks on economies in target countries, through research work and technical cooperation, in order to facilitate specific, data-driven policy design and implementation aimed at building resilience;
(h) Support African countries in addressing their special concerns and needs, including as articulated in the New Partnership for Africa’s Development and in Agenda 2063: The Africa We Want, and in implementing the Agreement Establishing the African Continental Free Trade Area through research, technical cooperation and targeted policy advice;
(i) Deliver training, capacity-building activities and ad hoc research on market access, the productive capacities index, trade preferences and rules of origin to support the WTO least developed countries group in identifying and negotiating, in line with the needs of the group, result-oriented common positions for the relevant WTO committee meetings.
12.77 The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) Enhanced role of productive capacities in fostering structural economic transformation for inclusive growth and development in beneficiary countries;
(b) The development and implementation by member States of evidence-based policies and programmes to deepen domestic and regional markets and accelerate industrialization and socioeconomic transformation;
(c) Improved development policy formulation and implementation capacities in countries with specific needs, including the least developed countries and small island developing States, for enhanced resilience and structural transformations;
(d) The adoption by graduating countries of policies and strategies, including to support a smooth transition out of the least developed country category;
(e) Enhanced implementation of the African Continental Free Trade Area by member States;
(f) Greater participation by member States in multilateral forums dedicated to enhanced trade through transparent, predictable and simple rules of origin;
(g) Improved development policy formulation, implementation and monitoring capacities of member States, including greater policy coherence, and capacities to account for current and future shocks, reduce exposure to external shocks and build resilience;
(h) Stronger representation of the interests of least developed countries in WTO policy discussions and ministerial meetings;
(i) Progress by member States, in particular least developed countries and other groups of countries in special situations, towards the achievement of Sustainable Development Goals 8, 9, 10 and 17.

27.II.4 To contribute to the objective, the Office will:
(a) Provide guidance to Member States and stakeholders on implementing the Sendai Framework and accelerating the implementation of disaster risk reduction action through the production and dissemination of knowledge resources and best practices and the provision of capacity development and assistance at the regional, national and local levels;
(b) Support Member States and other development stakeholders in strengthening policy, programmatic and financing coherence between national climate change adaptation and national disaster risk reduction strategies across and within sectors, including by delivering evidence-based advocacy initiatives with clear calls to action to invest in disaster risk reduction and climate change adaptation and raise public awareness on the importance of such efforts;
(c) Provide support to Member States for reporting on the global targets of the Sendai Framework and for the related indicators of the Sustainable Development Goals and other relevant global frameworks using the Sendai Framework monitoring system;
(d) Expand the availability, quality and usability of global disaster risk knowledge in order to generate, share and institutionalize actionable insights;
(e) Promote the systematic institutionalization and resourcing of local risk governance as a core pillar of resilience by supporting the role of subnational actors in driving effective, inclusive and sustained local disaster resilience;
(f) Support Member States and other stakeholders in embedding disaster risk reduction at the core of public financial management and investment decision-making across both the public and private sectors;
(g) Lead and support pre-disaster recovery readiness initiatives, including governance, institutional and financing arrangements, for post-disaster response and recovery;
(h) Co-lead the Early Warnings for All initiative, together with the World Meteorological Organization, and lead on the planning and implementation of the initiative’s pillar 1 focusing on risk knowledge and management, in line with target (g) of the Sendai Framework and General Assembly resolution 77/289;
(i) Support Member States in strengthening disaster risk reduction policies and programmes in the least developed countries, landlocked developing countries, small island developing States and in humanitarian contexts, through the provision of focused assistance in overcoming the challenges, constraints and gaps that they continue to face, in line with agreed programmes of action;
(j) Work closely with Member States and stakeholders to follow up on General Assembly resolution 77/289 on the political declaration of the high-level meeting on the midterm review of the Sendai Framework for Disaster Risk Reduction 2015–2030, with a strengthened focus on the four priority areas identified by Member States, as well as on relevant commitments and decisions contained in the Pact for the Future (Assembly resolution 79/1);
(k) As co-host of the secretariat for the Santiago network for averting, minimizing and addressing loss and damage associated with the adverse effects of climate change, catalyse technical assistance for developing countries that are particularly vulnerable to the adverse effects of climate change, in line with General Assembly decision 78/546 and resolutions 78/152 and 78/153.
27.II.5 The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) Increased accountability for and commitment to implementing the Sendai Framework, including progress towards its goal and targets;
(b) Improved management and decision-making at the local, national and regional levels regarding current and future disaster risks through an evidence-based understanding of the multi-hazard risk landscape, enabling risk-informed policies, plans, regulatory frameworks and investment that align with the Sendai Framework and the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development;
(c) A global shift from reactive and more costly post-disaster spending to forward-looking, cost-effective, risk-sensitive investment and the mobilization of greater investment in disaster risk reduction, contributing to higher standards of resilience;
(d) Resilient recovery that delivers more effective, efficient and timely post-disaster support;
(e) Increased availability of people-centred, end-to-end multi-hazard early warning systems that reduce disaster impacts;
(f) Multi-sector and inclusive disaster risk reduction interventions that address the needs of the most vulnerable, thereby contributing to leaving no one behind.
27.II.6 With regard to the external factors, the overall plan for 2027 is based on the following planning assumptions:
(a) Disasters will continue to increase in frequency and magnitude owing to, among other underlying factors, the adverse effects of climate change, necessitating the integration of disaster risk reduction, climate action and efforts to build resilience to reduce the impact of disasters;
(b) Socioeconomic disparities and political tensions have the potential to deepen vulnerabilities of people living in the most disaster-prone countries, necessitating policies, plans, regulatory frameworks and investment decisions based on an accessible and evidence-based understanding of risks;
(c) The risk of damage and losses from disasters will remain high for the least developed countries, landlocked developing countries and small island developing States, necessitating an increase in early warning coverage and support to adequately prepare for and respond to disasters.
27.II.7 With regard to cooperation with other entities at the global, regional, national and local levels, it is recognized in the Sendai Framework that, while States have the overall responsibility for reducing disaster risk, it is a shared responsibility between Governments and relevant stakeholders. Therefore, cooperation with stakeholders, including science and academia, the media, the private sector and civil society, will support an all-of-society approach to disaster risk reduction. The Office will support private sector coordination efforts with national and local governments and strengthening micro-, small and medium-sized enterprises’ resilience to disasters. The Office will continue to work through the Sendai Stakeholder Engagement Mechanism and other region-specific mechanisms to promote inclusive and coherent implementation of the Sendai Framework and risk-informed development that leaves no one behind. The Office will also emphasize cooperation at the local level by promoting disaster resilience-building in cities.
27.II.8 With regard to inter-agency coordination and liaison, as Chair of the Senior Leadership Group on Disaster Risk Reduction for Resilience and as co-lead of the Early Warnings for All initiative, the Office will promote synergies within the United Nations system on interventions relating to disaster risk reduction and multi-hazard early warning systems. The Office will continue leading and coordinating the efforts of more than 40 United Nations entities engaged in the implementation of the United Nations Plan of Action on Disaster Risk Reduction for Resilience, further strengthening system-wide coherence and risk-informed approaches.
27.II.9 The Office integrates a gender perspective into its operational activities, deliverables and results, as appropriate. In line with the Sendai Framework, the Office will work to promote the full, equal and effective participation and leadership of women in the design, management, resourcing and implementation of gender-sensitive disaster risk reduction policies, plans and programmes, and enable Governments to better incorporate a gender perspective into national risk reduction strategies, early warning systems, climate adaptation and resilience-building.
27.II.10 In line with the United Nations Disability Inclusion Strategy, and recognizing that disasters and the consequent disruption to physical, social, economic and environmental networks and support systems disproportionately affect persons living with disabilities and their families, the United Nations Office for Disaster Risk Reduction will increase advocacy on disability-inclusive disaster risk reduction and ensure the participation of persons with disability stakeholder groups in processes that set the global and regional disaster risk reduction agendas, with the active involvement of its regional offices. The Office will support States Parties’ efforts to implement the Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities. Emphasis will be placed on supporting Member States in ensuring that persons with disabilities and their needs are included in the implementation of the Sendai Framework and its expected results, goal and targets, including through the availability of disaggregated data.

14.16 To contribute to the objective, the subprogramme will:
(a) Support Member States with the most up-to-date science to underpin national and international policy-setting;
(b) Provide guidance and technical assistance on climate plans, sectoral road maps, transparency and technology transfer to support Member States in fulfilling their climate obligations under the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change and the Paris Agreement;
(c) Accelerate sectoral transformation models to help Member States with low-emission development and increased adaptation and resilience;
(d) Build the technical capacity of Member States and subnational governments to scale sectoral solutions through private sector partnerships and innovative climate finance.
14.17 These workstreams will help Member States make progress towards the achievement of Sustainable Development Goal 13.
14.18 The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) Member States translating science and policy recommendations into actionable and investable climate plans that strengthen sustainable development and increasing their capacity to monitor progress transparently and efficiently;
(b) Member States lowering greenhouse gas emissions by implementing sectoral mitigation solutions, including in the energy, transport, cooling, construction and land use sectors;
(c) Member States enhancing resilience and adaptation to the impacts of climate change, including through nature-based solutions and improved multi-hazard monitoring, forecasting and early warning systems;
(d) Member States using climate strategies (e.g. nationally determined contributions, national adaptation plans and biennial transparency reviews) to guide investment and attract innovative, concessional and private finance to accelerate mitigation, adaptation and resilience.

14.30 To contribute to the objective, the subprogramme will:
(a) Develop and maintain interoperable data systems, standards and analytical tools to support Sustainable Development Goal monitoring and the tracking of environmental impacts and compliance;
(b) Provide digital platforms and technical support services to Member States, related to access to, the integration of and the use of environmental data and evidence for policy development, planning and decision-making;
(c) Build digital capacity for environmental policy, planning and action, including through inclusive approaches, strengthened digital literacy, the integration of digital tools into national development planning and enhanced regional cooperation, particularly in developing countries, including the least developed countries, landlocked developing countries and small island developing States;
(d) Provide technical guidance, advisory services and capacity-building to Member States and partners on the responsible use of artificial intelligence and other digital technologies for environmental policy and action, including an understanding of opportunities, risks and governance approaches;
(e) Develop and disseminate shared principles, standards and good practices to promote environmentally sustainable and risk-informed digital innovation.
14.31 These workstreams will help Member States make progress towards the achievement of the Sustainable Development Goals, in particular Goals 9, 10, 16 and 17.
14.32 The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) Timely, evidence-based decision-making on environmental issues at the national and global levels;
(b) Policy coherence and improved accountability of Member States for progress towards internationally agreed environmental and development targets, including the Sustainable Development Goals and multilateral environmental agreements;
(c) Data-driven environmental policies, plans and investments, particularly in developing countries, including the least developed countries, landlocked developing countries and small island developing States, contributing to reduced digital divides and enhanced regional and cross-border cooperation;
(d) Increased adoption and responsible use of artificial intelligence and other emerging digital technologies for environmental applications, guided by sustainability principles and best practices that support innovation, while managing environmental, social and economic risks.

14.44 To contribute to the objective, the subprogramme will:
(a) Enhance the capacity of Member States to sustainably manage and restore their natural and productive ecosystems;
(b) Strengthen the capacity of Member States to integrate the sustainable management of nature into development planning and other cross-sectoral decision-making frameworks;
(c) Promote a people-oriented approach to nature conservation, restoration and sustainable use, recognizing the importance of policies that support poverty eradication and the needs and livelihoods of biodiversity-dependent communities;
(d) Promote a whole-of-government and whole-of-society approach to addressing interrelated nature conservation and development challenges.
14.45 These workstreams will help Member States make progress towards the achievement of the Sustainable Development Goals, in particular Goals 3, 6, 12, 13, 14 and 15.
14.46 The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) Effective integration of sustainable management of biodiversity, land, freshwater bodies and oceans across sectors and systems;
(b) Increased extent of landscapes and seascapes under improved ecosystem management;
(c) Strengthened transparency, accountability and governance to deliver on biodiversity-, land-, freshwater- and ocean-related commitments;
(d) Greater public and private financial flows directed towards sustainable ecosystem management and restoration.

14.58 To contribute to the objective, the subprogramme will:
(a) Facilitate relevant meetings and intergovernmental processes at the global, regional and national levels involving Governments, other United Nations entities, inter-agency mechanisms and regional ministerial forums through information management products, technical support and advisory services on environmental law and policy;
(b) Assist with the coherent implementation of multilateral environmental agreements by providing support to strengthen international and national efforts to achieve global environmental targets and by supporting Parties in national reporting to related conventions;
(c) Develop and disseminate innovative knowledge management tools and programmatic initiatives such as the United Nations Information Portal on Multilateral Environmental Agreements and the Programme for the Development and Periodic Review of Environmental Law (Montevideo Programme), supported by training, technical assistance and communication activities, including mainstreaming a gender perspective;
(d) Promote the uptake of environmentally sound practices across the United Nations system through the provision of policy advice and cooperate with United Nations country teams to mainstream lessons learned through the implementation of programmes, including replicating and scaling up interventions.
14.59 These workstreams will help Member States make progress towards the achievement of the Sustainable Development Goals, in particular Goals 5, 12, 13, 15, 16 and 17.
14.60 The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) Increased efficiency and effectiveness of environmental law and policymaking processes leading to coherent and science-based policies, including through Member States’ concerted action on international environmental issues;
(b) Improved governance and partnerships with major groups and stakeholders in the development of synergistic national programmes, as well as increased integration of environmental considerations into national and subnational planning and budgeting processes on sustainable development.

14.73 To contribute to the objective, the subprogramme will:
(a) Provide technical advice and capacity-building to Member States, the private sector and civil society, within the framework of relevant multilateral environmental agreements, the Global Framework on Chemicals – for a Planet Free of Harm from Chemicals and Waste and the broader international chemicals and waste agenda, with a view to strengthening legal, policy and regulatory frameworks and promoting the uptake of circular economy approaches and sustainable consumption and production approaches in key sectors and value chains;
(b) Promote the use of credible science and data on pollution and its sources, including through the new Intergovernmental Science-Policy Panel on Chemicals, Waste and Pollution;
(c) Collaborate with partners from the environment, health and other relevant sectors and support countries’ efforts in addressing issues at the interface of environment and health, in order to achieve environmental and health benefits, including through actions to improve air quality and to address the environmental dimensions of antimicrobial resistance and other related issues, including through a One Health approach.
14.74 These workstreams will help Member States make progress towards the achievement of the Sustainable Development Goals, in particular Goals 3, 6, 11, 12 and 14.
14.75 The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) A reduction in exposure to harmful chemicals and pollution;
(b) Countries developing, adopting, revising or implementing policies, laws or regulations that advance circular economy approaches, sound management for chemicals and waste and pollution prevention and control in key sectors;
(c) Private sector entities taking action to improve chemicals and waste management and reduce pollution;
(d) Sectoral and cross-sectoral efforts advancing positive outcomes for the environment and health.

14.87 To contribute to the objective, the subprogramme will:
(a) Support national and subnational governments in the design and implementation of policies and initiatives for sustainable consumption and production and circular economy approaches that reduce greenhouse gas emissions and pollutants and help restore ecosystem functions;
(b) Catalyse private sector investment for sustainability by engaging financial institutions and other private sector actors to align investment and lending with national development priorities, including support for small and medium-sized enterprises;
(c) Leverage public budgets and fiscal policies to mobilize private finance by advising Member States on public and fiscal measures that catalyse sustainable investment;
(d) Develop and refine metrics to track stocks and flows of natural and other forms of capital, by supporting the development and application of indicators that inform environmental, economic and financial decision-making;
(e) Support national and subnational governments in building institutional capacities and implementing development plans and policies and support businesses in adopting sustainable management and cleaner production practices across value chains.
14.88 These workstreams will help Member States make progress towards the achievement of the Sustainable Development Goals, in particular Goals 4, 8, 12, 13, 15 and 17.
14.89 The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) Governments and public and private sector institutions adopting or updating policy instruments and/or standards that accelerate sustainable consumption and production, including circular economy approaches and the green economy in the context of sustainable development and poverty eradication;
(b) Governments implementing fiscal, budgetary and policy measures that unlock or redirect public and private finance towards sustainable investments aligned with climate-, biodiversity- and pollution-related objectives and objectives relating to circular economy approaches;
(c) Banks and insurance institutions adopting governance arrangements, strategies, targets or disclosures to manage climate-, nature- and pollution-related risks and opportunities and reduce environmental footprints;
(d) Increased use of UNEP-supported knowledge, data, metrics and assessments to inform decision-making and strengthen evidence-based policymaking.

14.101 To contribute to the objective, the subprogramme will:
(a) Review the world environmental situation and strengthen the interface between science and policymaking across the sustainable development agenda, including through collaboration with the Statistical Commission and the five regional commissions;
(b) Develop scientifically credible and unbiased data, information and knowledge and provide policy-relevant analysis and policy recommendations to catalyse and accelerate solutions and action for the environment, based on a continuous review of the global status of the environment and trends;
(c) Catalyse systematic and transformative shifts that can drive progress towards the aspirations of achieving the environmental dimension of the 2030 Agenda, through gender-sensitive partnerships across all geographical areas, involving Governments and non-governmental organizations, the business and scientific communities, and citizens;
(d) Pursue broad stakeholder engagement as a critical step in enhancing societal understanding, acceptance and uptake of its scientific analysis and serve as a knowledge broker that builds consensus on existing and emerging environmental issues through platforms and networks, such as the Science-Policy-Business Forum on the Environment, the International Resource Panel, the World Adaptation Science Programme and the One Planet network;
(e) Provide analytical research, increase awareness about the environmental dimension of sustainable development and build capacity to measure, monitor and report on progress, helping Member States integrate common methods for tracking and measuring the environment-related Sustainable Development Goals, in particular Goals 1, 2, 4, 6, 11, 12, 13, 14, 15 and 17, including in their national processes.
14.102 The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) Increased production and use of scientific environmental data by Member States and other stakeholders;
(b) A more coherent approach to harmonizing country data, to tracking progress and to science-based environmental policymaking, within and among countries;
(c) Member States improving their tracking and comparability of progress towards achieving the Sustainable Development Goals, which enhances coherence for the integration of national sustainable development plans and other internationally agreed environmental goals.

25.3 To contribute to the objective, UNHCR will, in close cooperation with national and local authorities, partners in the United Nations system, non-governmental organizations, persons of concern, the private sector, development partners, academia and other relevant stakeholders:
(a) Swiftly and effectively respond to humanitarian crises, including in conflict-affected areas, by providing protection, humanitarian and life-saving assistance, while ensuring preparedness measures to respond to new emergencies with agility;
(b) Advocate for accession and adherence by States to relevant international and regional instruments, including the refugee and statelessness conventions;
(c) Promote and facilitate the admission, reception and treatment of refugees in accordance with internationally agreed standards and advocate for the prevention and reduction of statelessness;
(d) Support implementation of the Global Compact on Refugees, follow up on the implementation of pledges from the Global Refugee Forums, advocate for more active international solidarity and equitable burden- and responsibility-sharing to support communities and countries hosting refugees, and promote progress towards sustainable responses;
(e) Enhance the protection and assistance for internally displaced persons under the Inter-Agency Standing Committee coordination system, in line with relevant General Assembly resolutions;
(f) Contribute to the resilience of forcibly displaced persons and their host communities faced with the adverse effects of climate change and disasters, in consultation with national authorities and in cooperation with competent agencies.
25.4 By promoting comprehensive approaches, UNHCR will contribute to relevant goals contained in the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development, ensuring that refugees and other persons of concern are not left behind in development processes (see A/AC.96/76/5).
25.5 The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) Significant advances in the response to refugee situations through more predictable and equitable burden- and responsibility-sharing and through a multi-stakeholder approach, consistent with the Global Compact on Refugees;
(b) Strengthened national asylum systems that are compliant with international legal standards;
(c) The advancement of solutions for forcibly displaced people, including voluntary repatriation and, where appropriate and feasible, local integration and resettlement, while reaffirming the importance of support to facilitate sustainable reintegration in countries of origin;
(d) Reinforced solutions for stateless people, resulting in previously stateless individuals acquiring nationality or having their nationality confirmed;
(e) Improved access to protection and assistance for refugees and asylum-seekers through registration.
25.6 With regard to the external factors, the overall plan for 2027 is based on the following planning assumptions:
(a) Security conditions shall allow UNHCR to carry out its programmes;
(b) Humanitarian access will be granted in most countries, enabling UNHCR to respond to urgent protection and assistance needs;
(c) States and the international community at large will remain committed to contributing to and supporting the implementation of the Global Compact on Refugees, including pledges made at the Global Refugee Forum;
(d) Asylum space and policies will not further restrict admissions in some countries;
(e) Voluntary funding from donors will match the budgeted needs to respond effectively and adequately to situations of forced displacement and statelessness.
25.7 With regard to cooperation with other entities at the global, regional, national and local levels, UNHCR will engage a wide range of partners in programme delivery. UNHCR will strengthen partnerships with local actors, regional organizations, organizations led by refugees and other persons of concern, development actors and international financial institutions to advance the inclusion of displaced populations in United Nations programmes and national humanitarian and development policies and plans. UNHCR will strengthen feedback and response mechanisms as a means of consulting displaced and stateless people on its programmes. The Office will continue to invest in private sector partnerships in order to secure support for strategic and long-term responses. Efforts to address protection needs by empowering affected communities and fostering their resilience and self-reliance until durable solutions are found will be a priority.
25.8 With regard to inter-agency coordination and liaison, UNHCR will cooperate closely with resident and humanitarian coordinators to promote overarching comprehensive protection and solutions strategies. UNHCR will coordinate with other agencies by leveraging comparative advantages in support of nationally defined priorities and planning frameworks. This approach enhances complementarity and collective outcomes. The Office will remain engaged in United Nations coordination forums, including the Inter-Agency Standing Committee and the United Nations System Chief Executives Board for Coordination. Building on its institutional plan on solutions to internal displacement, UNHCR will continue to engage in situations of internal displacement.
25.9 UNHCR integrates a gender perspective into its operational activities, deliverables and results, as appropriate. UNHCR will remain fully committed to a non-discriminatory approach, ensuring that its responses and interventions consider the needs, priorities and capacities of affected communities. It will work to prevent, mitigate and respond to gender-based violence and violence against children, including sexual violence. The Office will also enhance collaboration with organizations led by forcibly displaced and stateless persons, including those led by women.
25.10 In line with the United Nations Disability Inclusion Strategy, UNHCR will enhance the participation of persons with disabilities and strengthen partnerships with organizations of persons with disabilities and the Global Network of Refugees with Disabilities. UNHCR will promote non-discrimination and disability inclusion across its programmes and will advocate for equal access for forcibly displaced persons with disabilities to social protection schemes.

3.IV.4 To contribute to the objective, the Office will:
(a) Support the implementation of the priorities of the African Peace and Security Architecture and the African Governance Architecture, and their operationalization, through conflict prevention, crisis/conflict management, post-conflict reconstruction and peacebuilding, a focus on strategic security issues, and coordination and partnership;
(b) Support regular, formal and informal engagements between the policy organs of the African Union, including the Peace and Security Council and the Permanent Representatives Committee, with the United Nations, including the Security Council and the Peacebuilding Commission;
(c) Engage with the African Union at senior and operational levels on political and operational priorities through the United Nations-African Union annual conference, the United Nations-African Union Joint Task Force on Peace and Security, the annual retreat of Special Representatives of the Secretary-General and the Chairperson of the African Union Commission and the annual United Nations-African Union consultative meeting on prevention and management of conflicts;
(d) Conduct good offices missions to address peace and security challenges, including with regard to dispute resolution and elections, upon request by or in collaboration with the African Union;
(e) Maintain regular interaction between the United Nations and the African Union, regional economic communities and regional mechanisms, as well as with Member States, on thematic and country-specific situations, including in support of the African Union early warning mechanism;
(f) Build institutional capacities of the African Union Commission to prevent, manage and respond to armed conflicts and address root causes of conflict in Africa, including by strengthening the capabilities of the African Union with regard to early warning and mediation;
(g) Support African Union efforts to advance implementation of Security Council resolution 1325 (2000) on women and peace and security, including by supporting efforts in addressing conflict-related sexual violence and enhancing the participation of women in peace and security processes;
(h) Support the implementation of the African Union Continental Framework for Youth, Peace and Security, including its Ten-Year Implementation Plan for 2020–2029;
(i) Support African Union efforts in advocating for the effective operationalization of relevant accountability frameworks for parties involved in armed conflict;
(j) Collaborate with the African Union to implement Security Council resolution 2719 (2023).
3.IV.5 The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) Strengthened strategic coherence and collaboration between the African Union, the United Nations and other partners in addressing Africa’s peace and security challenges, including the root causes of conflicts;
(b) Strengthened ability of the African Union, mainly through its Peace and Security Council, the African Union Commission, the regional economic communities and the regional mechanisms with regard to conflict prevention, management and resolution, including the capacity of the African Union to plan and manage its peace operations.
3.IV.6 With regard to the external factors, the overall plan for 2027 is based on the following planning assumptions:
(a) There is continued political will and commitment by the United Nations and the African Union in promoting cooperation with regional and subregional organizations in peace and security;
(b) The African Union will continue to develop the human and financial capacity necessary to effectively deliver on mandates to address peace and security challenges in Africa;
(c) Extrabudgetary funding is available.
3.IV.7 With regard to cooperation with other entities at the global, regional and national levels, the Office will maintain relationships with external partners and stakeholders, taking into consideration priorities expressed in Agenda 2063: The Africa We Want and relevant policy documents, the 2030 Agenda and the Joint United Nations-African Union Framework for Enhanced Partnership in Peace and Security. The Office will further maintain regular interaction on thematic and country-specific issues and advance common United Nations and African Union positions, while enhancing coordination and synergies between key partners, such as the European Union, regional economic communities and regional mechanisms.
3.IV.8 With regard to inter-agency coordination and liaison, the Office will continue to lead or support coordination mechanisms with United Nations entities to ensure coherence in delivering assistance to the African Union. The Office will also engage with relevant opportunities and issue-based coalitions of the Regional Collaborative Platform for Africa.
3.IV.9 The Office integrates a gender perspective into its operational activities, deliverables and results, as appropriate. A key focus will be on strengthening cooperation with the United Nations and the African Union to institutionalize gender-sensitive policymaking and programming. The Office will continue to support the full, equal and meaningful participation of women in preventive diplomacy, mediation, elections and early warning systems, and the deployment of women in peace support operations. This includes further mainstreaming the women and peace and security agenda in joint initiatives with the African Union Commission and subregional organizations.
3.IV.10 In line with the United Nations Disability Inclusion Strategy, the Office will provide support to the African Union, as relevant, to enhance the inclusion of persons with disabilities in peace and security initiatives.

To contribute to the objective, the mission will:
Engage in good offices activities to help countries to prevent political crises and election-related violence and peacefully resolve conflicts and crises stemming from political processes, including political transitions, and promote inclusive, peaceful and credible elections;
Leverage its partnership with subregional organizations, especially the Economic Community of Central African States (ECCAS) in conflict prevention, mediation and early warning, ensuring the participation of women, young people and other actors in inclusive peacebuilding and sustaining peace efforts, including the implementation of the regional strategy on hate speech, and addressing the adverse effects of climate change on peace and security;
Cooperate with the Lake Chad Basin Commission on the revision of the territorial action plans that will guide the implementation of the revised Regional Strategy for the Stabilization, Recovery and Resilience of the Boko Haram-Affected Areas of the Lake Chad Basin Region;
Address cross-regional peace and security challenges, including farmer-herder dynamics, the crisis in the Lake Chad Basin and maritime piracy in the Gulf of Guinea, in close coordination with United Nations entities in Central and West Africa;
Carry out regular early warning analysis to inform preventive diplomacy and good offices efforts for peace and security in Central Africa, and mobilize support for preventive diplomacy, in collaboration with ECCAS and United Nations entities;
Enhance the coherence of the work of the United Nations on peace and security issues in Central Africa through regular consultations with relevant United Nations entities in the subregion, coordinate the efforts of United Nations entities geared towards the implementation of the regional conflict prevention framework and in line with commitments of the Pact for the Future, and support the country teams in adopting and/or rolling out national sustaining peace strategies aligned with the framework;
Support the regular ministerial meetings of United Nations Standing Advisory Committee on Security Questions in Central Africa as a platform for confidence-building between Member States to discuss threats and challenges to regional peace and security, with a view to preventing or addressing conflicts, crises or inter-State tensions.
The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
Peaceful and credible electoral processes in the subregion, with relevant prevention mechanisms in place for potential election-related violence, Initiation or continuation of inclusive political dialogue processes, assisted by joint early-warning and good offices by the mission and ECCAS in countries holding elections or facing a political crisis;
The United Nations Standing Advisory Committee on Security Questions in Central Africa is contributing to reinforced cooperation and coordination among ECCAS member States, regional organizations and other key stakeholders of the subregion;
A shared understanding of key peace and security challenges and priorities among United Nations entities in Central Africa, integrating emerging drivers of conflicts, including the impact of climate change and environmental degradation;
Enhanced coordination of United Nations efforts in support of regional and subregional initiatives on peace and security and governance;
Improved interregional coordination of efforts between Central and West Africa on maritime security in the Gulf of Guinea, the impact of climate change on peace and security, farmer-herder dynamics and the crisis in the Lake Chad Basin region.
With regard to cooperation with other entities, the mission carries out joint analyses and activities on cross-regional peace and security challenges with other regional and subregional organizations. Early warning analysis and good offices are carried out, to the extent possible, jointly or in consultation with ECCAS.
With regard to inter-agency coordination and liaison, the mission works closely with the Office of the Special Envoy of the Secretary-General for the Great Lakes Region, United Nations Office for West Africa and the Sahel (UNOWAS), the United Nations Multidimensional Integrated Stabilization Mission in the Central African Republic (MINUSCA), the United Nations Organization Stabilization Mission in the Democratic Republic of the Congo (MONUSCO) and other relevant United Nations entities with respect to cross-border and transregional challenges to peace and security, in order to avoid duplication and ensure coherence and enhance synergies. Country-specific early warning and good offices activities are carried out in close coordination with the resident coordinators concerned.
With regard to external factors, the overall plan for 2027 is based on the following planning assumptions:
ECCAS institutional reform becomes increasingly more operational, with all peace and security institutions and mechanisms established and provided with adequate resources for their functioning in the context of new leadership of the sub-regional organization;
UNOCA and ECCAS maintain or strengthen their cooperation and regular consultations and adopt and roll out a joint plan of action, within the framework of a new memorandum of understanding between ECCAS and the United Nations on peace and security issues;
ECCAS member States remain committed to establishing a regional governance framework, work towards the adoption and implementation of regional governance standards and mechanisms and solicit or accept the support of UNOCA and regional and international partners;
The elections scheduled in the countries of the region, are held according to the constitutional and legal time frames and the countries concerned accept the political engagement of UNOCA and ECCAS to defuse tensions, prevent election-related violence and resolve election-related crises;
ECCAS and its member States show a commitment to the involvement of civil society actors, including the regional Coalition of Civil Society Organizations for Peace and Conflict Prevention in Central Africa, the Coalition of Youth, Peace and Security in Central Africa and women’s organizations, in the activities of ECCAS in the realm of peace and security, including early warning, conflict prevention and peacebuilding. The Coalition of Civil Society Organizations becomes fully operational, with adequate human resources, and is supported by ECCAS member States and partners;
ECCAS member States develop, adopt and begin the implementation of a conflict-sensitive regional strategy on climate change and resilient development and regional regulation on pastoralism and transhumance in Central Africa to address the impacts of climate change on peace and security, while strengthening national and local mechanisms to prevent and peacefully resolve farmer-herder conflicts and promote peaceful cross-border transhumance;
The international community increases support to Chad in light of the continued influx of Sudanese refugees and its impact on social cohesion and resources with a view to helping Chad responding to those challenges.
The mission works with other relevant United Nations entities and external partners to reinforce the capacities of women in decision-making and in mediation. It will continue to support the operationalization and build the capacity of the ECCAS network of women mediators established in December 2021 and endorsed by the Heads of State in January 2022. UNOCA will also support ECCAS in the finalization and dissemination of the updated Regional Action Plan on Women, Peace and Security.
In line with the United Nations Disability Inclusion Strategy, the mission advocates for political and electoral processes in the region to be disability-sensitive, by encouraging Member States to take appropriate measures to facilitate the participation of persons with disabilities. The mission also encourages and supports experience-sharing between the States of the region on disability inclusion, thus facilitating the adoption of measures that take into consideration the specificities of the regional and national contexts. The mission will also target partners such as organizations or associations of persons with disabilities as part of its capacity-building activities.

16.2 As a United Nations entity whose mandate contributes to the three pillars of the United Nations, the Office supports Member States through three broad, interconnected and mutually supportive workstreams:
(a) Normative work, including policy, advocacy and legislative assistance to promote the ratification and implementation of the relevant international treaties and the provision of secretariat and substantive services to the treaty-based, governing and other Member State-driven bodies that help identify priorities, challenges, responses and commitments in relevant mandate areas relating to drugs, crime, corruption and terrorism;
(b) Research and policy support work to expand the evidence base and inform policymaking processes at the national, regional and global levels, through increased knowledge and understanding of drug and crime issues;
(c) Technical cooperation to enhance the capacity of Member States and other stakeholders to prevent and counter illicit drugs, crime, corruption and terrorism at the local, national, regional and global levels through the Office’s specialized assistance, expertise and extensive field presence.
16.3 In 2027, the work of the Office will be guided by a UNODC strategy for the period 2026–2030, which is being finalized. Leveraging the strong global presence and thematic expertise of UNODC, the strategy seeks to enhance coordination and integration across the Office, identify emerging trends and threats, and develop effective responses to UNODC mandates.
16.4 With regard to cooperation with other entities, UNODC will continue to work closely with international and regional organizations and promote systematic South-South cooperation, enabling the exchange of information and expertise between countries. Moreover, the Office will facilitate multi-stakeholder engagement in partnerships with civil society, academia and the private sector. UNODC will promote an evidence-based analysis and understanding of the nature of the challenges faced by Member States and design coherent programmes and policies to support global peace and security.
16.5 With regard to inter-agency coordination and liaison, UNODC will continue to coordinate and participate in relevant groups linked to its mandated areas. It will continue to jointly implement projects on drug prevention, treatment and rehabilitation (World Health Organization (WHO)); corruption (United Nations Development Programme); terrorism prevention (Office of Counter-Terrorism (UNOCT)); access to justice for children (United Nations Children’s Fund); measuring illicit financial flows (United Nations Conference on Trade and Development); and women’s empowerment (United Nations Entity for Gender Equality and the Empowerment of Women (UN-Women)).
16.6 With regard to external factors, the overall plan for 2027 is based on the following planning assumptions:
(a) Member States recognize in their policies, programmes and budgets that challenges related to security, justice, governance and the rule of law must be addressed as part of an integrated effort to implement multilateral commitments;
(b) The United Nations system and other multilateral organizations highlight the importance of addressing issues under UNODC mandates, to contribute to the implementation of multilateral commitments in a holistic manner;
(c) Member States share real-time, statistical and operational data with the UNODC Research and Trend Analysis Branch and with their counterparts across borders;
(d) Extrabudgetary resources, including general-purpose funding for core functions, continue to be available.
16.7 The Office integrates a gender perspective into its operational activities, deliverables and results, as appropriate. In line with Economic and Social Council resolution 2025/4, the Office, through evidence-based engagement, will continue to strengthen Member States’ capacity to address the implications for, and needs and concerns of, women and men in relation to organized crime, corruption, terrorism and access to justice, enabling policies and institutions that better uphold the rule of law and generating measurable outcomes.
16.8 The Office will continue to promote meaningful youth participation in commissions and conventions, including at the twelfth session of the Conference of the States Parties to the United Nations Convention against Corruption, and the positive role of youth in peace and security.
16.9 In line with the United Nations Disability Inclusion Strategy, the Office will continue to promote and protect the rights of persons with disabilities.

16.18 To contribute to the objective, the subprogramme will:
(a) Promote the signature, ratification and implementation of the international drug control conventions, the United Nations Convention against Transnational Organized Crime and the Protocols thereto and the United Nations Convention against Cybercrime, including by developing tools and providing targeted assistance to policymakers, legislators, legislative drafters and criminal justice practitioners, as well as collecting and disseminating knowledge on organized crime and cybercrime;
(b) Support the Conference of the Parties to the United Nations Convention against Transnational Organized Crime, including the Mechanism for the Review of the Implementation of the Convention and the Protocols thereto, by assisting States Parties in participating successfully in the review process, both as States Parties under review and reviewing States Parties;
(c) Provide technical assistance to Member States in preventing and countering transnational organized crime, including in its evolving and emerging forms, ranging from trafficking in persons, smuggling of migrants and trafficking in firearms to other forms of organized crime and trafficking, such as trafficking in cultural property and falsified medical products, organized fraud, crimes that affect the environment, cybercrime and laundering the proceeds related to such crimes;
(d) Build the capacities of central authorities and other criminal justice actors in international cooperation on transnational organized crime and cybercrime and facilitate mutual legal assistance requests through its support to international judicial cooperation networks and to individual Member States, particularly in trafficking, cybercrime and any serious crime cases, as well as through global, regional and interregional initiatives;
(e) Provide support to Member States to strengthen border management, interdict contraband and develop post-seizure criminal justice cooperation, along drug and other trafficking routes aimed at disrupting the organized crime groups behind them, involving, as appropriate, relevant international organizations, civil society, the private sector and other actors and actively promote the use of special investigative techniques against drug trafficking and in organized crime and cybercrime investigations.
16.19 The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) Active and effective participation of States Parties to the United Nations Convention against Transnational Organized Crime in the Mechanism for the Review of the Implementation of the Convention and the Protocols thereto, and strengthened legislative, strategic and institutional frameworks to prevent and counter organized crime and cybercrime;
(b) Increased international cooperation and strengthened institutional and legislative capacity of Member States to detect, prevent, investigate and prosecute transnational organized crime and new and emerging crimes, including through enhanced cooperation on evidence in electronic form;
(c) Reduced trafficking in persons and smuggling of migrants, and trafficking in drugs, firearms and other contraband, through the dismantling of greater numbers of organized criminal groups and improved responses to criminal networks using information and communications technologies.

objectives:
16.30 The objective, to which this subprogramme contributes, is to ensure comprehensive and balanced responses to the world drug problem through integrated demand reduction and related measures, supply reduction and related measures, and enhanced international cooperation.
16.31 To contribute to the objective, the subprogramme will:
(a) Promote comprehensive and balanced approaches to countering the world drug problem in full conformity with the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations, the three international drug control conventions and in line with scientific evidence and assist Member States, upon request, in:
(i) Implementing the three international drug control conventions and all the international drug policy commitments to address and counter the world drug problem with a focus on public health, human rights and public security;
(ii) Establishing and/or expanding drug use prevention approaches and services, as well as drug dependence treatment, care and rehabilitation services and systems for people with drug use disorders;
(iii) Establishing and/or expanding HIV/AIDS prevention, treatment and care and support services for people who use drugs, including people in prison and other custodial settings;
(iv) Establishing and/or expanding access to and availability of controlled substances for medical and scientific use;
(b) Support Member States’ progress in each of the above-mentioned areas through advocacy, technical assistance, capacity-building, the provision of standards and operational guidelines based on science and evidence, and the development and dissemination of manuals, toolkits, reports and issue papers based on science and evidence as well as on applicable human rights obligations under international law;
(c) Assist Member States, upon request, by promoting sustainable livelihoods to reduce the illicit cultivation of drug crops through alternative development, including preventive alternative development;
(d) In coordination with subprogramme 1, promote the exchange of criminal intelligence and encourage multilateral operations targeting transnational criminal organizations involved in drug trafficking and provide enhanced technical assistance in accordance with the principle of shared responsibility;
(e) Build the capacity of national counterparts, at the request of Member States, to detect, interdict, investigate and prosecute drug-related offences, including the illicit cultivation, production, manufacturing and trafficking of narcotic drugs and psychotropic substances, taking into account socioeconomic factors;
(f) Facilitate the exchange of best practices and training curricula, methodologies and materials through the network of law enforcement training institutions, in coordination with subprogramme 1, helping Member States to build inclusive, healthy and resilient societies.
16.32 The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) Increased access to quality healthcare services that include scientific evidence-based interventions in the areas of drug abuse prevention, drug dependence treatment, HIV/AIDS prevention, treatment and care, and access to controlled substances, for medical and scientific use;
(b) Increased resilience, quality of life and diversified, licit, sustainable income for families in rural areas affected by or at risk of illicit cultivation, production of and trafficking in narcotic drugs and psychotropic substances;
(c) Reduced illicit supply of drugs through joint and coordinated work by law enforcement authorities to strengthen maritime, air and land border control, and to track and dismantle networks engaging in drug production, trafficking and distribution both online and offline.
16.33 The strategy and results contributing to the achievement of the objective are complemented by the strategies and results under subprogramme 1, Countering transnational organized crime, and subprogramme 6, Research, trend analysis and forensics, especially in relation to law enforcement and laboratory and scientific services.

