(b) Conformance with global United Nations Secretariat standards and technical procedures;
(c) Clients who are empowered during the decision-making process and whose administrative and management tasks are facilitated through access to appropriate information technology tools;
(d) Reduced fragmentation of digital tools and services, with staff incorporating tools and services seamlessly into their daily business workflows with increased effectiveness, efficiency and satisfaction;
(e) Greater scalability, with systems that are more adaptable to changing needs.

To contribute to the objective, the mission will:
Strengthen its early warning and conflict prevention and resolution action with relevant partners, with a focus on West African countries undergoing political transitions and those scheduled to hold elections in 2027; de-escalate crises relating to governance challenges; lead regional and international diplomatic engagements to build consensus on regional responses; support regional solutions to cross-cutting threats to peace and security; and monitor and assess political developments in the region;
Enhance partnerships with regional and subregional institutions, civil society organizations and United Nations entities to address regional peace and security challenges, focusing on root causes of instability and insecurity in the region, while reinforcing the peace-security-development nexus; and conduct high-level advocacy to facilitate humanitarian assistance and access to critical social services by populations affected by conflict and insecurity;
Accelerate the implementation of the women, peace and security and youth, peace and security agendas through the promotion of involvement of women and young people in political and decision-making processes and mediation initiatives; and advance the promotion of good governance and respect for human rights and the rule of law;
Strengthen its analytical work, including on governance, constitutional order, and the adverse implications of climate change.
The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
Prevention and mitigation of conflicts through consultative political processes, including in countries undergoing political transitions;
Strengthened regional and subregional cross-border responses and coherent, comprehensive and integrated approaches to challenges relating to climate change, transnational organized crime, illicit trafficking, farmer and herder dynamics, intercommunal violence, terrorism, violent extremism and humanitarian access;
Mainstreaming of women and youth participation into conflict prevention, management and resolution activities;
Strengthened initiatives to address violations of human rights and international humanitarian law and improve constitutional order and security sector governance.
With regard to cooperation with other entities, UNOWAS works with the African Union, the Economic Community of West African States (ECOWAS), the Mano River Union, the Lake Chad Basin Commission Initiative, the Liptako-Gourma Authority, the Gulf of Guinea Commission, the African Development Bank and other regional entities, national human rights institutions and other civil society groups in the region. The mission’s cooperation with these entities aim to strengthen and harmonize regional capacities for conflict prevention and resolution, peacebuilding; advance good governance, constitutional order and respect for human rights and the rule of law and the participation of women and young people in political and decision-making processes; and support regional responses to address climate change as well as cross-border threats to peace and security.
With regard to inter-agency coordination and liaison, UNOWAS collaborates with the Development Coordination Office and resident coordinators, humanitarian coordinators, United Nations country teams, United Nations agencies, funds and programmes based in Dakar and across the region and other United Nations partners to create political space for humanitarian access and developmental initiatives, including the United Nations integrated strategy for the Sahel. It coordinates with the United Nations Regional Office for Central Africa (UNOCA) on transregional issues affecting West and Central Africa, including terrorism in the Lake Chad Basin, farmer-herder conflicts, climate security, piracy and maritime security in the Gulf of Guinea.
In relation to the external factors, the overall plan for 2027 is based on the following planning assumptions:
Demand for UNOWAS good offices to facilitate political dialogue and defuse political tensions, and to support countries undergoing political transitions and institutional reforms;
Heads of State and Governments of West Africa, ECOWAS, the Lake Chad Basin Commission, the Mano River Union, the African Union, the Gulf of Guinea Commission and other regional entities demonstrate the political will for sustained support to regional peace and security mechanisms and a commitment to mobilizing the resources required to address challenges to peace and stability;
ECOWAS early warning and response mechanisms and other regional conflict prevention instruments are operational at the national and regional levels;
The international community remains committed to supporting the countries of the region and regional and subregional organizations in addressing governance and security challenges;
Peacebuilding and peace consolidation efforts by regional and subregional entities and other partners continue;
Political and technical-level engagements with UNOCA in support of the Gulf of Guinea Commission and the Interregional Coordination Centre for Maritime Safety and Security in the Gulf of Guinea continue;
Political commitment on the part of national, regional and international partners to work with the United Nations in the context of the United Nations integrated strategy for the Sahel;
Governments in the region remain willing to engage with UNOWAS and other partners to improve human rights and rule of law.

To contribute to the objective, the mission will:
Carry out good offices functions for preventive diplomacy in Kazakhstan, Kyrgyzstan, Tajikistan, Turkmenistan and Uzbekistan, as well as monitor and analyse regional developments in Central Asia and the wider geographical context, while maintaining contact with regional organizations and other entities of the United Nations system;
Organize an annual meeting of deputy ministers of foreign affairs of Central Asia and convene expert forums to advise the annual consultative meetings of the Central Asian Heads of State;
Advance political participation of women through the engagement of senior women political leaders in the Central Asia Women Leaders’ Caucus regional platform;
Promote arrangements on transboundary water management in the region, support initiatives and dialogue on water, energy and environmental and climate cooperation, and help to address challenges relating to transboundary water and climate change in line with the new strategy to support regional cooperation on water-energy, environment and climate for 2026-2030;
Implement the joint project with the Office of Counter-Terrorism based on the updated Joint Plan of Action for the Implementation of the United Nations Global Counter-Terrorism Strategy in Central Asia;
Continue the efforts of the Regional Centre to engage young people through the Preventive Diplomacy Academy;
Support the Central Asian States in addressing emerging threats to security and stability and the other external risk factors, including by maintaining close contact with the United Nations Assistance Mission in Afghanistan to ensure comprehensive analysis of the situation in the region.
The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
Improved regional cooperation and capacity in addressing transnational threats of terrorism, violent extremism conducive to terrorism, drug trafficking and organized crime, as well as coping with regional challenges concerning the management of water resources and climate change;
Political will to sustain the progress of the Governments of the Central Asian States regarding deeper engagements in promoting peace and stability through enhanced cooperation and trust-building in the region, including on high-level platforms such as the consultative meeting of Heads of State.
With regard to cooperation with other entities, the mission maintains cooperation with the Commonwealth of Independent States, the European Union, the Organization for Security and Co-operation in Europe, the Shanghai Cooperation Organization, as well as the International Fund for Saving the Aral Sea, among others. The Regional Centre continues exchanges with the Conference on Interaction and Confidence-building Measures in Asia and the Collective Security Treaty Organization on topics of common interest. It cooperates with relevant actors, including international financial institutions such as the World Bank. Such cooperation highly conducive to meaningful exchanges and coordination to avoid duplication and to look for synergy and complementarity.
With regard to inter-agency coordination and liaison, and to streamline the prevention efforts in the region, the Regional Centre cooperates closely with the resident coordinators in the five countries, the Development Coordination Office and other UN entities represented in the region, including UNOCT, UNDP, UN-Women, UNODC, CTED, UNESCO, UNECE and ESCAP. The mission regularly shares information and coordinates activities with the United Nations Assistance Mission in Afghanistan.
With regard to the external factors, the overall plan for 2027 is based on the following planning assumptions:
The continued commitment of the five Governments of the region to preventive diplomacy and strengthening regional dialogue and cooperation on issues of common concern;
The five countries of the region continue to monitor developments in Afghanistan and enhance preparedness to potential threats and risks of violent extremism and terrorism, drug trafficking and a potential humanitarian crisis. They continue to provide and advocate for humanitarian assistance to Afghanistan.
The Regional Centre integrates a gender perspective into operational and programmatic activities, deliverables and results, as appropriate. It encourages the countries to ensure gender balance in their national delegations and include gender-related issues in the agendas of regional expert-level workshops on preventive diplomacy and confidence-building for the states’ officials.
In line with the United Nations Disability Inclusion Strategy (UNDIS) and its action plan, the mission will work to ensure that the concerns and experiences of persons with disabilities are an integral part of the design, implementation, monitoring, and evaluation of all policies and programmes, so that persons with disabilities benefit equally. The UNDIS will be implemented internally through greater workforce diversity and a more inclusive physical environment, and externally through programming and stakeholder engagement.

3.III.3 To contribute to the objective, the Office will:
(a) Process and review the previously collected but not processed claims and present them to the Board of the Register of Damage for its decision;
(b) Undertake outreach and claim intake activities in the Occupied Palestinian Territory in relation to public claims and claims of natural persons, by maintaining a presence in the area of operation;
(c) Work on promoting the rule of law at the national and international levels, helping Member States to ensure equal access to justice for all claimants in the Occupied Palestinian Territory and make progress towards achieving Sustainable Development Goal 16.
3.III.4 The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) An increased number of efficiently processed claims, leading to a reduction in the gap between collected and unprocessed claims;
(b) Submission of new claims from Palestinian institutions and individuals;
(c) Processed claims decided upon by the Board and included in the Register.
3.III.5 With regard to the external factors, the overall plan for 2027 is based on the following planning assumptions:
(a) There is political will and cooperation on the part of all parties concerned;
(b) Extrabudgetary resources are available for maintaining a needed presence on the ground;
(c) The overall security situation on the ground and in the region allows for the mandate of the Register of Damage to be carried out.
3.III.6 With regard to inter-agency coordination and liaison, the Office will continue to cooperate closely with the United Nations Office for Project Services, which is the key provider of logistical, human and financial resources services in the implementation of outreach and claims collection activities funded by extrabudgetary resources. In addition, the Office will continue its close cooperation with the Department of Political and Peacebuilding Affairs, the United Nations Truce Supervision Organization and the Office for the Coordination of Humanitarian Affairs.
3.III.7 The Office integrates a gender perspective into its operational activities, deliverables and results, as appropriate. For example, consultations will be conducted with local women’s committees in all communities in which outreach and claim intake activities are planned. The Office will continue to pay special attention to informing potential female claimants of the possibility of and requirements for submitting claim forms and will provide them with support in the preparation of their claims, noting that 38 per cent of all claimants are female.
3.III.8 In line with the United Nations Disability Inclusion Strategy, the Office will continue to make special arrangements for the intake of claims from claimants with disabilities. These arrangements include on-site support of claimants, which enables the filing of claims without having to travel.

26.2 The mission of UNRWA is to help Palestine refugees achieve their full potential in human development under the difficult circumstances in which they live. In line with its mandate, UNRWA works towards seven programmatic objectives within its recalibrated strategic plan for 2026–2029, which replaces its previous strategic plan, for 2023–2028: (a) Palestine refugees are protected through the realization of their rights under international law; (b) Palestine refugees have universal access to quality primary healthcare and targeted access to secondary and tertiary care, leading to improved health outcomes; (c) Palestine refugees have access to inclusive and equitable quality education; (d) Palestine refugees have sustained and, where feasible, strengthened opportunities for decent work and income generation through skills, enterprise support and fair access to labour markets; (e) Palestine refugees have access to basic and public service infrastructure in safe and sustainable camp environments that protect their health and dignity; (f) the most vulnerable Palestine refugees receive more impactful, better-targeted social assistance; and (g) Palestine refugees are accurately registered and their status is securely maintained. In accordance with the strategic plan for 2026–2029, the proposed programme plan for 2027 has been updated to reflect the above-mentioned programmatic objectives and includes a new subprogramme 7 related to objective (g) above on the registration of Palestine refugees.
26.3 UNRWA operates in one of the world’s most complex and protracted refugee situations, characterized by recurrent conflict, mass displacement and poverty, with Palestine refugees facing structural barriers linked to statelessness, protracted displacement and discrimination. Against this backdrop, the Agency aims to discharge its mandate by providing education, health, relief and social services, livelihoods, infrastructure and camp improvement, protection and emergency assistance, serving approximately 2.6 million registered Palestine refugees in Gaza, Jordan, Lebanon, the Syrian Arab Republic and the occupied West Bank, including East Jerusalem, annually. The Agency’s operating space has significantly deteriorated since the attacks perpetrated by Hamas and other Palestinian armed groups on 7 October 2023 against Israel and the ensuing escalation of hostilities across Gaza, which has driven a catastrophic humanitarian situation marked by heavy loss of life among Palestine refugees, including Agency personnel, mass destruction of public and private infrastructure and the collapse of basic services.
26.4 In Gaza, UNRWA contributes to sustaining emergency water, shelter, primary healthcare, sanitation, education in emergencies and psychosocial support for a population of 2.1 million almost entirely dependent on humanitarian assistance, amid severe access constraints, near total market disruption and deliberate restrictions on the entry of humanitarian assistance that have led to critical shortages of food, non-food items, fuel and medicines. Repeated displacement and extensive damage to Agency installations have forced the delivery of life-saving services through temporary and mobile modalities, often in the vicinity of active military operations. The ceasefire that entered into force on 10 October 2025 has enabled only partial improvement in humanitarian access after more than two years of conflict. Furthermore, the UNRWA operating space has been degraded in both Gaza and the occupied West Bank, and almost eliminated in East Jerusalem, by Israeli legislation that entered into force in January 2025 which prohibits Agency activities and representation in East Jerusalem and imposes a no-contact policy between Israel and UNRWA officials in Gaza and the occupied West Bank, including East Jerusalem, despite two advisory opinions of the International Court of Justice (see A/78/968 and A/80/502). These laws undermine the Agency’s status, privileges and immunities and impede the delivery of life-saving assistance, including by affecting staff safety, severely disrupting supply-chain-dependent interventions, preventing the deployment of international staff and forcing the suspension of in-person basic education services for nearly 800 Palestine refugee children in East Jerusalem. In January 2026, Israeli authorities seized and demolished the UNRWA compound in East Jerusalem. Despite these constraints, UNRWA will continue to operate in Gaza and the occupied West Bank, including East Jerusalem, through the efforts of approximately 17,000 area staff supported by robust systems, structures and assurance mechanisms that allow reliable, at-scale provision of critical services. Within this context, the scale and configuration of Agency operations in Gaza in 2027 will depend on the existence of an enabling operational space and unhindered access, the pace of reconstruction, the restoration of basic services and the evolution of security conditions. UNRWA will pursue a flexible and adaptive operational approach, calibrated to evolving needs, access arrangements and operational restrictions. The Agency is expected to play a central role in future recovery and stabilization efforts and will sustain advocacy to secure predictable humanitarian access and safeguard the conditions necessary to discharge its mandate safely and effectively.
26.5 UNRWA will continue to uphold the humanitarian principles of humanity, impartiality, neutrality and independence as foundational values and operational imperatives. These principles are integral to the discharge of the Agency’s mandate. They are embedded across UNRWA policy, programming and oversight and underpin service delivery to protect humanitarian space, safeguard access, ensure the safety of Agency personnel and maintain the trust and confidence of Palestine refugees, host authorities, donors and Member States.
26.6 With regard to cooperation with other entities at the global, regional, national and local levels, UNRWA will maintain and strengthen a wide range of partnerships that reflect its commitment to Sustainable Development Goal 17, including with host and donor Governments, non-governmental organizations, UNRWA national committees and the private sector, which assist the Agency in delivering its mandate.
26.7 With regard to inter-agency coordination and liaison, UNRWA will maintain and expand partnerships with other United Nations entities. Many of these partnerships, such as those with the United Nations Educational, Scientific and Cultural Organization and the World Health Organization, date to the Agency’s founding in 1949. Inter-agency partners will continue to furnish strategic assistance to strengthen the Agency’s education and health programming. UNRWA will also work closely with other United Nations entities across its fields of operation to extend nutrition, shelter, water, sanitation and hygiene assistance, including in Gaza, as well as with United Nations country teams in its fields of operation to improve efficiencies, including through shared services.
26.8 With regard to the external factors, UNRWA considered applicable risks identified in its risk register, and, accordingly, the overall plan for 2027 is based on the following planning assumptions:
(a) Instability and, in some cases, unrest and possible instances of armed conflict will characterize the macroenvironment throughout the year;
(b) There is broad political support for the Agency’s mandate and operations;
(c) UNRWA will be able to maintain its presence and operational status in all fields of operation, with sufficient capacity to deliver planned services;
(d) Demand for Agency emergency assistance and core services will remain at a high level;
(e) Sufficient supplies will be admitted into Gaza to sustain the Agency’s operations;
(f) Conditions in Gaza will permit a phased transition from emergency to early recovery operations, although the pace and scope of the transition will remain uncertain and challenged by extensive damage to Agency installations and basic infrastructure;
(g) Voluntary contributions will be available for the implementation of the Agency’s mandate.
26.9 UNRWA integrates a gender perspective into its operations, deliverables and results, as appropriate. In 2027, the Agency will continue to provide: (a) survivors of gender-based violence with coordinated protection, health and case management assistance; and (b) women and girls with inclusive education, technical and vocational education and training and targeted livelihood opportunities. In parallel, UNRWA will strengthen its advocacy towards improved outcomes for Palestine refugee women and girls.
26.10 In line with the United Nations Disability Inclusion Strategy, the Agency will continue to: (a) ensure the physical accessibility of its installations, including health centres, schools and vocational training centres; (b) maintain health screening and early identification of disabilities among infants and children to support timely referral and intervention; (c) implement inclusive education practices that provide tailored support for students with disabilities; and (d) engage communities to address stigma and promote the rights, dignity and inclusion of Palestine refugees with disabilities.

26.19 To contribute to the objective, the subprogramme will:
(a) Mainstream cross-cutting issues that address protection risks and vulnerability across UNRWA systems, processes, methodologies, programming and service delivery;
(b) Provide medical, legal and/or psychosocial services for survivors of rights violations;
(c) Enhance protection monitoring, documentation and analysis to strengthen the evidence base for Agency interventions and advocate for the respect, protection and fulfilment of the rights of Palestine refugees under international law with duty bearers and external actors;
(d) Ensure that UNRWA services are equally accessible to Palestine refugee women and girls;
(e) Ensure gender-sensitive service provision for women and girls, including: (i) reproductive and maternal health services; (ii) livelihood support; (iii) education services; and (iv) advocacy aimed at ensuring their safety;
(e) Systematically incorporate humanitarian principles into all UNRWA services and systems and ensure that Agency premises are managed in line with those principles.
26.20 The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) Equitable, safe and dignified access to UNRWA services for Palestine refugees;
(b) The protection needs of vulnerable and at-risk Palestine refugees being addressed;
(c) Respect for and protection of the rights of Palestine refugees under international law;
(d) Empowerment of Palestine refugee women and girls and their access to services;
(e) Adherence to humanitarian principles by all UNRWA personnel across all Agency services and operations, and within all UNRWA premises.

26.32 To contribute to the objective, the subprogramme will:
(a) Address the maternal and child healthcare needs of Palestine refugees through the systematic delivery of preconception, prenatal and postnatal care, family planning services, childhood immunization, nutritional support and screening for disabilities;
(b) Control the status of noncommunicable diseases and mental health issues through screening, prevention and health promotion interventions;
(c) Control the status of communicable diseases by strengthening surveillance and response capacity and participating in host Government mass immunization campaigns;
(d) Provide safe, quality and accessible healthcare, even during times of crises, through fixed and mobile UNRWA health facilities.
26.33 The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) Improved maternal and child health among Palestine refugees;
(b) Improved control status of noncommunicable diseases and mental health;
(c) Improved control status of communicable diseases and prevention and control of infectious diseases;
(d) Safe access to quality primary, secondary and tertiary healthcare for Palestine refugees.

26.45 To contribute to the objective, the subprogramme will:
(a) Maintain universal access to formal education for Palestine refugee children and youth and, where conditions permit, help those who have lost access to regain it;
(b) Ensure that teaching approaches and learning materials used in UNRWA schools promote critical thinking, gender equality, human rights and peace education and adherence to United Nations values and principles, including neutrality;
(c) Strengthen the quality of education, address learning recovery where students have fallen behind and enhance the professional practice of teachers;
(d) Ensure that student well-being and inclusion is supported, including through tailored assistance to students with disabilities and psychosocial support to students exposed to protection risks.
26.46 The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) Access to education being protected and sustained, especially in unstable contexts;
(b) Improved learning outcomes for Palestine refugee girls and boys.

26.58 To contribute to the objective, the subprogramme will:
(a) Provide quality and market-relevant technical and vocational education and training to young Palestine refugees at UNRWA vocational training centres and education science facilities for degree-level teacher education in the occupied West Bank and Jordan;
(b) Extend higher education scholarships to young Palestine refugees;
(c) Provide financial services, including diversified business and consumer loan products, to low-income Palestine refugees;
(d) Generate employment opportunities through infrastructure works in camps.
26.59 The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) The enhancement of market-relevant skills;
(b) Improved income generation and job opportunities for Palestine refugees.

26.71 To contribute to the objective, the subprogramme will:
(a) Provide adequate and safe shelters for Palestine refugees living in camps, prioritizing shelter rehabilitation works for the most vulnerable;
(b) Provide Palestine refugees living in camps with effective environmental health services, including water, drainage and sewerage systems, as well as solid waste collection and disposal;
(c) Support community-driven camp improvement planning and works.
26.72 The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) Adequate and safe shelters for Palestine refugees residing in camps;
(b) Healthy and environmentally sustainable conditions for Palestine refugees residing in camps.

26.97 To contribute to the objective, the subprogramme will:
(a) Strengthen a comprehensive, accurate and secure registration system for Palestine refugees;
(b) Preserve, update and digitize registration records of Palestine refugees.
26.98 The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) Palestine refugees being able to access UNRWA services with greater ease, efficiency and dignity;
(b) The safeguarding of the status of Palestine refugees for current and future generations.

3.II.3 To contribute to the objective, the Office will:
(a) Intensify engagement with regional and international actors, including with members of the Middle East Quartet, to work towards ending the occupation and resolving the Israeli-Palestinian conflict on the basis of a two-State solution, and reducing regional tensions;
(b) Support the ceasefire in Gaza and the implementation of Security Council resolution 2803 (2025), including through humanitarian assistance, recovery, and reconstruction efforts in Gaza;
(c) Increase engagement with the parties and relevant partners to reduce violence and tensions across the Occupied Palestinian Territory, including East Jerusalem, and the region, and uphold the viability of the two-State solution;
(d) Strengthen the Palestinian Authority’s institutions and support its State-building agenda, including by supporting the parties to update their economic and administrative relationships and to enact policy reforms;
(e) Continue to work with the parties to uphold their obligations under United Nations resolutions and international law, including reporting to the Security Council on resolution 2334 (2016);
(f) Support efforts, including through recovery and reconstruction efforts in Gaza, in collaboration with the United Nations country team, and through engagement with entities established pursuant to Security Council resolution 2803 (2025), towards the political, economic and administrative unification of Gaza and the West Bank, including East Jerusalem.
3.II.4 The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) Movement towards meaningful negotiations aimed at ending the occupation and resolving the Israeli-Palestinian conflict on the basis of the two-State solution and internationally recognized parameters;
(b) Reduced tensions and violence, the full implementation of the Gaza ceasefire agreement and the prevention of renewed escalation;
(c) Reduced settlement activities, land confiscation and home demolitions, and reduced levels of settler violence against Palestinian civilians and their properties and increased implementation of Security Council resolution 2334 (2016);
(d) Increased institutional stability and governance capacity of the Palestinian Authority and tangible steps towards improving the fiscal situation;
(e) Mobilization of political, financial and technical support across the Occupied Palestinian Territory, including East Jerusalem, including for recovery and reconstruction efforts in Gaza;
(f) Provision and distribution of assistance to meet the needs of the Palestinian people, across the Occupied Palestinian Territory, including East Jerusalem;
(g) Advancement of a single, democratic, national Palestinian governing authority administering the entire Occupied Palestinian Territory;
(h) The parties identifying tangible steps to improve the situation on the ground.
3.II.5 With regard to the external factors, the overall plan for 2027 is based on the following planning assumptions:
(a) The two-State solution remains viable, in line with relevant United Nations resolutions and bilateral agreements;
(b) The Gaza ceasefire will continue to be implemented and relevant parties support de-escalation efforts;
(c) Governance arrangements, security arrangements and other enabling conditions are in place that allow humanitarian, recovery and reconstruction activities in Gaza;
(d) The security situation in the Occupied Palestinian Territory allows for the continued implementation of United Nations activities, including political outreach, development and humanitarian programming;
(e) Israeli and Palestinian interlocutors and regional and international stakeholders engage in meaningful dialogue and cooperation with the Office and the United Nations country team in the performance of their functions;
(f) There is adequate political support from Member States and financial support from donors.
3.II.6 With regard to cooperation with other entities at the global, regional, national and local levels, the Office will continue its active engagement, including promoting de-escalation, conducting preventive diplomacy, halting and reversing negative trends and enhancing coordination efforts to advance concrete steps towards a two-State solution to the Israeli-Palestinian conflict. The Office will remain an active contributor to the Ad Hoc Liaison Committee for the Coordination of the International Assistance to Palestinians and will continue to promote actionable recommendations to both parties. The Office will strengthen engagement with other forums and partners, such as the Development Partners Group, Arab and Gulf donors, and European Union-led support to Palestinian Authority reforms, including the Palestine Donor Group, in order to facilitate the work of the United Nations entities supporting the Palestinian people. The Office will advocate with the parties, Member States, humanitarian and development partners, regional actors, the private sector, civil society and other stakeholders to facilitate humanitarian relief, recovery, reconstruction, and economic and social development.
3.II.7 With regard to inter-agency coordination and liaison, the Office will continue to lead the coordination of more than 20 United Nations resident and non-resident agencies, funds and programmes in responding to the development and humanitarian needs of the Palestinian people. The Office will promote policies to improve movement and access for people and goods throughout the Occupied Palestinian Territory. The Office will foster inter-agency collaboration by identifying opportunities for improving socioeconomic conditions in the occupied West Bank, including East Jerusalem, facilitating humanitarian, recovery and reconstruction efforts in Gaza, and implementing the United Nations Sustainable Development Cooperation Framework and the United Nations early recovery approach and action plan for Gaza. The Office will also coordinate with regional entities, such as the regional Development Coordination Office and the regional offices of United Nations agencies, funds and programmes, to leverage expertise and identify opportunities. The Office will work with the United Nations Office for Project Services, which will continue to manage the day-to-day operations of the United Nations mechanism established pursuant to Security Council resolution 2720 (2023), reporting to the Deputy Special Coordinator/Resident Coordinator/ Humanitarian Coordinator, and through them to the Special Coordinator for the Middle East Peace Process.
3.II.8 The Office integrates a gender perspective into its operational activities, deliverables and results, as appropriate, including through engagement with women’s and youth groups in support of the implementation of Security Council resolution 1325 (2000). The Office will continue to promote women’s participation in political processes, conflict prevention and peacebuilding, including through engagement with women leaders and civil society, ensuring that their priorities inform policy dialogue and advocacy. Through its coordination role within the United Nations country team, the Office advances the women and peace and security agenda, including within recovery planning processes. The Office will continue to highlight the distinct needs of women and girls across the Occupied Palestinian Territory and ensure that publications and reporting systematically reflect differentiated impacts.
3.II.9 In line with the United Nations Disability Inclusion Strategy, the Office will advocate for the full inclusion of individuals with disabilities in all aspects of United Nations programmes. Programmatic efforts will prioritize disability inclusion in recovery and reconstruction efforts in Gaza, including by advocating for inclusive planning and facilitating access to specialized services.

To contribute to the objective, the mission will:
Exercise its good offices between the parties to preserve and consolidate the restoration of a cessation of hostilities, prevent a relapse into conflict and advance the full implementation of resolution 1701 (2006);
Intensify efforts to resolve outstanding obligations of all parties under resolution 1701 (2006) to secure a permanent ceasefire and long-term solution to the conflict, including through the restoration of Lebanon’s territorial integrity and delineation of Lebanon’s international borders;
Advocate for Lebanon’s political leadership to ensure the effective functioning of State institutions to provide essential services for its citizens, preparations towards the conduct of transparent, inclusive and credible parliamentary elections, the adoption of a macroeconomic stabilization programme and implementation of a comprehensive reform and recovery programme;
Facilitate urgently required humanitarian, recovery and reconstruction imperatives necessitated by hostilities and mitigate the impact of continued volatility in the Syrian Arab Republic on the security, stability and refugee situation in Lebanon, including through, inter alia, support for bilateral cooperation initiatives;
Support the accelerated extension of State authority and assertion of a State monopoly on arms across Lebanese territory, intensify engagement with national political stakeholders and with international partners to facilitate the disarmament of non-State armed groups through a whole-of-government approach, and engagement with Syrian authorities on unresolved border, security and judicial issues, in order to strengthen the State security presence along border points and ensure the effective enforcement of the arms embargo.
The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
An enduring cessation of hostilities with the requisite political support in Lebanon and Israel;
Direct or indirect negotiations between Lebanon and Israel to address outstanding obligations under resolution 1701 (2006) towards an eventual permanent ceasefire;
Effective Lebanese State institutions in place and comprehensive structural reforms initiated to put the country on the path to recovery, uphold its democratic principles and strengthen good governance;
The State’s effective exercise of authority over all Lebanese territory, including through the strengthened deployment of the Lebanese Armed Forces, and steps taken to secure its borders and other entry points;
Lebanon’s implementation of a political framework that establishes a State monopoly over the possession, transfer and production of arms;
Engagement on the Lebanon-Israel and Lebanon-Syrian Arab Republic tracks to delineate the international borders of Lebanon in its entirety, including by addressing the issue of the Shab’a Farms.
With regard to cooperation with other entities, the mission coordinates with the League of Arab States, the European Union and international financial institutions such as the World Bank and the International Monetary Fund to ensure coherence and alignment in political advocacy. The Special Coordinator engages international and regional stakeholders as part of her good offices mandate, to help build trust and defuse tensions, mobilize broad support for Lebanon’s stability and promote political and diplomatic processes towards the full implementation of resolution 1701 (2006).
With regard to inter-agency coordination and liaison, pending a decision by the Security Council on the options for the future of the implementation of resolution 1701 (2006) after UNIFIL withdraws, UNSCOL will lead efforts in support of the implementation of resolution 1701 (2006) to maintain the cessation of hostilities and to strengthen the extension of State authority across all of Lebanon’s territory. Leveraging the presence of all UN entities, the mission brings all available political, security and development tools to bear in a coordinated and harmonized manner in support of Lebanon’s recovery, stability, and extension of its State authority. The mission also contributes to the United Nations Sustainable Development Cooperation Framework and leads its peace and governance results group, including work on areas related to good governance, support to law enforcement and security entities, peacebuilding, and joint initiatives to enhance trust building and youth empowerment.
With regard to the external factors, the overall plan for 2027 is based on the following planning assumptions:
International consensus on Lebanon will continue to be challenged;
Political divisions and deepened sectarian tensions may hinder effective government decision-making and undermine internal dialogue and economic and governance reforms;
The prolonged socioeconomic crisis, compounded by the impact of the conflict, and its humanitarian repercussions will remain a source of instability and social unrest;
Lebanon’s internal stability and security will remain vulnerable to the volatile regional situation, highlighting the need for bilateral engagement to address potential security concerns and shield the country from spillover;
Internal dynamics in the Syrian Arab Republic, as well as Lebanese-Syrian relations, are likely to dictate cross-border movements;
Relations between Lebanon and Israel will remain fraught, underlining the imperative for engagement of the parties in a political process to consolidate an enduring cessation of hostilities and address the underlying causes of the conflict within the framework of resolution 1701 (2006) towards pursuit of a permanent ceasefire;
Extension of State authority in Lebanon and along its borders will continue to be affected by political will, other law-and-order requirements and financial, political and logistical challenges faced by the State security institutions in asserting the State’s monopoly on arms.
In coordination with national stakeholders and UN entities, the mission integrates a gender perspective, as appropriate, and is working to promote the adoption of measures to protect and prevent violence against women and enhance women’s political leadership and representation through a range of initiatives, including legislative reforms (e.g. special temporary measures for elections) to promote women’s political participation, as well as women’s engagement in inclusive peace dialogue networks at the local and national levels. Together with relevant partners in the United Nations country team, the mission supports networks engaged in reconciliation, conflict prevention, inclusive community dialogue and mediation.
In line with the United Nations Disability Inclusion Strategy, the mission will promote and mainstream disability inclusion in the implementation of its mandate within its internal work practices and operations and in broader joint UN system engagements. The mission also implements a specific action plan for disability inclusion and through active involvement within the broader UN System Working Group for disability inclusion at both local and global levels.

To contribute to the objective, the Mission will:
Provide mediation and good offices in support of a Libyan-led and Libyan-owned political process facilitated by the United Nations, in line with the political roadmap and its three core pillars announced by the Special Representative in August 2025;
Address long-term drivers of conflict, and work towards government unification and strengthen inclusive political engagement with diverse stakeholders, including women, youth, and persons with disabilities, in Libya taking into consideration the recommendations of the Structured Dialogue of 2025-2026;
Provide technical assistance to the High National Elections Commission in conducting transparent and inclusive national and municipal elections;
Support inclusive, victim-centred and rights-based national reconciliation processes based on transitional justice principles, capacity-building for reconciliation bodies and the facilitation of dialogue among diverse Libyan actors, including civil society, women, persons with disabilities and youth;
Establish a more consistent presence across Libya, including targeted expansions in Benghazi and Sabha within a cost-neutral realignment and existing resources, while increasing engagement with key stakeholders in eastern and southern Libya;
Strengthen the rule of law and accountability by supporting Libya’s judiciary and prosecutorial systems, including their critical role in ensuring justice for all Libyans;
Promote and protect human rights by monitoring, reporting and addressing violations, with a focus on sexual violence in conflict and violations against vulnerable groups, and by ensuring civic space for political participation;
Advance security sector reform, by supporting Libyan-led and Libyan owned initiatives that reduce the risk of a return to widespread conflict, including through confidence-building measures, dialogue among security and military actors, and engagement with legislative bodies on national security legislation, with a view to the reunification of security and military institutions under civilian oversight;
Facilitate disarmament, demobilization and reintegration through support to national coordination mechanisms and civil society engagement, including technical assistance for planning and capacity development in mine action coordination and weapons and ammunition management;
Support the 5+5 Joint Military Commission and its subcommittees, by facilitating confidence-building measures, dialogue, and reconciliation among armed actors to prevent violence and conflict escalation and support the implementation of the ceasefire agreement, including progress on the withdrawal of foreign forces, foreign fighters, and mercenaries, through engagement at national and local levels;
Support progress towards a unified national budget and unified institutions which promote independent and transparent economic and oversight institutions, in close cooperation with the international community, UN country team and international financial institutions;
Facilitate inclusive dialogue on economic governance and political economy dynamics to support a shared national economic vision and advance reforms that address conflict drivers and deliver tangible benefits for all Libyans, including women, youth, and persons with disabilities;
Engage with neighbouring countries, including Sahel countries, in support of regional cooperation and stabilization efforts, and ensuring their perspectives and security concerns are reflected in efforts to advance the Libyan-led and Libyan-owned political process.
The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
An inclusive Libyan-led and Libyan-owned political process leading to elected, unified, functional and representative state institutions, including economic institutions, contributing to political stability, sound economic governance, and the protection of civic space as an enabler of credible elections and legitimate institutions;
Enhanced accountability of institutions, promotion of the rule of law throughout the country, and reconciliation and transitional justice processes promoting and protecting human right;
Progress toward unified security and military institutions operating under civilian oversight, including improved coordination and accountability among security actors and professional defence and security forces, through support to the implementation of the ceasefire agreement and initiatives that reduce the risk of a return to widespread conflict, in support of the political process and electoral stability.
With regard to cooperation with other entities, the Mission cooperates with relevant local, national, regional and international partners and regional organizations, including the African Union, the League of Arab States and the European Union in support of the Libyan-led and Libyan-owned political process.
With regard to inter-agency coordination and liaison, the Mission and the United Nations country team will continue to implement the United Nations Sustainable Development Cooperation Framework, extended by one additional year. Coordination modalities will be adjusted as necessary to ensure coherence between political engagement and development priorities, taking into account the evolving context and system-wide guidance.
With regard to the external factors, the overall plan for 2027 is based on the following planning assumptions:
The Libyan Political Agreement of December 2015, Security Council resolutions 2510 (2020) and 2796 (2025), the conclusions of the Berlin Conference on Libya, the October 2020 Ceasefire Agreement, the Libyan Political Dialogue Forum Tunis roadmap and accompanying outputs and the UNSMIL political roadmap announced in 2025 will remain the guiding framework for United Nations support in Libya, including efforts to enable an inclusive political process and the conditions for elections;
Economic governance challenges, including limited oversight, weak transparency and accountability, and constrained institutional capacity, are expected to persist. External factors include oil price fluctuations affecting revenues and regional dynamics;
Progress in implementing the October 2020 Ceasefire Agreement, including the withdrawal of foreign forces, foreign fighters and mercenaries, is expected to remain uneven and dependent on sustained commitment by Libyan and external actors. The truce agreed between armed groups in western Libya following the May 2025 clashes in Tripoli will continue to hold;
UNSMIL’s efforts will be shaped by the willingness of Libyan actors to engage in confidence-building measures, dialogue, and steps toward unification and reform of security institutions under civilian oversight, as well as broader regional dynamics;
The human rights situation in Libya will remain challenging, particularly in strengthening the rule of law, ensuring accountability for violations, and protecting civic space. The marginalization of women in political and reconciliation processes will continue to constrain peacebuilding;
Institutional weaknesses and divisions among the country’s security actors are expected to persist, continuing to create security vacuums and ungoverned spaces that may be exploited by violent extremist organizations, thereby increasing the risk of terrorist activity;
The security situation is expected to remain fragile due to the proliferation of weapons and armed actors, the absence of unified security/military institutions, and competition among armed groups, compounded by smuggling and trafficking networks;
The humanitarian situation is expected to evolve unevenly, shaped by security and political developments. While internal displacement may remain limited, vulnerabilities among internally displaced persons and returnees will persist in areas with weak services and insecurity. Continued conflict in Sudan is likely to sustain or increase protection pressures related to the influx of Sudanese refugees, alongside ongoing risks to migrants, refugees and asylum-seekers, including abuse, exploitation and limited access to protection and services;
Strategic partnerships will continue among the international community, including regional organizations and the United Nations, and are expected to continue in support of an inclusive Libyan-led and Libyan-owned political process, while the United Nations sanctions regime continues to be applied.
The Mission integrates a gender perspective across operations. The revised 2026 Action Plan on Women, Peace and Security will guide efforts to strengthen gender integration and promote the full, equal and meaningful participation of women in political, electoral, governance and security processes. The Mission, together with the United Nations country team, will support women’s rights through advocacy, technical assistance and the promotion of gender-responsive legislation and policies, including measures addressing violence against women and service gaps affecting displaced, migrant and refugee women and girls.
The Mission will mainstream disability inclusion, promoting the full and meaningful participation of persons with disabilities in political dialogue, elections and public decision-making, including through targeted advocacy to address barriers to accessibility and participation.

5.II.4 To contribute to the objective, UNTSO will:
(a) Deploy trained military observers to UNDOF to assist in its mandate implementation, and to southern Lebanon in accordance with the mandate of UNTSO, by providing timely and accurate observation, reports, investigations, inspections and verification. The mission provides administrative, logistical, communications and security support to all outstations and liaison offices;
(b) Conduct periodic regional liaison through strategic engagement and regular interaction with the parties, as well as with senior representatives of troop-contributing countries and other Member States in the mandate area, and conduct regular liaison, through offices in Egypt, Lebanon, and the Syrian Arab Republic, and with Israel and Jordan from its headquarters;
(c) Support informed strategic analysis and decision-making within the Organization, and with peacekeeping partners and other regional actors through regional assessments, and support peacekeeping partnerships in coordination with other United Nations entities;
(d) Conduct seminars, briefings and information exchanges with regional stakeholders, think tanks and senior representatives of troop-contributing countries and other Member States.
5.II.5 The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) Reliable communications channels and relations of confidence with the parties, conflict resolution, reduced tensions, avoidance of incidents that could escalate tensions between the parties and expanded liaison and information networks with national and local community representatives and with actors with influence in the areas of interest;
(b) Strengthened situational awareness and decision-making by other United Nations entities, enhancing the impact of regional United Nations presence;
(c) Improved cooperation and understanding of cross-cutting regional issues among regional counterparts.
5.II.6 With regard to the external factors, the overall plan for 2027 is based on the following planning assumptions:
(a) All parties to the Armistice Agreements of 1949 and related agreements will cooperate with UNTSO in the performance of its functions;
(b) All parties will remain willing to resolve their disputes and to cooperate with the United Nations in fulfilment of the Security Council mandate.
5.II.7 With regard to cooperation with other entities and inter-agency coordination and liaison, UNTSO will foster cooperation with a range of national, regional and international entities. It will engage with relevant national and regional organizations and collaborate closely with other United Nations regional peacekeeping operations, special political missions, humanitarian coordinators, security management systems and relevant agencies in all five countries where it operates. These engagements are intended to facilitate the sharing and validation of information from the ground, integrate assessments, ensure consistency in key United Nations messages and capture synergies to enhance the collective impact of United Nations presences in the region. Strategic cooperation will occur at multiple levels, through regular consultations and inter-mission engagements with heads of missions and agencies, as well as at the working level. In addition, UNTSO will expand its efforts to share information and analyse regional trends that affect the implementation of United Nations mandates, including through consultative inter-mission coordination activities.
5.II.8 The mission will continue to integrate a gender perspective into its operational activities, deliverables and results, as appropriate. This will be strengthened through education and training for all military observers, as well as through the inclusion of female military observers in operations and female language assistants in UNTSO activities.

To contribute to the objective, the Mission will:
Apply a proactive and inclusive approach to verification, which includes advocacy, good offices and close engagement with the parties to the Final Agreement and relevant institutions and authorities at the international, national, regional and local levels, as well as vulnerable communities, with special attention to women, and children;
Conduct independent verification of the implementation of the Final Agreement and participate, jointly with the parties, in key forums and institutions created for the implementation of the Final Agreement;
Promote constructive dialogue and trust-building between the Government and the representatives of the former FARC-EP as the other signatory party of the Agreement to address pending issues relating to peace implementation, foster collaborative communication with key interlocutors, including actors across the political spectrum, civil society and communities at risk (such as women, indigenous and Afro-Colombian peoples, and victims organizations), the private sector and the international community, to support the implementation of the Final Agreement;
Support the effective implementation of the Women, Peace and Security agenda at national and territorial levels to advance sustainable peace;
Provide good offices to support the implementation of the Final Agreement, monitor evolving conflict dynamics at local level between armed groups, and help to build and sustain peace in Colombia.
The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
Increased trust between the parties to the Final Agreement; sustainability of the reintegration process; opportunities for political participation; strengthened accountability and national efforts to foster security guarantees for former combatants, social leaders and communities; enhanced safe and productive land access and progress in the implementation of development plans with a territorial focus, with the active participation of local communities, for advances in the implementation of the comprehensive rural reform;
Enhanced State capacity, presence and responsiveness to promote collective and territorial protection approaches and better prevent and investigate attacks against former combatants, social leaders and communities; increased trust between the parties through tripartite coordination mechanisms at the national and local levels, while accounting for specific needs of communities at risk;
Enhanced coordination between government institutions, communities in situations of vulnerability, and women-led organizations; increased engagement of the international community, including the United Nations system;
Increased trust in the peace consolidation efforts, and progress in the comprehensive implementation of the Final Agreement to advance reconciliation, non-repetition and sustainable peace, contributing to a reduction in violence and improvements in the humanitarian situation in conflict-affected regions.
With regard to cooperation with other entities at the global, regional, national and local levels, the Mission engages extensively, building on synergies with other entities to support mandate implementation and peace consolidation in Colombia. Extensive engagement with the diplomatic community and regional organizations help to identify areas of mutual interest. At the national and local levels, the Mission strengthens ties with political, civil society and private sector actors, including community, religious, youth, women, peasant leaders, victims and persons with disabilities’ organizations. The Mission continues to bolster its relationships with Colombian departmental and local authorities, as they gain more responsibilities regarding the reintegration process, ensuring security guarantees for communities and supporting society’s participation in peace building.
With regard to inter-agency coordination and liaison, the Mission strengthened its coordination with the United Nations country team and the Resident Coordinator. The Mission also remains a member of the Strategic Committee of the United Nations Multi-Partner Trust Fund for Peace, thereby contributing to coherence across the peace and security and development pillars.
With regard to the external factors, the overall plan for 2027 is based on the following planning assumptions:
Continued international support for the close engagement of the Mission and the United Nations system in the peace process, and continued commitment by national and local authorities and relevant parties remain to collaborating with the Mission;
The new governmental and legislative configuration emerging from the 2026 general elections will significantly influence the Mission’s work and the implementation of the Final Agreement;
National and local authorities remain engaged in increasing effective State presence; authorities, State entities and coordination mechanisms tasked with preventing and investigating security incidents remain committed to the prioritized populations in the Final Agreement;
Continued trust in the peace process and the comprehensive implementation of the Final Agreement, enabling advancements towards reconciliation and non-repetition, including for vulnerable communities, women, and children;
Continued efforts to advance the comprehensive rural reform with appropriate resource allocation and increased interinstitutional coordination.
The Mission integrates a gender perspective into its operational activities, deliverables and results, as appropriate, and ensures effective implementation of its gender parity directives and strategy. It also applies gender and intersectional analysis in verifying its core mandate, supporting effective referral pathways to responsible institutions for cases of gender-based and conflict-related sexual violence and - for the latter - contributing to improved reporting. The Mission provides support to the national action plan on Security Council resolution 1325 (2000) adopted in 2024, which is expected to strengthen the implementation of the gender provisions of the Final Agreement and reinforce the full, equal and meaningful participation of women in peacebuilding.

To contribute to the objective, the Office will:
(a) Lead engagement and advocacy for the advancement of youth issues across the United Nations in the areas of peace and security, sustainable development and human rights, through engagement with Member State representatives, principals of United Nations organs and senior management of United Nations entities, and in close consultation with young people;
(b) Promote meaningful, inclusive and effective engagement of young people and of youth-led and youth-focused organizations in the work of the United Nations, in close collaboration with United Nations system entities;
(c) Encourage greater United Nations system-wide collaboration, coordination and accountability on the advancement of youth issues, including United Nations support to Member States, as appropriate and upon request, in promoting the effective and meaningful engagement, participation and empowerment of young people;
(d) Engage with youth-focused envoys, representatives and offices of regional organizations on advancing youth issues;
(e) Propose innovative approaches to advance multi-stakeholder initiatives on the engagement and empowerment of young people, for the consideration of Member States, including on quality and inclusive education, decent jobs, skills development, digital transformation and youth-led innovation and solutions;
(f) Promote and facilitate intergenerational and intercultural dialogue, collaboration and solidarity by creating opportunities for young people to not only share and collaborate among one another, but also engage with Member States, the private sector, academia and the United Nations, including through intergovernmental policy forums;
(g) Support efforts to enhance youth representation at the United Nations, including through increasing and improving the conditions of internships and employment opportunities for young people, especially for those from developing countries and with due regard to gender balance;
(h) Support efforts towards the mobilization of financial and technical support and investment to promote and upscale quality and inclusive education and training, skills development, capacity-building and bridging the digital divide, in tandem with job creation, for young people and creating an enabling environment for harnessing their talents and abilities to contribute to their societies;
(i) Conduct outreach to young people and to youth-led and youth-focused organizations, through innovative means, including the use of social media, narrative storytelling and audiovisual content, with an emphasis on the creation of multilingual assets and templates, where possible, for global dissemination through United Nations country teams and United Nations information centres.
The above-mentioned work is expected to result in:
(a) Meaningful youth engagement being systematically integrated into governance structures, policymaking and decision-making processes at the intergovernmental, regional and national, levels;
(b) Strengthened coherence, accountability and collaboration on youth issues across the United Nations system;
(c) Enhanced mechanisms and increased investments being made available to support socioeconomic opportunities for young people, across social, economic and environmental priorities at the global, regional and national levels.
With regard to the external factors, the overall plan for 2027 is based on the following planning assumptions:
(a) Member States continue to support the advancement of the situation of young people, including in the implementation of relevant international frameworks and agreements;
(b) Sustained recognition by the global community of the important role that young people play in the promotion of peace and security, sustainable development and human rights;
(c) There is adequate allocation of resources for action on young people and meaningful youth engagement across the United Nations system and national, regional and global institutions.
With regard to cooperation with other entities at the global, regional, national and local levels, the Office will collaborate with regional and national youth authorities, envoys and regional organizations. It will also prioritize the forging of new relationships and the strengthening of existing collaboration with relevant youth organizations, civil society organizations, academia and the private sector.
With regard to inter-agency coordination and liaison, the Office will continue to coordinate more than 60 United Nations entities and all United Nations country teams for improved collaboration, coherence and accountability in youth work across the United Nations system under the United Nations Youth Strategy (Youth2030). The Office will provide secretariat support to the strategy’s High-level Steering Committee and the joint working group responsible for advancing coherent action by the United Nations system on youth-related matters. It will also continue to work with the Department of Management Strategy, Policy and Compliance, the Department of Operational Support and other members of the United Nations Youth Strategy to improve internships and employment opportunities across the United Nations system and beyond, with a focus on advancing socioeconomic opportunities for young people. It will collaborate with the Department of Global Communications to elevate the Organization’s work with and for youth and advocate for their rights, participation and protection. It will continue to exercise its system-wide lead in Youth2030 to advance the Youth, Peace and Security agenda across the United Nations, in line with Security Council resolution 2807 (2025) and the UN Youth Office Strategic Plan 2025–2028. Building on its engagement in the inter-agency joint secretariat on the Youth, Peace and Security agenda, alongside UNFPA and DPPA/PBSO, and through systematic global-level engagement—with intentional linkages to regional and national processes—the Office will advocate for and support the translation of the Youth, Peace and Security commitments into practice, including through structured engagement with Member States, parliamentarians, and regional organizations. It will also contribute regularly to the work of various inter-agency working groups and partner with relevant United Nations entities, as mandated, on meaningful youth participation in major intergovernmental meetings and across the UN system, to accelerate innovative governance models and advocate for global action on youth inclusion and youth leadership. As part of the efforts to strengthen support for young people, the Office will continue to collaborate with OHCHR, including on advancing mental health as a human right.
The Office integrates a gender perspective into its operational activities, deliverables and results, as appropriate, by ensuring the meaningful participation of girls and young women in United Nations processes and intergovernmental decision-making, facilitating intergenerational discussions on women’s leadership, promoting the integration of a gender perspective into youth inclusion and youth-led action and by promoting discussions on sexual and reproductive health and rights as an integral part of the youth agenda.
In line with the United Nations Disability Inclusion Strategy and guided by the Office’s research findings and recommendations on young people with disabilities, the Office will advocate for meaningful participation of young persons with disabilities in the work of the United Nations and beyond. In consultation with young people with disabilities and their networks and organizations, the Office will mainstream disability inclusion into all aspects of its work, with the aim of ensuring accessibility to opportunities, information and events, to the greatest extent possible.
